window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"Every labeled structure on the 226 Visible Body and zSpace 3-D models from the thorax and GI lab, as a “what is this?” question. All labels are covered and the yellow ? marks the one asked; pick its name from five look-alike choices. Each region opens with a short summary of the structures it covers. Each part has short notes, then its core questions (one per structure), then a “More questions” dropdown with the same structures on other pictures. After you answer: where it lies, how to find it, why each other choice is wrong, and more pictures of it.","feat":1,"fold":1,"tier":1,"uw":"Region","type":"vb","npic":226},"lectures":[{"id":"VB-T1","t":"Thoracic wall & bony thorax"},{"id":"VB-T2","t":"Lungs, pleura & airways"},{"id":"VB-T3","t":"Mediastinum & great vessels"},{"id":"VB-T4","t":"Heart & coronary vessels"},{"id":"VB-A1","t":"Abdominal wall"},{"id":"VB-A2","t":"Stomach, duodenum & peritoneum"},{"id":"VB-A3","t":"Liver, gallbladder, pancreas & spleen"},{"id":"VB-A4","t":"Small & large intestine"},{"id":"VB-A5","t":"Kidneys & posterior abdominal wall"}],"groups":[{"l":"VB-T1","u":0,"label":"Serratus anterior muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_118_1347.jpg","cap":"Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.13","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8033,0.6138,0.8892,0.6894]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s002~serratus-anterior-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Innermost intercostal muscle","B. Serratus anterior muscle","C. Sternothyroid muscle","D. Internal intercostal muscle","E. External intercostal muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Serratus anterior muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Serratus anterior muscle.</b> In axial sections serratus anterior is the muscle lying on the outer surface of the lateral ribs, passing back between ribs and scapula to the scapula's medial border.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Outer surfaces of the lateral parts of ribs 1-8, by fleshy digitations</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Anterior (costal) surface of the medial border of the scapula</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pectoralis major and minor in front; long thoracic nerve on its outer surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ribs 1-8 and the external intercostal muscles; subscapularis and scapula lie behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the lateral chest wall below the axilla first; the muscle slips attached to the outer ribs that disappear deep to the scapula are serratus anterior.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s002.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 2"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2027,0.9112,0.4815,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.51574}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":1,"label":"External intercostal muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_121_1354.jpg","cap":"Dissection of posterior aspect of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.17","tb":1,"ov":[[0.719,0.8397,0.9327,0.8831]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s004~external-intercostal-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Internal intercostal muscle","B. Innermost intercostal muscle","C. External intercostal muscle","D. Intercostal nerve","E. Transversus thoracis muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"External intercostal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. External intercostal muscle.</b> The external intercostal is the outermost muscle layer in each intercostal space, its fibres running down and forward from one rib to the rib below.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower border of the rib above</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Upper border of the rib below; fibres run down and forward</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pectoral muscles, serratus anterior or latissimus dorsi, depending on the region</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle, whose fibres run at right angles to it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find two adjacent ribs on the lateral or posterior chest wall first; the first muscle layer between them, with fibres running down and forward, is the external intercostal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s004.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 4"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.201,0.8963,0.5033,0.9839,"",1]],"ar":0.42929}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":1,"label":"Internal intercostal muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_180_138.jpg","cap":"Intercostal muscles","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.27","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0075,0.7056,0.2693,0.7472]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s006.jpg","cap":"Internal intercostal muscle on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 6","ov":[[0.199,0.9065,0.4974,0.9884]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st13~internal-intercostal-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Innermost intercostal muscle","B. Intercostal nerve","C. External intercostal muscle","D. Internal intercostal muscle","E. Serratus anterior muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal intercostal muscle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Internal intercostal muscle.</b> The internal intercostal lies deep to the external intercostal in each rib space, its fibres running down and back, with the intercostal vessels and nerve beneath it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower border of the rib above, at the edge of the costal groove</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Upper border of the rib below; fibres run down and back</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External intercostal muscle (external intercostal membrane in front)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the innermost intercostal muscle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the external intercostal first, then lift or cut it; the next muscle layer, with fibres crossing at right angles, is the internal intercostal (anteriorly near the sternum it is visible through the external intercostal membrane).","ex":"","fl":["The arrow is on muscle fibres between two ribs; no artery is drawn in this view."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st13.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 13"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2301,0.9508,0.6157,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.27943}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":1,"label":"Innermost intercostal muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_121_1354.jpg","cap":"Dissection of posterior aspect of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.17","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7198,0.6935,0.9516,0.737]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s048~innermost-intercostal-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. External intercostal muscle","B. Serratus anterior muscle","C. Innermost intercostal muscle","D. Intercostal nerve","E. Internal intercostal muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Innermost intercostal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Innermost intercostal muscle.</b> The innermost intercostal is the deepest intercostal layer, lying against the parietal pleura and separated from the internal intercostal by the intercostal vein, artery and nerve.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surface of the rib above, near the costal groove</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inner surface of the rib below</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the internal intercostal muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the intercostal neurovascular bundle in the costal groove first (from inside the chest after removing pleura, or by deepening the space); the thin muscle on the pleural side of the bundle is the innermost intercostal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s048.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 48"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.201,0.9059,0.5219,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.475}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":1,"label":"Intercostal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_178_133.jpg","cap":"Intercostal space","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.26","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6942,0.8253,0.8419,0.8565]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s050~intercostal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Innermost intercostal muscle","C. Intercostal nerve","D. External intercostal muscle","E. Internal intercostal muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Intercostal nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Intercostal nerve.</b> Each intercostal nerve is a thoracic spinal nerve's front branch, running in the costal groove below its rib, between internal and innermost intercostals, beneath the vein and artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of spinal nerves T1-T11</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Intercostal muscles, chest wall skin and parietal pleura; T7-T11 continue to the abdominal wall</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle; lowest in the bundle, below the intercostal vein and artery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal muscle (endothoracic fascia and pleura where it is absent)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal groove on the inferior border of a rib from inside the chest first; the lowest, solid white cord of the bundle under it is the intercostal nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s050.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 50"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1867,0.9048,0.3819,0.9856,"",1]],"ar":0.46929}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":1,"label":"Internal intercostal muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_180_138.jpg","cap":"Intercostal muscles","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.27","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0075,0.7056,0.2693,0.7472]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st13.jpg","cap":"Internal intercostal muscle on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 13","ov":[[0.2281,0.9488,0.6177,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s006~internal-intercostal-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Intercostal nerve","B. Innermost intercostal muscle","C. Internal intercostal muscle","D. Serratus anterior muscle","E. External intercostal muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal intercostal muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Internal intercostal muscle.</b> The internal intercostal lies deep to the external intercostal in each rib space, its fibres running down and back, with the intercostal vessels and nerve beneath it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower border of the rib above, at the edge of the costal groove</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Upper border of the rib below; fibres run down and back</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External intercostal muscle (external intercostal membrane in front)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the innermost intercostal muscle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the external intercostal first, then lift or cut it; the next muscle layer, with fibres crossing at right angles, is the internal intercostal (anteriorly near the sternum it is visible through the external intercostal membrane).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s006.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 6"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.201,0.9085,0.4954,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.48929,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st13~internal-intercostal-muscle"}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":2,"label":"Internal thoracic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg","cap":"Arteries of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0154,0.2714,0.2528,0.3203]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s010.jpg","cap":"Internal thoracic artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 10","ov":[[0.1902,0.9097,0.4562,0.989]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st11~internal-thoracic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Transversus thoracis muscle","B. Circumflex artery","C. Left coronary artery","D. Internal thoracic artery","E. Musculophrenic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal thoracic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Internal thoracic artery.</b> The internal thoracic artery is a branch of the subclavian artery that runs down behind the costal cartilages about 1 cm lateral to the edge of the sternum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>First part of the subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 1-6; ends as musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and pleura above; transversus thoracis separates it from pleura lower down</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn over the removed anterior chest wall and find the lateral edge of the sternum first; the vertical artery running parallel to it about a finger-width lateral, behind the cartilages, is the internal thoracic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st11.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 11"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2359,0.9508,0.5925,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.31116}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":2,"label":"Musculophrenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_160_1444.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and venous drainage of pericardium","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.47","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0247,0.7725,0.2564,0.8232]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s014~musculophrenic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Musculophrenic artery","B. Transversus thoracis muscle","C. Right coronary artery","D. Posterior interventricular artery","E. Internal thoracic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Musculophrenic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Musculophrenic artery.</b> The musculophrenic artery is the lateral end branch of the internal thoracic artery, leaving it in the 6th intercostal space to run along the costal margin.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery (its lateral terminal branch, in the 6th intercostal space)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 7-9 and the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 7-9 along the costal margin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm, whose costal attachment it pierces along the costal margin</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the internal thoracic artery and follow it down to its division at the 6th intercostal space; the branch that turns laterally along the costal margin is the musculophrenic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s014.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 14"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1874,0.9042,0.4404,0.9855,"",1]],"ar":0.46643}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":2,"label":"Transversus thoracis muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_118_1347.jpg","cap":"Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.13","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0094,0.5933,0.1972,0.6393]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s008~transversus-thoracis-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Internal intercostal muscle","B. Transversus thoracis muscle","C. External intercostal muscle","D. Internal thoracic artery","E. Musculophrenic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversus thoracis muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Transversus thoracis muscle.</b> Transversus thoracis lies on the inner surface of the front chest wall, fanning from the lower sternum and xiphoid to costal cartilages 2–6, behind the internal thoracic vessels.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surface of the lower body of the sternum and the xiphoid process</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2-6</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal thoracic vessels, then the costal cartilages and internal intercostals</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn the removed anterior chest wall (sternum and cartilages) over and find the internal thoracic artery first; the thin muscle slips covering it and fanning out from the lower sternum are transversus thoracis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s008.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 8"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.201,0.9023,0.5219,0.9852,"",1]],"ar":0.45714}]},{"l":"VB-T1","u":2,"label":"Internal thoracic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg","cap":"Arteries of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0154,0.2714,0.2528,0.3203]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st11.jpg","cap":"Internal thoracic artery on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 11","ov":[[0.2339,0.9488,0.5945,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s010~internal-thoracic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Transversus thoracis muscle","B. Musculophrenic artery","C. Internal thoracic artery","D. Left marginal artery","E. Left subclavian artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal thoracic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Internal thoracic artery.</b> The internal thoracic artery is a branch of the subclavian artery that runs down behind the costal cartilages about 1 cm lateral to the edge of the sternum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>First part of the subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 1-6; ends as musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and pleura above; transversus thoracis separates it from pleura lower down</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn over the removed anterior chest wall and find the lateral edge of the sternum first; the vertical artery running parallel to it about a finger-width lateral, behind the cartilages, is the internal thoracic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s010.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 10"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1922,0.9117,0.4542,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.5205,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st11~internal-thoracic-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":3,"label":"Horizontal fissure (right lung)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_141_1401.jpg","cap":"Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.34","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0124,0.5807,0.1488,0.6599]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s024~horizontal-fissure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","B. Oblique fissure","C. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","D. Right crus of diaphragm","E. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Horizontal fissure (right lung)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Horizontal fissure (right lung).</b> Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Middle lobe below it; meets the oblique fissure at the midaxillary line</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure on the lateral surface of the right lung first; the fissure that branches off it and runs forward horizontally is the horizontal fissure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s024.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 24"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1884,0.918,0.4195,0.9876,"",1]],"ar":0.63645}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":3,"label":"Oblique fissure","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_264_259.jpg","cap":"Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.103","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8425,0.3487,0.9933,0.3958]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s026~oblique-fissure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right pulmonary artery","B. Left crus of diaphragm","C. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","D. Oblique fissure","E. Horizontal fissure (right lung)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Oblique fissure","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Oblique fissure.</b> The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; runs from the T2 spine to the 6th costal cartilage</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the long cleft that starts high on the back of the lung and reaches the inferior border anteriorly; on the right confirm the horizontal fissure branches from it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s026.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 26"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1667,0.8995,0.3447,0.9846,"",1]],"ar":0.44357}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":4,"label":"Cardiac notch (left lung)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_138_1395.jpg","cap":"Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7605,0.393,0.8922,0.4383]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s030~cardiac-notch-of-left-lung","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inferior border of lung","B. Left main (primary) bronchus","C. Lingula (of left lung)","D. Pulmonary veins","E. Cardiac notch (left lung)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cardiac notch (left lung)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cardiac notch (left lung).</b> The cardiac notch is the deep scoop in the anterior border of the left lung's upper lobe where the heart pushes in, just above the lingula.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left 4th and 5th costal cartilages and the left costomediastinal recess</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Pericardium and heart (bare area), just left of the sternum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior border of the left lung first; the scooped-out gap in that border above the lingula is the cardiac notch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s030.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 30"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2078,0.9134,0.386,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.54223}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":4,"label":"Lingula (of left lung)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_138_1395.jpg","cap":"Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7605,0.678,0.9369,0.7514]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s032~lingula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Lingula (of left lung)","B. Cardiac notch (left lung)","C. Pulmonary veins","D. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","E. Hilum of the lung"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lingula (of left lung)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lingula (of left lung).</b> The lingula is the tongue-shaped lower front part of the left superior lobe, just below the cardiac notch and over the left side of the heart.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior chest wall at the left 5th-6th costal cartilages</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left ventricle (left heart border) medially; inferior lobe behind across the oblique fissure</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cardiac notch on the anterior border of the left lung first; the tongue of lung tissue curling below and in front of it is the lingula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s032.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 32"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.201,0.9157,0.3735,0.9876,"",1]],"ar":0.53323}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":5,"label":"Central tendon of diaphragm","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_396_432.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.132","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6578,0.0846,0.7932,0.1272]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s495~central-tendon-of-diaphragm","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left dome of diaphragm","B. Central tendon of diaphragm","C. Left crus of diaphragm","D. Right dome of diaphragm","E. Right crus of diaphragm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Central tendon of diaphragm","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Central tendon of diaphragm.</b> The central tendon is the pale three-lobed tendon at the top of the diaphragm, fused above to the pericardium, holding the inferior vena cava opening at T8.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium fused to it, with the heart's inferior surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Liver's upper surface; IVC (inferior vena cava) passes through its right part at T8</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Remove the heart in its pericardium and look at the floor it sat on first; the pale tendinous area fused to the pericardium is the central tendon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s495.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 495"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9076,0.5547,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.48429}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":5,"label":"Right crus of diaphragm","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_396_432.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.132","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2235,0.8839,0.3241,0.9269]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s497~right-crus-of-diaphragm","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Central tendon of diaphragm","B. Right dome of diaphragm","C. Left dome of diaphragm","D. Left crus of diaphragm","E. Right crus of diaphragm"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right crus of diaphragm","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right crus of diaphragm.</b> The right crus is the thick diaphragm band rising from the L1–L3 bodies on the right front of the aorta, behind the inferior vena cava, and splitting around the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Anterior bodies of L1-L3 and their discs</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Central tendon; its fibres split around the esophageal hiatus at T10</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) and right suprarenal gland, with the caudate lobe in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Bodies of L1-L3, with the aorta passing behind the median arcuate ligament on its left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal aorta at the level of the celiac trunk first; the thick muscle band rising on its right side from the vertebral bodies is the right crus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s497.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 497"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3158,0.9321,0.7082,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.99636}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":5,"label":"Left crus of diaphragm","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_335_1778.jpg","cap":"Attachments, disposition, and features of the abdominal aspect of the diaphragm","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.91","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8986,0.6296,0.9545,0.6799]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s499~left-crus-of-diaphragm","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left crus of diaphragm","B. Right dome of diaphragm","C. Right crus of diaphragm","D. Central tendon of diaphragm","E. Left dome of diaphragm"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left crus of diaphragm","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left crus of diaphragm.</b> In an axial section the left crus is the muscle band rising from L1 and L2 on the left of the aorta, smaller than the right crus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Anterior bodies of L1-L2 and their disc</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Central tendon; joins the right crus by the median arcuate ligament over the aorta</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left gastric artery, then the lesser sac and stomach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Bodies of L1-L2, with the aorta to its right</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal aorta at the level of the celiac trunk; the muscle band rising from the vertebral bodies on its left side is the left crus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s499.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 499"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3158,0.9321,0.6852,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.99636}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":6,"label":"Right main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.5771,0.0996,0.6192]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s166.jpg","cap":"Right main (primary) bronchus on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 166","ov":[[0.214,0.908,0.4539,0.9887]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s044~right-main-bronchus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right main (primary) bronchus","B. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","C. Carina (tracheal bifurcation)","D. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","E. Left main (primary) bronchus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right main (primary) bronchus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right main (primary) bronchus.</b> The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina at the tracheal bifurcation first; the short, steep, wide bronchus going right, under the azygos arch, is the right main bronchus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s044.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 44"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3875,0.9186,0.6417,0.9877,"",1]],"ar":0.60302}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":6,"label":"Left main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.6654,0.0996,0.7078]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s164~left-main-bronchus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right main (primary) bronchus","B. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","C. Left main (primary) bronchus","D. Central tendon of diaphragm","E. Pulmonary veins"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left main (primary) bronchus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left main (primary) bronchus.</b> The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Aortic arch hooking over it; left pulmonary artery in front, then above</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus and descending aorta behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina first; the long bronchus running left under the aortic arch, below the left pulmonary artery, is the left main bronchus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s164.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 164"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9129,0.4369,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.515}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":6,"label":"Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_190_1499.jpg","cap":"Relationships of structures in superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.65","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0091,0.3616,0.1979,0.4385]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s168~tracheobronchial-lymph-nodes","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left main (primary) bronchus","B. Lingula (of left lung)","C. Hilum of the lung","D. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","E. Right main (primary) bronchus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes.</b> The tracheobronchial lymph nodes cluster around the tracheal bifurcation, in the angles between the trachea and each main bronchus and below the carina.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Bronchopulmonary (hilar) nodes, which drain the lungs</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Paratracheal nodes, then the bronchomediastinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Deep cardiac plexus, right pulmonary artery and aortic arch in front of the bifurcation</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus behind; the subcarinal group rests on the pericardium over the left atrium</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina; the small, often carbon-blackened nodes in the angles beside it and below the bifurcation are tracheobronchial nodes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s168.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 168"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9021,0.5554,0.9851,"",1]],"ar":0.45571}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":6,"label":"Right main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.5771,0.0996,0.6192]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s044.jpg","cap":"Right main (primary) bronchus on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 44","ov":[[0.3855,0.9166,0.6437,0.9897]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s166~right-main-bronchus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes","B. Left main (primary) bronchus","C. Oblique fissure","D. Right main (primary) bronchus","E. Left crus of diaphragm"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right main (primary) bronchus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right main (primary) bronchus.</b> The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina at the tracheal bifurcation first; the short, steep, wide bronchus going right, under the azygos arch, is the right main bronchus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s166.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 166"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.91,0.4519,0.9867,"",1]],"ar":0.49786,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s044~right-main-bronchus"}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":7,"label":"Hilum of the lung","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_145_1414.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary circulation","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.37","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3807,0.0122,0.504,0.0593]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s034~hilum-of-lung","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","B. Hilum of the lung","C. Right pulmonary artery","D. Pulmonary veins","E. Lingula (of left lung)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hilum of the lung","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hilum of the lung.</b> The hilum is the pleura-ringed area on the lung's medial surface where the main bronchus, pulmonary artery and pulmonary veins enter, with the pulmonary ligament hanging below it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels, in front of the root</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Vagus nerve and posterior pulmonary plexus behind the root; descending aorta behind the left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cut bronchus and pulmonary vessels on the medial surface of the removed lung first; the pleura-bounded area containing them is the hilum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s034.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 34"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.286,0.8261,0.484,0.9112,"",0],[0.2671,0.9186,0.5063,0.9877,"",1]],"ar":0.78406}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":7,"label":"Right pulmonary artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_145_1414.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary circulation","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.37","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0051,0.1216,0.1487,0.1993]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s036~right-pulmonary-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left pulmonary artery","B. Right pulmonary artery","C. Hilum of the lung","D. Pulmonary veins","E. Left coronary artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right pulmonary artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right pulmonary artery.</b> The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ascending aorta and SVC (superior vena cava), in front of it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Carina and right main bronchus, directly behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk first and follow its right branch; the vessel that disappears behind the ascending aorta and SVC toward the right hilum is the right pulmonary artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s036.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 36"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3875,0.9186,0.6647,0.9877,"",1]],"ar":0.60302}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":7,"label":"Pulmonary veins","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_202_165.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary vessels","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.45","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1258,0.6287,0.2788,0.6894]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s096.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary veins on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 96","ov":[[0.214,0.9106,0.4124,0.9892]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s042~pulmonary-veins","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hilum of the lung","B. Right pulmonary artery","C. Posterior intercostal vein","D. Pulmonary veins","E. Accessory hemiazygos vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary veins","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pulmonary veins.</b> The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Each lung, as superior and inferior veins per side; right middle lobe vein joins the superior</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve in front; right veins pass behind the SVC (superior vena cava) and right atrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Main bronchus behind them in the root; descending aorta behind the left veins</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left atrium on the back of the heart (or the lowest part of the hilum) first; the vessels entering the left atrium from each side are the pulmonary veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s042.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 42"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3875,0.9186,0.6585,0.9877,"",1]],"ar":0.60302}]},{"l":"VB-T2","u":7,"label":"Pulmonary veins","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_202_165.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary vessels","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.45","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1258,0.6287,0.2788,0.6894]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s042.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary veins on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 42","ov":[[0.3855,0.9166,0.6605,0.9897]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s096~pulmonary-veins","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pulmonary veins","B. Accessory hemiazygos vein","C. Anterior cardiac veins","D. Hilum of the lung","E. Right pulmonary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary veins","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Pulmonary veins.</b> The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Each lung, as superior and inferior veins per side; right middle lobe vein joins the superior</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve in front; right veins pass behind the SVC (superior vena cava) and right atrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Main bronchus behind them in the root; descending aorta behind the left veins</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left atrium on the back of the heart (or the lowest part of the hilum) first; the vessels entering the left atrium from each side are the pulmonary veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s096.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 96"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9126,0.4104,0.9872,"",1]],"ar":0.51357,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s042~pulmonary-veins"}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":8,"label":"Trachea","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_193_1505.jpg","cap":"Great vessels and nerves","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.68","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2051,0.0312,0.2838,0.0947]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s162~trachea","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Esophagus","B. Left common carotid artery","C. Thymus","D. Posterior vagal trunk","E. Trachea"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Trachea","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Trachea.</b> The trachea runs down the midline in front of the esophagus to the sternal angle, where it splits into the main bronchi, with the aortic arch curving along its left side.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein and brachiocephalic trunk in front; aortic arch on its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus, directly behind its membranous posterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the esophagus on the front of the vertebral column first; the ringed airway directly in front of it is the trachea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s162.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 162"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9065,0.389,0.986,"",1]],"ar":0.47857}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":8,"label":"Esophagus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.0785,0.8064,0.1209]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s170~esophagus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Sympathetic trunk","C. Trachea","D. Thymus","E. Esophagus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Esophagus.</b> The esophagus runs behind the trachea, then behind the left atrium, first right of and then in front of the descending aorta, to pierce the diaphragm at T10.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Trachea, then the left main bronchus, then pericardium over the left atrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebrae, thoracic duct and azygos vein; descending aorta behind it lower down</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the trachea and look directly behind it; below the carina follow the same tube down behind the left atrium, with the descending aorta on its left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s170.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 170"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9098,0.389,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49643}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":9,"label":"Thymus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_191_1501.jpg","cap":"Dissections of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.66","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1014,0.6115,0.1892,0.6572]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s148~thymus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left brachiocephalic vein","B. Thymus","C. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","D. Hemiazygos vein","E. Right brachiocephalic vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thymus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Thymus.</b> The thymus lies in the front of the superior mediastinum, behind the manubrium and in front of the left brachiocephalic vein and aortic arch; in adults it is mostly fat.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Manubrium and upper body of the sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein, aortic arch and upper fibrous pericardium, behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the posterior surface of the manubrium first; the tissue between it and the left brachiocephalic vein is the thymus (or its fatty remnant).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s148.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 148"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9138,0.389,0.9875,"",1]],"ar":0.52071}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":9,"label":"Left brachiocephalic vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_160_1444.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and venous drainage of pericardium","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.47","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7637,0.0867,0.9576,0.1717]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s152~left-brachiocephalic-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right subclavian vein","B. Thymus","C. Left brachiocephalic vein","D. Right brachiocephalic vein","E. Azygos vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left brachiocephalic vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left brachiocephalic vein.</b> The left brachiocephalic vein crosses down and right behind the manubrium, in front of the aortic arch branches, to join the right one and form the superior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left internal jugular and left subclavian veins, joining behind the left sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the right brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC (superior vena cava), behind the right 1st costal cartilage</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Manubrium and the thymus (or its fatty remnant), in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Origins of the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the manubrium and look deep to it; the first large vessel, crossing from upper left to lower right above the aortic arch, is the left brachiocephalic vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s152.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 152"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9085,0.4954,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.48929}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":9,"label":"Right brachiocephalic vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_246_237.jpg","cap":"Right vagus nerve passing through the superior mediastinum","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.87","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7062,0.1728,0.9451,0.2118]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s150~right-brachiocephalic-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left subclavian vein","B. Right subclavian vein","C. Thymus","D. Left brachiocephalic vein","E. Right brachiocephalic vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right brachiocephalic vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right brachiocephalic vein.</b> The right brachiocephalic vein descends vertically from behind the right sternoclavicular joint, right of the brachiocephalic trunk, to join the left brachiocephalic vein forming the superior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right internal jugular and right subclavian veins, joining behind the right sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the left brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC (superior vena cava), behind the right 1st costal cartilage</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right edge of the manubrium and 1st costal cartilage; right phrenic nerve on its lateral side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve behind it; brachiocephalic trunk behind and medial</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SVC and follow it upward; above the right first costal cartilage it divides into the short vertical right and the long oblique left brachiocephalic veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s150.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 150"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9085,0.5104,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.48929}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":10,"label":"Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_196_1509.jpg","cap":"(Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7901,0.4135,0.8969,0.4534]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s068~arch-of-aorta","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left subclavian artery","B. Thoracic (descending) aorta","C. Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","D. Brachiocephalic trunk","E. Left common carotid artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Arch of aorta (aortic arch).</b> The aortic arch curves back and left from the sternal angle over the left lung root, beside the trachea, and forms the aortic knob on chest films.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ascending aorta, at the sternal angle (behind the 2nd right sternocostal joint)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries; becomes descending aorta at T4</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left phrenic and vagus nerves and mediastinal pleura on its left; thymus remnant in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea and esophagus behind and right; it arches over the left main bronchus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ascending aorta leaving the pericardium; follow it up to the sternal angle where it curves backward and to the left, then confirm the three branches rising from its top.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s068.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 68"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9212,0.389,0.9889,"",1]],"ar":0.57214}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":10,"label":"Brachiocephalic trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_192_1503.jpg","cap":"Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5014,0.1321,0.8688,0.1875]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s156~brachiocephalic-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","B. Anterior vagal trunk","C. Left common carotid artery","D. Left subclavian artery","E. Brachiocephalic trunk"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Brachiocephalic trunk","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Brachiocephalic trunk.</b> The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right subclavian and right common carotid arteries, behind the right sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein crossing in front; then the manubrium and thymus remnant</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea, crossing in front of it to lie on its right side</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left brachiocephalic vein and lift it; the large artery directly behind it, running from the arch up toward the right sternoclavicular joint, is the brachiocephalic trunk.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s156.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 156"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9077,0.464,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.485}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":10,"label":"Left common carotid artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_192_1503.jpg","cap":"Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5276,0.1897,0.9804,0.2447]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s158~left-common-carotid-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left common carotid artery","B. Right subclavian artery","C. Right common carotid artery","D. Left subclavian artery","E. Brachiocephalic trunk"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left common carotid artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left common carotid artery.</b> The left common carotid artery is the middle of the three aortic arch branches, rising along the left side of the trachea in front of the left subclavian artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its second branch, just left of the brachiocephalic trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Internal and external carotid arteries at the upper thyroid cartilage border (C4); no thoracic branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein, crossing in front of its origin; then the manubrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea behind and medial, with the left subclavian artery behind and lateral</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aortic arch and count its branches from right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, then the left common carotid in the middle, then the left subclavian.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s158.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 158"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9094,0.5169,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49429}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":10,"label":"Left subclavian artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_1032_2524.jpg","cap":"Subclavian and carotid arteries and their branches","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 8.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8605,0.749,0.9788,0.7905]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s160~left-subclavian-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left common carotid artery","B. Brachiocephalic trunk","C. Right subclavian artery","D. Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","E. Left subclavian artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left subclavian artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left subclavian artery.</b> The left subclavian artery is the last, most posterior branch of the aortic arch, rising behind the left common carotid and arching over the left lung apex.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its third and last branch, from the back of the arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Vertebral, internal thoracic, thyrocervical and costocervical branches; becomes axillary at the 1st rib</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left common carotid artery in front; left vagus and phrenic nerves descend between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left lung and pleura behind; trachea, esophagus and thoracic duct on its medial side</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch and follow its last, most posterior branch; it is the vessel that curves over the apex of the left lung rather than going straight up the neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s160.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 160"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9001,0.4611,0.9847,"",1]],"ar":0.44643}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7509,0.2287,0.8677,0.2726]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s028.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 28","ov":[[0.19,0.9117,0.3704,0.9892]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s016~phrenic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Left vagus nerve","D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Phrenic nerve (right/left).</b> Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s016.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 16"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1867,0.906,0.3597,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.47571}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Pericardiacophrenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_209_178.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerves and pericardiacophrenic vessels","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.54","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3286,0.8053,0.5447,0.8667]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s056~pericardiacophrenic-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","B. Pericardiacophrenic artery","C. Anterior vagal trunk","D. Left vagus nerve","E. Right vagus nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pericardiacophrenic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pericardiacophrenic artery.</b> The pericardiacophrenic artery is a thin branch of the internal thoracic artery running with the phrenic nerve between the pericardium and mediastinal pleura, in front of the lung root.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery, near the thoracic inlet</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Pericardium, mediastinal pleura and diaphragm, running with the phrenic nerve</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root, beside the phrenic nerve</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the phrenic nerve on the side of the pericardium; the thin vessel hugging it is the pericardiacophrenic artery, with its vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s056.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 56"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.7367,0.7591,0.9945,0.8616,"",0],[0.2531,0.9085,0.549,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.48929}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Right vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_260_1619.jpg","cap":"Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.35","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7188,0.0717,0.8824,0.1127]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s176.jpg","cap":"Right vagus nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 176","ov":[[0.214,0.9097,0.4267,0.989]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s084~right-vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Left vagus nerve","D. Right vagus nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right vagus nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right vagus nerve.</b> The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the posterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front; azygos arch lateral to it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch of the azygos vein; the nerve passing medial to the arch on the side of the trachea and then behind the right lung root is the right vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s084.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 84"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2877,0.9282,0.6045,0.9897,"",1]],"ar":0.99232}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Left vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_148_1418.jpg","cap":"Nerves of lungs and visceral pleura","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.40","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7747,0.0269,0.9316,0.1005]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s174.jpg","cap":"Left vagus nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 174","ov":[[0.214,0.9071,0.4117,0.9885]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s082~left-vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","D. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","E. Left vagus nerve"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left vagus nerve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left vagus nerve.</b> The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the anterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left side of the aortic arch; of the two nerves crossing it, follow the posterior one: if it gives a branch that hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, it is the left vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s082.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 82"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2981,0.9312,0.6008,0.9904,"",1]],"ar":1.0756}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_1046_1141.jpg","cap":"Superior and inferior thyroid arteries and left and right recurrent laryngeal nerves: posterior view","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 8.176","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7323,0.7012,0.981,0.7767]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s086~right-recurrent-laryngeal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Left vagus nerve","C. Right vagus nerve","D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve.</b> The right recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the right vagus in front of the right subclavian artery, hooks under that artery and climbs in the groove between trachea and esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve, as it crosses in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid; laryngeal mucosa below the vocal folds</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right common carotid artery and right thyroid lobe, in front as it ascends</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Loops under the right subclavian artery, then lies in the tracheoesophageal groove</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the right subclavian artery where the right vagus crosses in front of it; trace the branch that curves under the artery and turns upward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s086.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 86"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2946,0.9293,0.8258,0.99,"",1]],"ar":1.03277}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.1186,0.9564,0.161]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s080~left-recurrent-laryngeal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Left vagus nerve","D. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","E. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve.</b> The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid; laryngeal mucosa below the vocal folds</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum, around which it hooks; left thyroid lobe higher up</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea and esophagus, in the left tracheoesophageal groove</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk bifurcation and the underside of the arch; the nerve curving under the arch just lateral to it is the left recurrent laryngeal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s080.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 80"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2981,0.9312,0.8104,0.9904,"",1]],"ar":1.0756}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Anterior vagal trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_148_1418.jpg","cap":"Nerves of lungs and visceral pleura","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.40","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7861,0.6688,0.9091,0.7707]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s178~anterior-vagal-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Anterior vagal trunk","B. Posterior vagal trunk","C. Left vagus nerve","D. Right vagus nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior vagal trunk","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Anterior vagal trunk.</b> The anterior vagal trunk forms from the esophageal plexus on the front of the esophagus at the esophageal hiatus and branches over the front of the stomach.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Esophageal plexus, mainly left vagus fibers, at the esophageal hiatus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior stomach (anterior gastric branches); hepatic branches in the lesser omentum to liver and pylorus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum over the esophagus; left lobe of the liver in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the abdominal esophagus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal esophagus at the hiatus and look on its front surface for the nerve running onto the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s178.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 178"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1867,0.9209,0.4133,0.9889,"",1]],"ar":0.57}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Posterior vagal trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg","cap":"Innervation of small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51","tb":1,"ov":[[0.456,0.5717,0.5661,0.6624]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s180~posterior-vagal-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pericardiacophrenic artery","B. Right vagus nerve","C. Posterior vagal trunk","D. Anterior vagal trunk","E. Left vagus nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior vagal trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Posterior vagal trunk.</b> The posterior vagal trunk lies on the back of the esophagus at the esophageal hiatus, carrying mostly right vagus fibres to the posterior stomach and celiac plexus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Esophageal plexus, mainly right vagus fibers, at the esophageal hiatus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior stomach (posterior gastric branches); celiac branch to the celiac plexus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Posterior surface of the abdominal esophagus</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right crus of the diaphragm, behind the esophagus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Pull the abdominal esophagus forward and look on its back surface, near the right crus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s180.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 180"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9182,0.4533,0.9883,"",1]],"ar":0.55}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7509,0.2287,0.8677,0.2726]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s016.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 16","ov":[[0.1847,0.904,0.3617,0.9879]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s028~phrenic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Right vagus nerve","C. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","D. Left vagus nerve","E. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Phrenic nerve (right/left).</b> Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s028.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 28"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.192,0.9137,0.3684,0.9872,"",1]],"ar":0.53279,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s016~phrenic-nerve"}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Right vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_260_1619.jpg","cap":"Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.35","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7188,0.0717,0.8824,0.1127]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s084.jpg","cap":"Right vagus nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 84","ov":[[0.2857,0.9262,0.6065,0.9917]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s176~right-vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Posterior vagal trunk","C. Left vagus nerve","D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right vagus nerve","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right vagus nerve.</b> The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the posterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front; azygos arch lateral to it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch of the azygos vein; the nerve passing medial to the arch on the side of the trachea and then behind the right lung root is the right vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s176.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 176"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9117,0.4247,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.50786,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s084~right-vagus-nerve"}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":11,"label":"Left vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_148_1418.jpg","cap":"Nerves of lungs and visceral pleura","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.40","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7747,0.0269,0.9316,0.1005]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s082.jpg","cap":"Left vagus nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 82","ov":[[0.2961,0.9292,0.6028,0.9924]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s174~left-vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Right vagus nerve","D. Left vagus nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left vagus nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left vagus nerve.</b> The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the anterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left side of the aortic arch; of the two nerves crossing it, follow the posterior one: if it gives a branch that hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, it is the left vagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s174.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 174"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9091,0.4097,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49286,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s082~left-vagus-nerve"}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":12,"label":"Thoracic (descending) aorta","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0079,0.7036,0.1558,0.7672]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s172~thoracic-aorta","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Posterior intercostal vein","B. Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","C. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","E. Thoracic (descending) aorta"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic (descending) aorta","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Thoracic (descending) aorta.</b> The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior intercostal (3rd–11th), subcostal, bronchial and esophageal arteries; becomes abdominal aorta at T12</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lung root, pericardium over the left atrium, then the esophagus in front lower down</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the thoracic vertebral bodies; hemiazygos veins cross behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Pull the left lung forward and look behind its root; the large artery on the left side of the vertebral column, with paired intercostal branches, is the thoracic aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s172.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 172"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9118,0.5083,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.50857}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":12,"label":"Posterior intercostal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_125_1361.jpg","cap":"Veins of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.20","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0193,0.4235,0.213,0.4709]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s182~posterior-intercostal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left brachiocephalic vein","B. Thoracic (descending) aorta","C. Posterior intercostal vein","D. Azygos vein","E. Hemiazygos vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior intercostal vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Posterior intercostal vein.</b> Each posterior intercostal vein runs highest in the costal groove, above its artery and nerve, draining into the azygos vein on the right or hemiazygos veins on the left.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Intercostal space, plus a dorsal branch from the back muscles and vertebral venous plexus</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein (right); hemiazygos or accessory hemiazygos (left); upper spaces to superior intercostal veins</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle; highest in the costal groove (vein, artery, nerve)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal muscle, then endothoracic fascia and costal pleura</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein (right) or hemiazygos vein (left) on the vertebral bodies first; the segmental veins entering it from each intercostal space are the posterior intercostal veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s182.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 182"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.899,0.5083,0.9845,"",1]],"ar":0.44143}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":13,"label":"Azygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0072,0.39,0.1288,0.4303]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s154.jpg","cap":"Azygos vein on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 154","ov":[[0.1704,0.9093,0.3474,0.989]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st12~azygos-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Azygos vein","B. Left brachiocephalic vein","C. Posterior intercostal vein","D. Thoracic duct","E. Accessory hemiazygos vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Azygos vein","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Azygos vein.</b> The azygos vein climbs the right side of the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, then arches over the right main bronchus at T4 into the superior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right ascending lumbar and subcostal veins; then right posterior intercostals, hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Esophagus and right lung root in front; costal pleura on its lateral side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right side of the thoracic vertebral bodies, crossing the right posterior intercostal arteries</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SVC first, then the vein that arches over the right main bronchus into its back; follow that vein down along the right side of the vertebral bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st12.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 12"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2273,0.9508,0.4529,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.26413}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":13,"label":"Hemiazygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_200_1515.jpg","cap":"Azygos system of veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.74","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6063,0.4533,0.8654,0.5023]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s186~hemiazygos-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Azygos vein","B. Posterior intercostal vein","C. Left brachiocephalic vein","D. Hemiazygos vein","E. Thoracic duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemiazygos vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hemiazygos vein.</b> The hemiazygos vein climbs the left side of the lower thoracic vertebrae, then crosses right at T9 behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct to join the azygos.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left ascending lumbar and left subcostal veins, plus the lowest left posterior intercostals</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein on the right; look for a vein joining it from the left across the front of the lower vertebral bodies behind the aorta, and follow it back to the left side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s186.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 186"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9109,0.4133,0.9869,"",1]],"ar":0.50286}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":13,"label":"Accessory hemiazygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_184_141.jpg","cap":"Veins of the thoracic wall","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.30","tb":1,"ov":[[0.768,0.2147,0.9946,0.2666]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s188~accessory-hemiazygos-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Thoracic duct","B. Azygos vein","C. Posterior intercostal vein","D. Accessory hemiazygos vein","E. Left brachiocephalic vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Accessory hemiazygos vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Accessory hemiazygos vein.</b> The accessory hemiazygos vein runs down the left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies, draining upper left intercostal veins, then crosses behind the aorta to the azygos vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left posterior intercostal veins of about the 4th–8th spaces; sometimes left bronchial veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta and thoracic duct</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein, then look for veins joining it from the left: the higher crossing (about T7–T8), fed from the upper left intercostal spaces, is the accessory hemiazygos.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s188.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 188"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9109,0.5261,0.9869,"",1]],"ar":0.50286}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":13,"label":"Thoracic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.1827,0.825,0.2248]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s184~thoracic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Thoracic (descending) aorta","B. Azygos vein","C. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Thoracic duct","E. Accessory hemiazygos vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic duct","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Thoracic duct.</b> The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Cisterna chyli at about L1–L2, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Esophagus in front; azygos vein on its right and descending aorta on its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein and the descending aorta; gently separate the tissue between them behind the esophagus to find the thin whitish duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s184.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 184"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9137,0.389,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.52}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":13,"label":"Azygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0072,0.39,0.1288,0.4303]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st12.jpg","cap":"Azygos vein on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 12","ov":[[0.2253,0.9488,0.4549,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s154~azygos-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Thoracic duct","B. Hemiazygos vein","C. Right brachiocephalic vein","D. Left brachiocephalic vein","E. Azygos vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Azygos vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Azygos vein.</b> The azygos vein climbs the right side of the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, then arches over the right main bronchus at T4 into the superior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right ascending lumbar and subcostal veins; then right posterior intercostals, hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Esophagus and right lung root in front; costal pleura on its lateral side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right side of the thoracic vertebral bodies, crossing the right posterior intercostal arteries</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SVC first, then the vein that arches over the right main bronchus into its back; follow that vein down along the right side of the vertebral bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s154.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 154"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1724,0.9113,0.3454,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.50571,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st12~azygos-vein"}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":14,"label":"Sympathetic trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg","cap":"Innervation of small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0158,0.052,0.2201,0.0932]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s493.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic trunk on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 493","ov":[[0.214,0.9085,0.4281,0.9888]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s190~sympathetic-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Sympathetic trunk","C. Anterior vagal trunk","D. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","E. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic trunk","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Sympathetic trunk.</b> The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gray rami to every spinal nerve; cardiac, pulmonary and splanchnic branches to the viscera</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura (thorax); IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right lumbar trunk, aorta beside the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Heads of the ribs in the thorax; sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies in the abdomen</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Strip the costal pleura from the posterior thoracic wall beside the vertebral bodies and look for the cord running vertically over the rib heads.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s190.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 190"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9139,0.4261,0.9875,"",1]],"ar":0.52143}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":14,"label":"Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_423_467.jpg","cap":"Nerve fibers passing through the abdominal prevertebral plexus and ganglia","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.158","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6661,0.6678,0.8699,0.7053]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s192~greater-splanchnic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Sympathetic trunk","C. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Left vagus nerve","E. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve.</b> The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Celiac ganglion (foregut), after piercing the crus; some fibers reach the suprarenal medulla</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies, medial to the sympathetic trunk</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the sympathetic trunk on the rib heads, then follow the medial branches from its middle ganglia as they join into one nerve heading down onto the vertebral bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s192.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 192"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.913,0.4969,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.51571}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":14,"label":"Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_396_432.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.132","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7944,0.6236,0.9917,0.6689]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s194~lesser-splanchnic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sympathetic trunk","B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","C. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","D. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","E. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve.</b> The lesser splanchnic nerve leaves the T10 and T11 sympathetic ganglia, descends lateral to the greater splanchnic nerve, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the aorticorenal ganglion.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglia T10–T11</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Aorticorenal ganglion (midgut and kidney), after piercing the crus of the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sides of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies, lateral to the greater splanchnic nerve</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the sympathetic trunk down to the T10-T11 ganglia and look for the small nerve leaving them medially, below the greater splanchnic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s194.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 194"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9138,0.4897,0.9875,"",1]],"ar":0.52071}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":14,"label":"Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_396_432.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.132","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7745,0.7125,0.9616,0.7578]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s196~least-splanchnic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","C. Right vagus nerve","D. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","E. Sympathetic trunk"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve.</b> The least splanchnic nerve is a small branch of the T12 sympathetic ganglion, below the lesser splanchnic nerve, that ends in the renal plexus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglion T12 (small and inconstant)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Renal plexus (kidney)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Side of the T12 vertebral body, beside the sympathetic trunk</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the lowest thoracic ganglion (T12) on the sympathetic trunk and look for a fine medial branch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s196.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 196"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9166,0.4761,0.988,"",1]],"ar":0.53929}]},{"l":"VB-T3","u":14,"label":"Sympathetic trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg","cap":"Innervation of small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0158,0.052,0.2201,0.0932]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s190.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic trunk on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 190","ov":[[0.214,0.9119,0.4281,0.9895]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s493~sympathetic-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Posterior vagal trunk","C. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","E. Sympathetic trunk"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic trunk","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Sympathetic trunk.</b> The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gray rami to every spinal nerve; cardiac, pulmonary and splanchnic branches to the viscera</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura (thorax); IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right lumbar trunk, aorta beside the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Heads of the ribs in the thorax; sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies in the abdomen</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Strip the costal pleura from the posterior thoracic wall beside the vertebral bodies and look for the cord running vertically over the rib heads.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s493.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 493"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9105,0.4261,0.9868,"",1]],"ar":0.50071,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s190~sympathetic-trunk"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":15,"label":"Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8133,0.5467,0.9738,0.5909]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s018~fibrous-pericardium","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Middle cardiac vein","B. Pulmonary trunk","C. Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)","D. Papillary muscle","E. Left ventricle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pericardium (fibrous pericardium).</b> The fibrous pericardium is the tough sac around the heart in the middle mediastinum, fused below to the diaphragm's central tendon, with the phrenic nerves on its sides.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Parietal serous pericardium, fused to its inner surface; pericardial cavity beyond</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the phrenic nerve on the side of the heart; the membrane it lies on, deep to the mediastinal pleura, is the fibrous pericardium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s018.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 18"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9055,0.389,0.9858,"",1]],"ar":0.47286}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":16,"label":"Right atrium","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg","cap":"Interior of right ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0068,0.381,0.1198,0.4295]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s070~right-atrium","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right auricle","B. Right atrium","C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","D. Left atrium","E. Left auricle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right atrium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right atrium.</b> On a frontal chest film the right atrium forms the right heart border, receiving the superior vena cava above, the inferior vena cava below and the coronary sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium with the right phrenic nerve; right lung edge and right 3rd–6th costal cartilages</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right pulmonary veins and left atrium behind it; IVC (inferior vena cava) enters below</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the superior or inferior vena cava to the heart; the chamber they open into is the right atrium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s070.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 70"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9199,0.389,0.9887,"",1]],"ar":0.56286}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":16,"label":"Left atrium","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_171_1463.jpg","cap":"Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7421,0.2619,0.8559,0.3071]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s078~left-atrium","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pulmonary trunk","B. Left atrium","C. Right atrium","D. Left auricle","E. Right auricle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left atrium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left atrium.</b> The left atrium forms most of the back of the heart, receiving the four pulmonary veins and lying directly in front of the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk in front (transverse pericardial sinus between)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus directly behind it (oblique pericardial sinus between), then the descending aorta</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary veins at the lung roots and follow them medially to the chamber they enter on the back of the heart.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s078.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 78"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9025,0.389,0.9852,"",1]],"ar":0.45786}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":16,"label":"Right auricle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1358,0.339,0.2518,0.3814]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s074~right-auricle","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right atrium","B. Left atrium","C. Great cardiac vein","D. Left auricle","E. Right auricle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right auricle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right auricle.</b> The right auricle is the broad triangular pouch off the upper front of the right atrium, overlapping the ascending aorta's root, with the right coronary artery emerging below it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the sternum and anterior edge of the right lung</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Root of the ascending aorta; right coronary artery runs in the groove beneath it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the ascending aorta; the flap of right atrium lying against its right-anterior side is the right auricle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s074.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 74"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8742,0.3464,0.9985,0.3795,"",0],[0.216,0.9184,0.389,0.9884,"",1]],"ar":0.55143}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":16,"label":"Left auricle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_171_1463.jpg","cap":"Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0086,0.3583,0.126,0.4038]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s076~left-auricle","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Left auricle","B. Left atrium","C. Right auricle","D. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","E. Right atrium"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left auricle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left auricle.</b> The left auricle is the small hooked pouch of the left atrium curling around the left side of the pulmonary trunk, with the left coronary artery emerging beneath it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium with the left phrenic nerve, then the left lung's anterior edge</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the pulmonary trunk root, over the left coronary and circumflex arteries</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk; the small flap of atrium curling around its left side is the left auricle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s076.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 76"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.4983,0.354,0.5487,0.387,"",0],[0.216,0.9154,0.389,0.9878,"",1]],"ar":0.53143}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":17,"label":"Right ventricle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_166_1454.jpg","cap":"Shape, orientation, surfaces, and borders of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.52","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0117,0.8767,0.1788,0.9233]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s072~right-ventricle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right auricle","B. Left ventricle","C. Apex of the heart","D. Right ventricle","E. Pulmonary valve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right ventricle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right ventricle.</b> The right ventricle forms most of the heart's front surface and inferior border, behind the sternum, between the right atrium and the pulmonary trunk rising from its top.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the body of the sternum and left costal cartilages</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left ventricle (across the septum) behind; central tendon of the diaphragm below</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and follow it down to the chamber it leaves; that anterior chamber is the right ventricle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s072.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 72"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2211,0.9297,0.3999,0.9901,"",1]],"ar":0.67662}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":17,"label":"Left ventricle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_171_1463.jpg","cap":"Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7444,0.5264,0.8776,0.5719]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s088~left-ventricle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left ventricle","B. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","C. Inferior vena cava","D. Right ventricle","E. Apex of the heart"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left ventricle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left ventricle.</b> The left ventricle forms the apex and lower left heart border, behind and left of the right ventricle, with a wall two to three times thicker.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right ventricle in front; pericardium, left phrenic nerve and left lung on its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm under its diaphragmatic surface; left lung against its left surface</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the apex and the anterior interventricular groove; the thick-walled chamber to the left of the groove is the left ventricle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s088.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 88"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2193,0.9117,0.3924,0.9868,"",1]],"ar":0.50993}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":18,"label":"Inferior vena cava","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg","cap":"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0069,0.0121,0.1738,0.0546]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s349.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 349","ov":[[0.214,0.9089,0.4239,0.9889]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s463.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 463","ov":[[0.3084,0.9344,0.6008,0.9935]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s094~inferior-vena-cava","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right auricle","B. Superior vena cava","C. Pulmonary trunk","D. Ascending aorta","E. Inferior vena cava"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior vena cava","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Inferior vena cava.</b> The inferior vena cava rises just right of the aorta, passes behind the duodenum, pancreatic head and liver, and pierces the diaphragm at T8 into the right atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right and left common iliac veins at L5, behind the right common iliac artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium, lower part, after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>3rd part of duodenum, head of pancreas, portal vein (epiploic foramen), then bare area of liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lumbar vertebral bodies, right psoas and right crus; right renal artery passes behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta on the vertebral body; the larger, flatter vein immediately to its right is the IVC. Higher up, follow it into the groove on the back of the liver to the right of the caudate lobe and on through the diaphragm into the right atrium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s094.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 94"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9061,0.4219,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.47643}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":18,"label":"Pulmonary trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg","cap":"Interior of right ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54","tb":1,"ov":[[0.008,0.087,0.1549,0.1358]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s064~pulmonary-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ascending aorta","B. Pulmonary valve","C. Inferior vena cava","D. Pulmonary trunk","E. Brachiocephalic trunk"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary trunk","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pulmonary trunk.</b> The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle in front of and left of the ascending aorta and divides under the aortic arch into the two pulmonary arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left pulmonary arteries, dividing under the aortic arch at sternal-angle level</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage; left auricle curls around its left side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ascending aorta to its right; transverse pericardial sinus and left atrium behind</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the most anterior great vessel leaving the front of the heart, to the left of the ascending aorta; follow it up to where it forks under the arch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s064.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 64"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2194,0.9191,0.4152,0.9885,"",1]],"ar":0.5649}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":18,"label":"Ascending aorta","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1356.jpg","cap":"Arteries of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0147,0.2798,0.2359,0.3268]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s066~ascending-aorta","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pulmonary trunk","B. Ascending aorta","C. Great cardiac vein","D. Aortic valve","E. Inferior vena cava"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending aorta","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ascending aorta.</b> The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left ventricle, through the aortic valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left coronary arteries (its only branches); continues as the arch at the sternal angle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right auricle and pulmonary trunk overlap its root; then pericardium and sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse pericardial sinus, then the right pulmonary artery and left atrium behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk leaving the front of the heart; the larger vessel just to its right, between it and the superior vena cava, is the ascending aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s066.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 66"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9225,0.4069,0.9892,"",1]],"ar":0.58286}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":18,"label":"Inferior vena cava","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg","cap":"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0069,0.0121,0.1738,0.0546]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s094.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 94","ov":[[0.214,0.9041,0.4239,0.9879]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s463.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 463","ov":[[0.3084,0.9344,0.6008,0.9935]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s349~inferior-vena-cava","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ascending aorta","B. Inferior vena cava","C. Pulmonary trunk","D. Superior vena cava","E. Chordae tendineae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior vena cava","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior vena cava.</b> The inferior vena cava rises just right of the aorta, passes behind the duodenum, pancreatic head and liver, and pierces the diaphragm at T8 into the right atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right and left common iliac veins at L5, behind the right common iliac artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium, lower part, after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>3rd part of duodenum, head of pancreas, portal vein (epiploic foramen), then bare area of liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lumbar vertebral bodies, right psoas and right crus; right renal artery passes behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta on the vertebral body; the larger, flatter vein immediately to its right is the IVC. Higher up, follow it into the groove on the back of the liver to the right of the caudate lobe and on through the diaphragm into the right atrium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s349.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 349"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9109,0.4219,0.9869,"",1]],"ar":0.50286,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s094~inferior-vena-cava"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":18,"label":"Inferior vena cava","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg","cap":"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0069,0.0121,0.1738,0.0546]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s094.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 94","ov":[[0.214,0.9041,0.4239,0.9879]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s349.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 349","ov":[[0.214,0.9089,0.4239,0.9889]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s463~inferior-vena-cava","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Anterior cardiac veins","B. Pulmonary trunk","C. Inferior vena cava","D. Ascending aorta","E. Superior vena cava"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior vena cava","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Inferior vena cava.</b> The inferior vena cava rises just right of the aorta, passes behind the duodenum, pancreatic head and liver, and pierces the diaphragm at T8 into the right atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right and left common iliac veins at L5, behind the right common iliac artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium, lower part, after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>3rd part of duodenum, head of pancreas, portal vein (epiploic foramen), then bare area of liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lumbar vertebral bodies, right psoas and right crus; right renal artery passes behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta on the vertebral body; the larger, flatter vein immediately to its right is the IVC. Higher up, follow it into the groove on the back of the liver to the right of the caudate lobe and on through the diaphragm into the right atrium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s463.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 463"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.3104,0.9364,0.5988,0.9915,"",1]],"ar":1.05006,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s094~inferior-vena-cava"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":19,"label":"Right coronary artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_175_1470.jpg","cap":"Coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7528,0.5789,0.8616,0.6285]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s106~right-coronary-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery","B. Left coronary artery","C. Right marginal artery","D. Posterior interventricular artery","E. Right coronary artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right coronary artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right coronary artery.</b> The right coronary artery leaves the right aortic sinus, passes between the right auricle and pulmonary trunk, and runs in the right coronary groove around to the diaphragmatic surface.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right aortic sinus of the ascending aorta</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right atrium and ventricle, usually the SA (sinoatrial) and AV (atrioventricular) nodes, and posterior interventricular artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right auricle at its origin; epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; anterior cardiac veins cross it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the right atrium and right ventricle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the right auricle and lift it; the artery in the fat of the groove beneath it, coming from the aortic root, is the right coronary artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s106.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 106"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5194,0.9217,1,0.9884,"",1]],"ar":1.40278}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":19,"label":"Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_187_1496.jpg","cap":"","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2555,0.0199,0.4086,0.1177]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s120~sinoatrial-nodal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Posterior interventricular artery","B. Right marginal artery","C. Right coronary artery","D. Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery","E. Circumflex artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery.</b> The sinoatrial nodal artery leaves the first part of the right coronary artery (the circumflex in about 40%) and climbs the right atrium toward the superior vena cava opening.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right coronary artery near its origin (about 60%); otherwise the circumflex artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>SA (sinoatrial) node, where the SVC (superior vena cava) joins the right atrium</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; right auricle near its origin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right atrial wall between right auricle and aorta, curving behind the SVC (superior vena cava)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the first centimetre of the right coronary artery; the small branch climbing up the right atrium toward the superior vena cava is the sinoatrial nodal artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s120.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 120"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1922,0.9219,0.4469,0.9891,"",1]],"ar":0.59224}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":19,"label":"Right marginal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_176_1473.jpg","cap":"Variations in distribution of coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.60","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0266,0.7968,0.1915,0.887]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s112~right-marginal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right marginal artery","B. Posterior interventricular artery","C. Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery","D. Left marginal artery","E. Right coronary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right marginal artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right marginal artery.</b> The right marginal artery is a branch of the right coronary artery that runs along the inferior (acute) border of the heart toward the apex, with the small cardiac vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right coronary artery, at the inferior (acute) border of the heart</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right ventricle, along the inferior border toward the apex</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium, with the small cardiac vein beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right ventricle surface along the acute (inferior) margin</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the right coronary artery to the sharp inferior edge of the heart; the branch turning along that edge toward the apex is the right marginal artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s112.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 112"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1888,0.9177,0.4295,0.9878,"",1]],"ar":0.55514}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":19,"label":"Posterior interventricular artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_214_186.jpg","cap":"Diaphragmatic surface of the heart","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5019,0.9493,0.7671,0.9906]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s122~posterior-interventricular-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right marginal artery","B. Right coronary artery","C. Posterior interventricular artery","D. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","E. Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior interventricular artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Posterior interventricular artery.</b> On a coronary angiogram the posterior interventricular artery runs down the posterior interventricular groove toward the apex, arising from the right coronary artery in right-dominant hearts.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Usually the right coronary artery at the crux (right-dominant); circumflex in left-dominant hearts</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior third of the interventricular septum (septal branches) and adjacent ventricular walls</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat, with the middle cardiac vein beside it; diaphragm below the heart</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular sulcus, over the posterior interventricular septum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn the heart up onto its apex; follow the right coronary artery to the crux, then follow its branch down the posterior interventricular groove.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s122.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 122"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1874,0.9104,0.5333,0.9868,"",1]],"ar":0.5}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":20,"label":"Left coronary artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_175_1470.jpg","cap":"Coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0099,0.3059,0.1652,0.3554]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s108~left-coronary-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left marginal artery","B. Right coronary artery","C. Circumflex artery","D. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","E. Left coronary artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left coronary artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left coronary artery.</b> The left coronary artery leaves the left aortic sinus and runs between the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle before splitting into the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left aortic sinus of the ascending aorta</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular (LAD, left anterior descending) and circumflex arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk in front and left auricle to its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left atrial wall, running behind the pulmonary trunk to the coronary sulcus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and lift the left auricle; the short artery coming from the aortic root between them is the left coronary artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s108.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 108"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5344,0.9282,1,0.9897,"",1]],"ar":1.58163}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":20,"label":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_176_1474.jpg","cap":"Variations in distribution of coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.60","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0301,0.721,0.3788,0.8856]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s110~anterior-interventricular-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Posterior interventricular artery","B. Circumflex artery","C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","D. Left marginal artery","E. Left coronary artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD).</b> The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior two-thirds of septum, front of both ventricles and apex, via diagonal and septal branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; great cardiac vein runs beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle; trace the left coronary artery from between them to the branch that turns straight down the front of the heart.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s110.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 110"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1874,0.9176,0.5226,0.9882,"",1]],"ar":0.54643}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":20,"label":"Circumflex artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_184_1490.jpg","cap":"","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7572,0.4167,0.9248,0.4738]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s114.jpg","cap":"Circumflex artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 114","ov":[[0.1877,0.9198,0.3943,0.9902]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q28~circumflex-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Circumflex artery","B. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","C. Left marginal artery","D. Left coronary artery","E. Posterior interventricular artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumflex artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Circumflex artery.</b> The circumflex artery leaves the left coronary artery and runs left under the left auricle in the coronary groove, beside the great cardiac vein, onto the heart's back.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left atrium and lateral and posterior left ventricle; left marginal artery; SA (sinoatrial) nodal artery in about 40%</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left auricle at its start; great cardiac vein and coronary sinus beside it in the groove</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the left atrium and left ventricle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left coronary artery between the pulmonary trunk and left auricle; follow the branch that turns left under the auricle into the atrioventricular groove.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q28.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 28"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2503,0.9531,0.5035,0.995,"",1]],"ar":1.30458}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":20,"label":"Left marginal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_175_1469.jpg","cap":"Coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.77,0.6531,0.9306,0.705]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s116~left-marginal-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","B. Right marginal artery","C. Left coronary artery","D. Circumflex artery","E. Left marginal artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left marginal artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left marginal artery.</b> The left marginal artery is a branch of the circumflex artery that runs down the left (obtuse) border of the heart over the left ventricle toward the apex.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Circumflex artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left ventricle, along the left (obtuse) border toward the apex</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left ventricle surface along the obtuse (left) margin</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the circumflex artery around the left border of the heart; the branch leaving it to run down that border is the left marginal artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s116.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 116"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5077,0.7042,0.5923,0.7659,"",0],[0.216,0.9127,0.4397,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.51429}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":20,"label":"Circumflex artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_184_1490.jpg","cap":"","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7572,0.4167,0.9248,0.4738]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q28.jpg","cap":"Circumflex artery on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 28","ov":[[0.2483,0.9511,0.5055,0.997]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s114~circumflex-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Posterior left ventricular artery","B. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","C. Left marginal artery","D. Circumflex artery","E. Left coronary artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Circumflex artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Circumflex artery.</b> The circumflex artery leaves the left coronary artery and runs left under the left auricle in the coronary groove, beside the great cardiac vein, onto the heart's back.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left atrium and lateral and posterior left ventricle; left marginal artery; SA (sinoatrial) nodal artery in about 40%</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left auricle at its start; great cardiac vein and coronary sinus beside it in the groove</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the left atrium and left ventricle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left coronary artery between the pulmonary trunk and left auricle; follow the branch that turns left under the auricle into the atrioventricular groove.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s114.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 114"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1897,0.9218,0.3923,0.9882,"",1]],"ar":0.58937,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q28~circumflex-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":21,"label":"Great cardiac vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_232_215.jpg","cap":"Major cardiac veins","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.75","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7445,0.6885,0.993,0.7333]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s098~great-cardiac-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Great cardiac vein","B. Anterior cardiac veins","C. Middle cardiac vein","D. Right brachiocephalic vein","E. Small cardiac vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Great cardiac vein","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Great cardiac vein.</b> The great cardiac vein climbs the anterior interventricular groove beside the anterior interventricular artery, then turns left in the coronary groove to become the coronary sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Apex and front of both ventricles, ascending beside the LAD (left anterior descending) artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus, where the oblique vein of the left atrium joins on the back of the heart</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; left auricle where it turns left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, then the left coronary sulcus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior interventricular artery; the thin-walled vein running beside it up the groove and then turning left is the great cardiac vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s098.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 98"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2196,0.9169,0.4337,0.9872,"",1]],"ar":0.5541}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":21,"label":"Middle cardiac vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_177_1477.jpg","cap":"Cardiac veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8845,0.808,0.9874,0.9114]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s102~middle-cardiac-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Small cardiac vein","B. Great cardiac vein","C. Middle cardiac vein","D. Pulmonary veins","E. Anterior cardiac veins"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle cardiac vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Middle cardiac vein.</b> The middle cardiac vein climbs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the right end of the coronary sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Diaphragmatic surfaces of both ventricles, from near the apex</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus, near its right end</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; diaphragm below the heart</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular sulcus, beside the posterior interventricular artery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the coronary sinus on the back of the heart; the vein entering it from the groove that runs down toward the apex is the middle cardiac vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s102.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 102"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2311,0.9261,0.4657,0.9893,"",1]],"ar":0.67023}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":21,"label":"Small cardiac vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_177_1477.jpg","cap":"Cardiac veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0042,0.683,0.0726,0.8302]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s100~small-cardiac-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hemiazygos vein","B. Anterior cardiac veins","C. Small cardiac vein","D. Middle cardiac vein","E. Great cardiac vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Small cardiac vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Small cardiac vein.</b> The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the heart's inferior border, then back in the right coronary groove to end in the coronary sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Inferior (acute) margin of the right ventricle, running with the right marginal artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus near its right end (sometimes the middle cardiac vein)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right coronary sulcus posteriorly, after running along the acute margin</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the right marginal artery along the inferior border; the vein beside it, curving back into the right coronary groove toward the coronary sinus, is the small cardiac vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s100.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 100"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2178,0.9187,0.4316,0.9876,"",1]],"ar":0.56245}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":21,"label":"Anterior cardiac veins","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_177_1477.jpg","cap":"Cardiac veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0044,0.276,0.0821,0.4227]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s104~anterior-cardiac-veins","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Great cardiac vein","B. Middle cardiac vein","C. Accessory hemiazygos vein","D. Small cardiac vein","E. Anterior cardiac veins"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior cardiac veins","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Anterior cardiac veins.</b> The anterior cardiac veins are small veins on the front of the right ventricle that cross the right coronary artery and open directly into the right atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium directly, not the coronary sinus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the right ventricle, then over the right coronary artery in its sulcus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the right coronary artery in the right coronary groove; the small veins crossing over it from the front of the right ventricle into the right atrium are anterior cardiac veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s104.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 104"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2073,0.9177,0.4621,0.9878,"",1]],"ar":0.56697}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":22,"label":"Opening of the coronary sinus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_168_1458.jpg","cap":"Right atrium of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.53","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7752,0.4776,0.9949,0.5252]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s132~opening-of-coronary-sinus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Opening of the coronary sinus","B. Left coronary artery","C. Pectinate muscles","D. Atrioventricular (AV) node","E. Right coronary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Opening of the coronary sinus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Opening of the coronary sinus.</b> The opening of the coronary sinus lies in the right atrium between the inferior vena cava opening and the tricuspid orifice, guarded by a thin valve fold.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Thin valve of the coronary sinus, guarding its edge</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower septal wall of the right atrium, between the IVC (inferior vena cava) opening and tricuspid orifice</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium, find the inferior vena cava opening, then move toward the tricuspid valve; the smaller hole between them is the coronary sinus opening.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s132.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 132"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2609,0.9415,0.6303,0.9925,"",1]],"ar":1.00626}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":22,"label":"Atrioventricular (AV) node","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_178_1478.jpg","cap":"Conducting system of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.62","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7815,0.1783,0.9887,0.2709]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st15~atrioventricular-node","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Opening of the coronary sinus","B. Crista terminalis","C. Atrioventricular (AV) node","D. Pectinate muscles","E. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Atrioventricular (AV) node","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Atrioventricular (AV) node.</b> The atrioventricular node sits in the lower interatrial septum on the right atrial side, beside the coronary sinus opening, just above the tricuspid valve's septal cusp.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right atrial endocardium in the triangle of Koch, near the coronary sinus opening</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower interatrial septum on the right atrial side, above the septal tricuspid cusp</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the coronary sinus opening in the right atrium; the node sits in the septum next to it, where the conduction pathway enters the top of the interventricular septum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st15.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 15"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2794,0.9492,0.6631,0.9942,"",1]],"ar":1.49534}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":22,"label":"Pectinate muscles","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_442_1973.jpg","cap":"Transitions occurring at pectinate line","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0292,0.604,0.9822,0.6624]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s124~pectinate-muscles","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Opening of the coronary sinus","B. Papillary muscle","C. Atrioventricular (AV) node","D. Pectinate muscles","E. External intercostal muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pectinate muscles","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pectinate muscles.</b> The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the crista terminalis; the ridges running forward from it across the anterior wall and into the auricle are the pectinate muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s124.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 124"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.201,0.9127,0.4119,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.51429}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Trabeculae carneae","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4411,0.9498,0.622,0.9898]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s126~trabeculae-carneae","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Papillary muscle","B. Chordae tendineae","C. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","D. Trabeculae carneae","E. Small cardiac vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Trabeculae carneae","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Trabeculae carneae.</b> The trabeculae carneae are the rough muscular ridges lining the inner walls of both ventricles below the valves, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle; the rough, interlacing muscle on the walls below the valves, apart from the large papillary muscles, is the trabeculae carneae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s126.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 126"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2058,0.9229,0.4353,0.9893,"",1]],"ar":0.59942}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7714,0.7042,0.9939,0.7446]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s049.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 49","ov":[[0.1004,0.9559,0.386,0.9982]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q21.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 21","ov":[[0.2851,0.9566,0.6317,0.9981]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s146.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 146","ov":[[0.214,0.9051,0.491,0.9881]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st14~septomarginal-trabecula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)","B. Chordae tendineae","C. Papillary muscle","D. Trabeculae carneae","E. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band).</b> The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st14.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 14"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2145,0.9499,0.7771,0.9943,"",1]],"ar":1.17146}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Papillary muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_223_199.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the left ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0062,0.5785,0.1699,0.6198]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s144~papillary-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Papillary muscle","B. Pectinate muscles","C. Chordae tendineae","D. Trabeculae carneae","E. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Papillary muscle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Papillary muscle.</b> Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner ventricular wall (right: anterior, posterior, septal; left: anterior, posterior)</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Chordae tendineae, to the cusps of the tricuspid or mitral valve</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; chordae tendineae attach at its tip</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ventricular wall muscle, from which it projects into the cavity</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle and follow the chordae tendineae down from a valve cusp; the muscle mound they attach to is a papillary muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s144.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 144"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9124,0.4204,0.9872,"",1]],"ar":0.51214}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Chordae tendineae","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_223_199.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the left ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0912,0.4204,0.2682,0.4617]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s142~chordae-tendineae","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Chordae tendineae","B. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","C. Trabeculae carneae","D. Papillary muscle","E. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Chordae tendineae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Chordae tendineae.</b> The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Fibrous collagen cords covered by endocardium</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Papillary muscle tips to the free edges and ventricular surfaces of tricuspid and mitral cusps</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle, find a cusp of the atrioventricular valve, and follow the threads hanging from its edge down to a papillary muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s142.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 142"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9139,0.4319,0.9875,"",1]],"ar":0.52143}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7714,0.7042,0.9939,0.7446]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st14.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 14","ov":[[0.2125,0.9479,0.7791,0.9963]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q21.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 21","ov":[[0.2851,0.9566,0.6317,0.9981]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s146.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 146","ov":[[0.214,0.9051,0.491,0.9881]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s049~septomarginal-trabecula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Papillary muscle","B. Pulmonary valve","C. Trabeculae carneae","D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","E. Chordae tendineae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band).</b> The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s049.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 49"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1024,0.9579,0.384,0.9962,"",1]],"ar":1.14332,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st14~septomarginal-trabecula"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7714,0.7042,0.9939,0.7446]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st14.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 14","ov":[[0.2125,0.9479,0.7791,0.9963]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s049.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 49","ov":[[0.1004,0.9559,0.386,0.9982]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s146.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 146","ov":[[0.214,0.9051,0.491,0.9881]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q21~septomarginal-trabecula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","B. Aortic valve","C. Papillary muscle","D. Chordae tendineae","E. Trabeculae carneae"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band).</b> The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q21.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 21"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2871,0.9586,0.6297,0.9961,"",1]],"ar":1.47739,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st14~septomarginal-trabecula"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":23,"label":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7714,0.7042,0.9939,0.7446]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st14.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 14","ov":[[0.2125,0.9479,0.7791,0.9963]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s049.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 49","ov":[[0.1004,0.9559,0.386,0.9982]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q21.jpg","cap":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 21","ov":[[0.2851,0.9566,0.6317,0.9981]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s146~septomarginal-trabecula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Aortic valve","B. Chordae tendineae","C. Papillary muscle","D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","E. Trabeculae carneae"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band).</b> The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s146.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 146"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9071,0.489,0.9861,"",1]],"ar":0.48143,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st14~septomarginal-trabecula"}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":24,"label":"Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg","cap":"Interior of right ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0075,0.9016,0.1576,0.9505]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s134~tricuspid-valve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pulmonary valve","B. Aortic valve","C. Cusp of the mitral valve","D. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","E. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve.</b> The tricuspid valve guards the opening from the right atrium into the right ventricle, its three cusps tied by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles, behind the lower sternal body.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right half of the sternal body opposite the 4th intercostal space</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Mitral valve behind and to its left; septal cusp against the interventricular septum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and look into the opening that leads forward into the right ventricle; the cusps around it are the tricuspid valve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s134.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 134"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9198,0.3947,0.9887,"",1]],"ar":0.56214}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":24,"label":"Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_170_1462.jpg","cap":"Valves of heart and great vessels","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.55","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.9396,0.9209,0.9777]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s136~mitral-valve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","B. Cusp of the mitral valve","C. Pulmonary valve","D. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve","E. Aortic valve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve.</b> The mitral valve is the two-cusp valve between the left atrium and left ventricle, tied by chordae tendineae to two papillary muscles, its anterior cusp beside the aortic opening.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left half of the sternum at the 4th costal cartilage; right ventricle in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left atrium behind it, then the esophagus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the left atrium from behind and look into the opening leading forward into the left ventricle; the two cusps around it are the mitral valve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s136.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 136"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9214,0.389,0.989,"",1]],"ar":0.57357}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":24,"label":"Pulmonary valve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_170_1462.jpg","cap":"Valves of heart and great vessels","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.55","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7918,0.5314,0.8876,0.5905]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s140~pulmonary-valve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve","B. Aortic valve","C. Cusp of the mitral valve","D. Pulmonary valve","E. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary valve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pulmonary valve.</b> The pulmonary valve is the three-cusp semilunar valve at the top of the right ventricle's outflow tract, in front of, above and left of the aortic valve.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Medial end of the left 3rd costal cartilage and adjacent sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Aortic valve, behind, below and to its right</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the pulmonary trunk down to the heart and open it; the three pocket-shaped cusps at its base are the pulmonary valve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s140.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 140"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9046,0.4097,0.9856,"",1]],"ar":0.46857}]},{"l":"VB-T4","u":24,"label":"Aortic valve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_170_1462.jpg","cap":"Valves of heart and great vessels","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.55","tb":1,"ov":[[0.797,0.6072,0.9005,0.6455]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s138~aortic-valve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","B. Aortic valve","C. Pulmonary valve","D. Cusp of the mitral valve","E. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Aortic valve","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Aortic valve.</b> The aortic valve lies between the left ventricle and ascending aorta, behind the sternum at the third intercostal space, behind and right of the pulmonary valve.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pulmonary valve and right ventricular outflow in front; left sternum at the 3rd intercostal space</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left atrium behind it; anterior mitral cusp continuous with its base</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the ascending aorta just above the heart and look down; the three cusps at its base, with coronary openings above two of them, form the aortic valve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s138.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 138"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9158,0.389,0.9879,"",1]],"ar":0.53357}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":25,"label":"External oblique muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_220_1563.jpg","cap":"Inferior abdominal wall and inguinal region of a male","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.8","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0059,0.1524,0.1546,0.1959]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s203~external-oblique-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pyramidalis muscle","B. Internal oblique muscle","C. Transversus abdominis muscle","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. External oblique muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"External oblique muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. External oblique muscle.</b> In axial sections the external oblique is the outermost flat muscle of the lateral abdominal wall, superficial to internal oblique and transversus abdominis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>External surfaces of ribs 5–12</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, pubic tubercle and anterior half of the iliac crest</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Investing fascia, then subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa's and Camper's layers) and skin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Internal oblique muscle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Clear the superficial fascia over the side of the abdomen; the first muscle layer, whose upper digitations interlock with serratus anterior on the lower ribs, is external oblique.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s203.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 203"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9117,0.4847,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.50786}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":25,"label":"Internal oblique muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_218_1560.jpg","cap":"Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4049,0.0569,0.5834,0.1056]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s044.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique muscle on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 44","ov":[[0.0969,0.9363,0.4739,0.9944]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s205.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique muscle on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 205","ov":[[0.214,0.9118,0.4789,0.9895]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st16~internal-oblique-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pyramidalis muscle","B. Transversus abdominis muscle","C. Internal oblique muscle","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. External oblique muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Internal oblique muscle.</b> The internal oblique is the middle flat abdominal muscle, between the external oblique outside and transversus abdominis inside, with fibres running up and toward the midline.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inferior borders of ribs 10–12, linea alba, and pubic crest via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External oblique muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Cut and reflect external oblique; the next fleshy layer, with fibres crossing those of external oblique, is internal oblique; its lowest fibres arch over the spermatic cord and continue onto it as cremaster.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st16.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 16"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2084,0.9508,0.5619,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.16068}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":25,"label":"Transversus abdominis muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_312_317.jpg","cap":"Transversus abdominis muscle and its aponeurosis","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0409,0.4658,0.2329,0.5074]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s207.jpg","cap":"Transversus abdominis muscle on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 207","ov":[[0.214,0.9064,0.5617,0.9884]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q30~transversus-abdominis-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Rectus abdominis muscle","B. External oblique muscle","C. Pyramidalis muscle","D. Internal oblique muscle","E. Transversus abdominis muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversus abdominis muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Transversus abdominis muscle.</b> In axial sections transversus abdominis is the innermost of the three flat lateral muscles, deep to internal oblique and just outside the transversalis fascia and peritoneum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 7–12, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral third of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, and pubic crest and pecten pubis via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal oblique muscle, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Reflect internal oblique; the deepest fleshy layer, with horizontal fibres and the lower thoracic and L1 nerves lying on its outer surface, is transversus abdominis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q30.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 30"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2596,0.9639,0.6944,0.9973,"",1]],"ar":1.74362}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":25,"label":"Internal oblique muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_218_1560.jpg","cap":"Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4049,0.0569,0.5834,0.1056]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st16.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique muscle on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 16","ov":[[0.2064,0.9488,0.5639,0.9965]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s205.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique muscle on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 205","ov":[[0.214,0.9118,0.4789,0.9895]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s044~internal-oblique-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Rectus abdominis muscle","B. Internal oblique muscle","C. External oblique muscle","D. Pyramidalis muscle","E. Transversus abdominis muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Internal oblique muscle.</b> The internal oblique is the middle flat abdominal muscle, between the external oblique outside and transversus abdominis inside, with fibres running up and toward the midline.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inferior borders of ribs 10–12, linea alba, and pubic crest via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External oblique muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Cut and reflect external oblique; the next fleshy layer, with fibres crossing those of external oblique, is internal oblique; its lowest fibres arch over the spermatic cord and continue onto it as cremaster.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s044.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 44"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.0989,0.9383,0.4719,0.9924,"",1]],"ar":0.74143,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st16~internal-oblique-muscle"}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":25,"label":"Internal oblique muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_218_1560.jpg","cap":"Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4049,0.0569,0.5834,0.1056]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st16.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique muscle on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 16","ov":[[0.2064,0.9488,0.5639,0.9965]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s044.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique muscle on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 44","ov":[[0.0969,0.9363,0.4739,0.9944]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s205~internal-oblique-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Internal oblique muscle","B. Pyramidalis muscle","C. External oblique muscle","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. Transversus abdominis muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique muscle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Internal oblique muscle.</b> The internal oblique is the middle flat abdominal muscle, between the external oblique outside and transversus abdominis inside, with fibres running up and toward the midline.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inferior borders of ribs 10–12, linea alba, and pubic crest via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External oblique muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Cut and reflect external oblique; the next fleshy layer, with fibres crossing those of external oblique, is internal oblique; its lowest fibres arch over the spermatic cord and continue onto it as cremaster.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s205.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 205"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9138,0.4769,0.9875,"",1]],"ar":0.52071,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st16~internal-oblique-muscle"}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":25,"label":"Transversus abdominis muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_312_317.jpg","cap":"Transversus abdominis muscle and its aponeurosis","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0409,0.4658,0.2329,0.5074]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q30.jpg","cap":"Transversus abdominis muscle on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 30","ov":[[0.2576,0.9619,0.6964,0.9993]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s207~transversus-abdominis-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pyramidalis muscle","B. Internal oblique muscle","C. External oblique muscle","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. Transversus abdominis muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversus abdominis muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Transversus abdominis muscle.</b> In axial sections transversus abdominis is the innermost of the three flat lateral muscles, deep to internal oblique and just outside the transversalis fascia and peritoneum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 7–12, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral third of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, and pubic crest and pecten pubis via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal oblique muscle, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Reflect internal oblique; the deepest fleshy layer, with horizontal fibres and the lower thoracic and L1 nerves lying on its outer surface, is transversus abdominis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s207.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 207"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9084,0.5597,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.48857,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q30~transversus-abdominis-muscle"}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":26,"label":"Rectus abdominis muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_219_1561.jpg","cap":"Anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.7","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.2974,0.9103,0.3361]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s209~rectus-abdominis-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","B. Internal oblique muscle","C. Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)","D. Pyramidalis muscle","E. Rectus abdominis muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectus abdominis muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Rectus abdominis muscle.</b> Rectus abdominis is the paired vertical strap muscle on each side of the linea alba, running from the pubis to the xiphoid and lower costal cartilages.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Pubic symphysis and pubic crest</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5–7</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior rectus sheath, fused to its tendinous intersections</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line; transversalis fascia below it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the linea alba in the midline, open the anterior rectus sheath beside it, and the vertical-fibred muscle inside is rectus abdominis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s209.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 209"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.91,0.5033,0.9867,"",1]],"ar":0.49786}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":26,"label":"Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_219_1561.jpg","cap":"Anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.7","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7665,0.4792,0.9424,0.5177]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s231~tendinous-intersections","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)","B. Rectus abdominis muscle","C. Linea alba","D. Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","E. Pyramidalis muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis).</b> The tendinous intersections are three horizontal fibrous bands across rectus abdominis, at the umbilicus, near the xiphoid and midway between, fused to the anterior rectus sheath.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior rectus sheath, to which they are firmly fused</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Deeper rectus abdominis fibres and the posterior rectus sheath, to which they do not attach</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the anterior rectus sheath; the horizontal lines where the sheath stays stuck to the muscle are the tendinous intersections.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s231.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 231"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9213,0.7183,0.989,"",1]],"ar":0.57286}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":26,"label":"Linea alba","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_220_1563.jpg","cap":"Inferior abdominal wall and inguinal region of a male","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.8","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7132,0.2295,0.8146,0.2732]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s235~linea-alba","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","B. Pyramidalis muscle","C. Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. Linea alba"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Linea alba","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Linea alba.</b> The linea alba is the midline fibrous seam between the two rectus abdominis muscles, running from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis and containing the umbilicus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Interlacing aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Subcutaneous tissue and skin; the umbilicus lies within it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Start at the umbilicus; the white vertical seam running above and below it between the two recti is the linea alba.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s235.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 235"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3075,0.9364,0.5518,0.9915,"",1]],"ar":1.04014}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":26,"label":"Pyramidalis muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_219_1561.jpg","cap":"Anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.7","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.2974,0.9103,0.3361]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s211~pyramidalis-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","B. Rectus abdominis muscle","C. Pyramidalis muscle","D. Linea alba","E. External oblique muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyramidalis muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyramidalis muscle.</b> Pyramidalis is a small triangular muscle in front of the lowest part of rectus abdominis inside the rectus sheath, running from the pubis up to the linea alba.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the pubis and the anterior pubic ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, below the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior rectus sheath</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lowest part of rectus abdominis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the anterior rectus sheath just above the pubis; a small triangle of muscle lying on the front of the lowest rectus and pointing up toward the linea alba is pyramidalis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s211.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 211"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9076,0.4397,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.48429}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":26,"label":"Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_221_1564.jpg","cap":"Formation of rectus sheath and neurovascular structures of anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.9","tb":1,"ov":[[0.676,0.7014,0.804,0.7415]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s233~arcuate-line","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Linea alba","B. Rectus abdominis muscle","C. Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","D. Pyramidalis muscle","E. Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Arcuate line (of rectus sheath).</b> The arcuate line is the curved lower edge of the posterior rectus sheath, a third of the way from umbilicus to pubic crest, where inferior epigastric vessels enter.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Rectus abdominis, which lies directly on transversalis fascia below this line</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia and extraperitoneal fat; inferior epigastric vessels pass up in front of it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Look at the back of rectus abdominis from inside (or lift the muscle); follow the posterior sheath downward until it stops in a curved edge, where the inferior epigastric artery passes up into the sheath.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s233.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 233"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9091,0.389,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49286}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Superior epigastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_182_140.jpg","cap":"Arteries of the thoracic wall","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.29","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7167,0.7694,0.9217,0.8183]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s012.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 12","ov":[[0.1854,0.8976,0.4739,0.9866]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s227.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 227","ov":[[0.214,0.9068,0.5024,0.9884]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s051~superior-epigastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inferior epigastric artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Superior epigastric artery","E. Ilioinguinal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior epigastric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Superior epigastric artery.</b> The superior epigastric artery is the medial terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery, entering the rectus sheath below the 6th intercostal space and running down behind rectus abdominis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the inferior epigastric artery near the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Rectus abdominis muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior rectus sheath</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the internal thoracic artery and follow it below the 6th intercostal space; the medial branch that continues straight down behind rectus abdominis inside its sheath is the superior epigastric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s051.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 51"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0989,0.9398,0.3833,0.9927,"",1]],"ar":0.76214}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Inferior epigastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_376_1869.jpg","cap":"Pelvic fascial ligaments","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.14","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7076,0.1607,0.8425,0.2073]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s225~inferior-epigastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric artery","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Ilioinguinal nerve","D. Inferior epigastric artery","E. Inguinal ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Inferior epigastric artery.</b> The inferior epigastric artery leaves the external iliac above the inguinal ligament, climbs medial to the deep inguinal ring, and enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>External iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lower rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the superior epigastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then rectus abdominis once inside the rectus sheath</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), then posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> From inside the lower abdominal wall, find the deep inguinal ring with the ductus deferens or round ligament entering it; the vessel climbing along its medial edge toward rectus is the inferior epigastric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s225.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 225"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9088,0.4861,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.49071}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Inguinal ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_231_1574.jpg","cap":"Formations of inguinal region","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.14","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0491,0.2897,0.1889,0.3477]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s213~inguinal-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatogastric ligament","B. Inferior epigastric artery","C. Superior epigastric artery","D. Inguinal ligament","E. Ilioinguinal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inguinal ligament","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Inguinal ligament.</b> The inguinal ligament is the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, running from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle over the femoral vessels.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Rolled-under lower free edge of the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) to the pubic tubercle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat (Camper's and Scarpa's layers) at the groin crease; the spermatic cord rests on its upper grooved surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliopsoas, femoral nerve and femoral vessels, passing beneath it into the thigh</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Locate the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle; the firm band connecting them along the groin is the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s213.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 213"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9006,0.4133,0.9848,"",1]],"ar":0.44857}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Ilioinguinal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg","cap":"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0272,0.3236,0.1753,0.3616]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s219.jpg","cap":"Ilioinguinal nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 219","ov":[[0.1847,0.9047,0.386,0.988]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s475.jpg","cap":"Ilioinguinal nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 475","ov":[[0.214,0.9059,0.4153,0.9883]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s046~ilioinguinal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Obturator nerve","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Inferior epigastric artery","E. Ilioinguinal nerve"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ilioinguinal nerve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ilioinguinal nerve.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) runs across quadratus lumborum just below the iliohypogastric nerve, then through the inguinal canal beside the spermatic cord, exiting the superficial inguinal ring.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of medial thigh, penile root or mons, anterior scrotum or labium majus; lower internal oblique</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney at first; in the inguinal canal, the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; then the spermatic cord (or round ligament) in the inguinal canal</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the external oblique aponeurosis over the inguinal canal; the thin white nerve lying on the front of the spermatic cord and emerging with it at the superficial ring is the ilioinguinal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s046.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 46"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0989,0.9222,0.2961,0.9891,"",1]],"ar":0.58}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Superior epigastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_182_140.jpg","cap":"Arteries of the thoracic wall","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.29","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7167,0.7694,0.9217,0.8183]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s051.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 51","ov":[[0.0969,0.9378,0.3853,0.9947]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s227.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 227","ov":[[0.214,0.9068,0.5024,0.9884]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s012~superior-epigastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ilioinguinal nerve","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Inguinal ligament","E. Right hepatic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior epigastric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Superior epigastric artery.</b> The superior epigastric artery is the medial terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery, entering the rectus sheath below the 6th intercostal space and running down behind rectus abdominis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the inferior epigastric artery near the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Rectus abdominis muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior rectus sheath</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the internal thoracic artery and follow it below the 6th intercostal space; the medial branch that continues straight down behind rectus abdominis inside its sheath is the superior epigastric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s012.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 12"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1874,0.8996,0.4719,0.9846,"",1]],"ar":0.44429,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s051~superior-epigastric-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Superior epigastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_182_140.jpg","cap":"Arteries of the thoracic wall","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.29","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7167,0.7694,0.9217,0.8183]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s051.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 51","ov":[[0.0969,0.9378,0.3853,0.9947]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s012.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 12","ov":[[0.1854,0.8976,0.4739,0.9866]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s227~superior-epigastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inguinal ligament","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Ilioinguinal nerve","D. Inferior epigastric artery","E. Proper hepatic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior epigastric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Superior epigastric artery.</b> The superior epigastric artery is the medial terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery, entering the rectus sheath below the 6th intercostal space and running down behind rectus abdominis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the inferior epigastric artery near the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Rectus abdominis muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior rectus sheath</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the internal thoracic artery and follow it below the 6th intercostal space; the medial branch that continues straight down behind rectus abdominis inside its sheath is the superior epigastric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s227.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 227"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9088,0.5004,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.49071,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s051~superior-epigastric-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Ilioinguinal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg","cap":"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0272,0.3236,0.1753,0.3616]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s046.jpg","cap":"Ilioinguinal nerve on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 46","ov":[[0.0969,0.9202,0.2981,0.9911]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s475.jpg","cap":"Ilioinguinal nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 475","ov":[[0.214,0.9059,0.4153,0.9883]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s219~ilioinguinal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inguinal ligament","B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Superior epigastric artery","E. Ilioinguinal nerve"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ilioinguinal nerve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ilioinguinal nerve.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) runs across quadratus lumborum just below the iliohypogastric nerve, then through the inguinal canal beside the spermatic cord, exiting the superficial inguinal ring.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of medial thigh, penile root or mons, anterior scrotum or labium majus; lower internal oblique</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney at first; in the inguinal canal, the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; then the spermatic cord (or round ligament) in the inguinal canal</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the external oblique aponeurosis over the inguinal canal; the thin white nerve lying on the front of the spermatic cord and emerging with it at the superficial ring is the ilioinguinal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s219.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 219"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1867,0.9067,0.384,0.986,"",1]],"ar":0.47929,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s046~ilioinguinal-nerve"}]},{"l":"VB-A1","u":27,"label":"Ilioinguinal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg","cap":"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0272,0.3236,0.1753,0.3616]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s046.jpg","cap":"Ilioinguinal nerve on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 46","ov":[[0.0969,0.9202,0.2981,0.9911]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s219.jpg","cap":"Ilioinguinal nerve on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 219","ov":[[0.1847,0.9047,0.386,0.988]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s475~ilioinguinal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Ilioinguinal nerve","D. Inferior epigastric artery","E. Inguinal ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ilioinguinal nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ilioinguinal nerve.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) runs across quadratus lumborum just below the iliohypogastric nerve, then through the inguinal canal beside the spermatic cord, exiting the superficial inguinal ring.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of medial thigh, penile root or mons, anterior scrotum or labium majus; lower internal oblique</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney at first; in the inguinal canal, the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; then the spermatic cord (or round ligament) in the inguinal canal</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the external oblique aponeurosis over the inguinal canal; the thin white nerve lying on the front of the spermatic cord and emerging with it at the superficial ring is the ilioinguinal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s475.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 475"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9079,0.4133,0.9863,"",1]],"ar":0.48571,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s046~ilioinguinal-nerve"}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":28,"label":"Stomach","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_267_1632.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.42","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8783,0.0523,0.9864,0.0938]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s263~stomach","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Uncinate process of pancreas","B. Short gastric arteries","C. Stomach","D. Splenic artery","E. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Stomach.</b> The stomach is the J-shaped sac in the left upper abdomen, running from the esophagus at the cardia to the duodenum at the pylorus, in front of the pancreas.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lobe of liver, diaphragm and anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Stomach bed: lesser sac, pancreas, left kidney, left suprarenal gland, spleen, transverse mesocolon</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the esophagus passing through the esophageal hiatus, then follow it into the cardia and along the curvatures to the firm pyloric ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s263.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 263"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9091,0.389,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49286}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":29,"label":"Parietal peritoneum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_249_1601.jpg","cap":"Principal formations of peritoneum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.25","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0167,0.6887,0.3092,0.7375]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s237~parietal-peritoneum","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Lesser omentum","B. Right gastric artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Greater omentum","E. Parietal peritoneum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal peritoneum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Parietal peritoneum.</b> The parietal peritoneum is the shiny membrane lining the inside of the abdominal walls and the underside of the diaphragm, separated from the transversalis fascia by extraperitoneal fat.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Extraperitoneal fat, then transversalis fascia and transversus abdominis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Peritoneal cavity, with the greater omentum and intestines against its inner surface</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the anterior abdominal wall and look at its inner surface; the shiny lining carrying the five umbilical folds below the umbilicus is parietal peritoneum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s237.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 237"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3443,0.2523,0.4296,0.329,"",0],[0.216,0.9057,0.439,0.9858,"",1]],"ar":0.47429}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":29,"label":"Greater omentum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_334_343.jpg","cap":"Greater omentum","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.402,0.9573,0.7028,0.9931]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s249~greater-omentum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Parietal peritoneum","B. Lesser omentum","C. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Greater omentum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater omentum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Greater omentum.</b> The greater omentum is the fatty apron hanging from the greater curvature of the stomach, draping in front of the small intestine and attaching to the transverse colon.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Dorsal mesogastrium (a four-layered peritoneal fold)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Greater curvature of stomach to the transverse colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse colon and coils of small intestine</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the abdomen; the fatty sheet covering the intestines is the greater omentum; lift it upward and confirm it stays attached along the greater curvature and the transverse colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s249.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 249"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9215,0.4183,0.989,"",1]],"ar":0.575}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":29,"label":"Lesser omentum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_335_344.jpg","cap":"Lesser omentum","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.57","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6893,0.0551,0.8456,0.0946]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s265~lesser-omentum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Parietal peritoneum","B. Right hepatic artery","C. Lesser omentum","D. Greater omentum","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lesser omentum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Lesser omentum.</b> The lesser omentum is the double peritoneal sheet from the stomach's lesser curvature and first part of the duodenum to the liver, with a thick free right edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesogastrium (ventral mesentery)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Lesser curvature and first part of duodenum to porta hepatis and fissure for ligamentum venosum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lobe of the liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac (omental bursa), with the caudate lobe and pancreas behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the inferior border of the liver and pull the stomach down; the sheet between the lesser curvature and the liver, ending on the right in a thick free edge, is the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s265.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 265"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9093,0.4111,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49357}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":29,"label":"Hepatogastric ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg","cap":"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7856,0.2286,0.8963,0.3024]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st18~hepatogastric-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left triangular ligament","B. Greater omentum","C. Parietal peritoneum","D. Hepatogastric ligament","E. Falciform ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatogastric ligament","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepatogastric ligament.</b> The hepatogastric ligament is the thin left part of the lesser omentum, spanning from the stomach's lesser curvature to the fissure for the ligamentum venosum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesogastrium; it is the thin left part of the lesser omentum</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of stomach to the fissure for ligamentum venosum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lobe of the liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac (omental bursa), with the caudate lobe and celiac trunk behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the liver and pull the stomach down; the thin membrane spanning from the lesser curvature up to the liver is the hepatogastric ligament, and the thick right free edge beyond it is the hepatoduodenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st18.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 18"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1817,0.944,0.4548,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.86909}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Head of pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_271_1643.jpg","cap":"Relationships of duodenum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.45","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0061,0.5652,0.1701,0.6074]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s409~head-of-pancreas","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Tail of pancreas","B. Uncinate process of pancreas","C. Head of pancreas","D. Body of pancreas","E. Neck of pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Head of pancreas","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Head of pancreas.</b> The head of the pancreas sits in the duodenal C-curve, right of the superior mesenteric vessels, in front of the inferior vena cava, with the bile duct behind.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pylorus and transverse colon, with the transverse mesocolon root crossing it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), right renal vessels and left renal vein; bile duct grooves its back</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the descending duodenum and look medial to it: the gland tissue in the concavity of the C is the head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s409.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 409"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.6431,0.7567,0.9719,0.8306,"",0],[0.2621,0.8351,0.5908,0.909,"",0],[0.6431,0.8351,0.9719,0.909,"",0],[0.2621,0.9136,0.5908,0.9874,"",0],[0,0.6878,0.1178,0.747,"",0],[0.2621,0.7567,0.5908,0.8306,"",1]],"ar":0.51929}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Uncinate process of pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_295_1691.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and venous drainage of pancreas","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.60","tb":1,"ov":[[0.586,0.7354,0.8672,0.7873]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s409~uncinate-process-of-pancreas","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Body of pancreas","B. Neck of pancreas","C. Tail of pancreas","D. Head of pancreas","E. Uncinate process of pancreas"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Uncinate process of pancreas","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Uncinate process of pancreas.</b> The uncinate process hooks left from the lower pancreatic head, behind the superior mesenteric vein and artery and in front of the aorta and inferior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Superior mesenteric vein and artery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMV as it passes behind the pancreas, then look behind and below it for the tongue of gland coming from the head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s409.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 409"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2621,0.7567,0.5908,0.8306,"",0],[0.6431,0.7567,0.9719,0.8306,"",0],[0.2621,0.8351,0.5908,0.909,"",0],[0.6431,0.8351,0.9719,0.909,"",0],[0,0.6878,0.1178,0.747,"",0],[0.2621,0.9136,0.5908,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.51929}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Neck of pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_269_1638.jpg","cap":"Duodenum, pancreas, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.44","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0105,0.6576,0.1201,0.773]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s409~neck-of-pancreas","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Body of pancreas","B. Head of pancreas","C. Uncinate process of pancreas","D. Tail of pancreas","E. Neck of pancreas"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Neck of pancreas","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Neck of pancreas.</b> The pancreatic neck is the short narrow part between head and body, lying directly in front of the superior mesenteric vein and the start of the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pylorus of the stomach, with the lesser sac between</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>SMV (superior mesenteric vein) joining the splenic vein to form the portal vein</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find where the SMV and splenic vein join; the gland lying directly in front of that junction is the neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s409.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 409"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2621,0.7567,0.5908,0.8306,"",0],[0.2621,0.8351,0.5908,0.909,"",0],[0.6431,0.8351,0.9719,0.909,"",0],[0.2621,0.9136,0.5908,0.9874,"",0],[0,0.6878,0.1178,0.747,"",0],[0.6431,0.7567,0.9719,0.8306,"",1]],"ar":0.51929}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Body of pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_264_1628.jpg","cap":"Omental bursa and stomach bed","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.39","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1635,0.3841,0.2558,0.4323]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s409~body-of-pancreas","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Uncinate process of pancreas","B. Tail of pancreas","C. Neck of pancreas","D. Head of pancreas","E. Body of pancreas"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Body of pancreas","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Body of pancreas.</b> The body of the pancreas runs left from the neck across the aorta and left kidney, with the splenic vein behind it and splenic artery along its top.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Aorta, SMA (superior mesenteric artery), left suprarenal, left kidney and renal vessels; splenic vein behind</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the lesser sac and look at its floor behind the stomach: the gland lying over the aorta and left kidney is the body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s409.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 409"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2621,0.7567,0.5908,0.8306,"",0],[0.6431,0.7567,0.9719,0.8306,"",0],[0.6431,0.8351,0.9719,0.909,"",0],[0.2621,0.9136,0.5908,0.9874,"",0],[0,0.6878,0.1178,0.747,"",0],[0.2621,0.8351,0.5908,0.909,"",1]],"ar":0.51929}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Tail of pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_264_1628.jpg","cap":"Omental bursa and stomach bed","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.39","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8103,0.3534,0.9026,0.4018]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s409~tail-of-pancreas","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Body of pancreas","B. Neck of pancreas","C. Tail of pancreas","D. Uncinate process of pancreas","E. Head of pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tail of pancreas","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Tail of pancreas.</b> The tail is the narrow left end of the pancreas, lying in front of the left kidney and running with the splenic vessels in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach (across the lesser sac) and the left colic flexure</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left kidney, then the splenorenal ligament with the splenic vessels to the splenic hilum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic hilum and follow the splenic vessels medially; the gland lying among them is the tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s409.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 409"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2621,0.7567,0.5908,0.8306,"",0],[0.6431,0.7567,0.9719,0.8306,"",0],[0.2621,0.8351,0.5908,0.909,"",0],[0.2621,0.9136,0.5908,0.9874,"",0],[0,0.6878,0.1178,0.747,"",0],[0.6431,0.8351,0.9719,0.909,"",1]],"ar":0.51929}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Main pancreatic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_362_390.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic duct system","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.100","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8182,0.2969,0.9942,0.3514]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s411~main-pancreatic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Head of pancreas","B. Main pancreatic duct","C. Uncinate process of pancreas","D. Body of pancreas","E. Neck of pancreas"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Main pancreatic duct","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Main pancreatic duct.</b> The main pancreatic duct runs through the pancreas from tail to head, then turns down to join the bile duct, opening at the major duodenal papilla.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Small lobular ducts joining along the gland from tail to head</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla; opens at the major duodenal papilla</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pancreatic tissue, which must be dissected away from the front to expose it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Scrape away pancreatic tissue along the middle of the body and follow the white duct to the right into the head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s411.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 411"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.8912,0.4519,0.9829,"",1]],"ar":0.40857}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":30,"label":"Spleen","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_272_1644.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of duodenum, pancreas, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.46","tb":1,"ov":[[0.9069,0.2237,0.9915,0.2657]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s261~spleen","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Body of pancreas","B. Neck of pancreas","C. Main pancreatic duct","D. Head of pancreas","E. Spleen"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Spleen.</b> The spleen sits in the left upper abdomen against the diaphragm at ribs 9–11, behind and left of the stomach, lateral to the left kidney and above the left colic flexure.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach anteriorly (gastric impression); left colic flexure below</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm against ribs 9–11 posterolaterally; left kidney medially</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the fundus of the stomach and follow the gastrosplenic ligament laterally to the solid organ tucked under the left dome of the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s261.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 261"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9059,0.389,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.475}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":31,"label":"Celiac trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg","cap":"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0069,0.1377,0.1233,0.1804]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s299~celiac-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Celiac lymph nodes","B. Left gastric vein","C. Celiac trunk","D. Left gastric artery","E. Short gastric arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Celiac trunk.</b> The celiac trunk is the first unpaired front branch of the abdominal aorta at T12, just above the pancreas, splitting into left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta, anterior surface at T12, just below the aortic hiatus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries (foregut supply)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac and the lesser omentum; body of pancreas just below</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the abdominal aorta, just below the median arcuate ligament</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal aorta emerging through the aortic hiatus, then follow its anterior surface down to the first large anterior branch above the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s299.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 299"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9041,0.389,0.9855,"",1]],"ar":0.46571}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":31,"label":"Celiac lymph nodes","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_306_1718.jpg","cap":"Arteries supplying the biliary duct and lymphatic drainage of gallbladder and bile duct","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.71","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7682,0.5729,0.8944,0.6841]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s355~celiac-lymph-nodes","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatic lymph nodes","B. Left gastric artery","C. Celiac trunk","D. Celiac lymph nodes","E. Left gastric vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac lymph nodes","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Celiac lymph nodes.</b> The celiac lymph nodes cluster around the celiac trunk's root on the front of the aorta, collecting from gastric, hepatic, splenic and pancreatic nodes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Gastric, hepatic, splenic and pancreatic lymph nodes</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Intestinal lymph trunk, then the cisterna chyli</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the abdominal aorta, around the celiac trunk root</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk leaving the aorta; the small soft nodes clustered around its root are celiac nodes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s355.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 355"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9099,0.4397,0.9867,"",1]],"ar":0.49714}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":31,"label":"Left gastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_260_1619.jpg","cap":"Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.35","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0836,0.9464,0.2303,0.9874]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s313~left-gastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastric artery","B. Short gastric arteries","C. Celiac lymph nodes","D. Left gastric vein","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left gastric artery.</b> The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, climbing to the esophagogastric junction and then running down the lesser curvature inside the lesser omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its smallest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of the stomach and the abdominal esophagus; joins the right gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left crus of the diaphragm and the left suprarenal area, as it rises in the posterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then take the branch that heads up to the esophagogastric junction before curving down the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s313.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 313"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2171,0.9357,0.4231,0.9913,"",1]],"ar":0.73009}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":31,"label":"Left gastric vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg","cap":"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41","tb":1,"ov":[[0.274,0.2111,0.4098,0.2616]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s341~left-gastric-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastric vein","B. Celiac lymph nodes","C. Left gastric vein","D. Celiac trunk","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left gastric vein.</b> The left gastric vein runs with the left gastric artery along the upper lesser curvature, receives veins from the lower esophagus, and drains into the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of stomach and lower esophagus (esophageal veins)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein; its esophageal tributaries anastomose with azygos veins (portocaval site)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lesser omentum along the lesser curvature, then lesser sac peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior wall of the lesser sac, beside the left gastric artery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left gastric artery rising from the celiac trunk to the lesser curvature, then the vein beside it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s341.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 341"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9146,0.4276,0.9876,"",1]],"ar":0.52571}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":32,"label":"Splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_296_1694.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3944,0.2704,0.5244,0.3312]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s327~splenic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Short gastric arteries","B. Right gastric artery","C. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Splenic vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Splenic artery.</b> The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its largest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Spleen; also pancreatic, short gastric, left gastro-omental and posterior gastric branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then follow its left branch along the top of the pancreas toward the spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s327.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 327"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9089,0.389,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49143}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":32,"label":"Splenic vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg","cap":"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5875,0.5828,0.7033,0.6341]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s397~splenic-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","B. Splenic vein","C. Short gastric arteries","D. Splenic artery","E. Left gastric vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic vein","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Splenic vein.</b> The splenic vein runs right from the splenic hilum along the back of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Veins at the splenic hilum; receives short gastric, pancreatic and IMV (inferior mesenteric vein)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Body and tail of pancreas, which it grooves from behind</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left kidney, then the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and aorta as it runs right</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the body of the pancreas and look on its back surface for the straight, wide vein heading right toward the pancreatic neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s397.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 397"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9013,0.389,0.9849,"",1]],"ar":0.45214}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":32,"label":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_264_1628.jpg","cap":"Omental bursa and stomach bed","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.39","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8292,0.8593,0.995,0.8915]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s331.jpg","cap":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 331","ov":[[0.214,0.9098,0.5074,0.9891]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st20~left-gastro-omental-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","B. Splenic artery","C. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Short gastric arteries","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery, near the splenic hilum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; anastomoses with the right gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature; lesser sac behind</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic artery at the hilum of the spleen, then follow the branch that turns onto the greater curvature heading toward the pylorus.","ex":"","fl":["The artery on the greater curvature next to the spleen is the left gastro-omental artery."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st20.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 20"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1814,0.9461,0.4843,0.9937,"",1]],"ar":0.90487}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":32,"label":"Short gastric arteries","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_265_1629.jpg","cap":"Arteries of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.40","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7617,0.1768,0.9525,0.2312]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s329~short-gastric-arteries","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Splenic vein","B. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","C. Short gastric arteries","D. Splenic artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric arteries","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Short gastric arteries.</b> The short gastric arteries are four or five small branches of the splenic artery near the splenic hilum that run in the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus of the stomach.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery near the hilum, or its terminal splenic branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Fundus of the stomach</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front peritoneal layer of the gastrosplenic ligament</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Splenic recess of the lesser sac, as they run in the gastrosplenic ligament</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic hilum, then look in the gastrosplenic ligament for small vessels running to the fundus and upper greater curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s329.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 329"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9202,0.4554,0.9888,"",1]],"ar":0.565}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":32,"label":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_264_1628.jpg","cap":"Omental bursa and stomach bed","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.39","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8292,0.8593,0.995,0.8915]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st20.jpg","cap":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 20","ov":[[0.1794,0.9441,0.4863,0.9957]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s331~left-gastro-omental-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Short gastric arteries","B. Splenic artery","C. Splenic vein","D. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","E. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery, near the splenic hilum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; anastomoses with the right gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature; lesser sac behind</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic artery at the hilum of the spleen, then follow the branch that turns onto the greater curvature heading toward the pylorus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s331.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 331"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9118,0.5054,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.50857,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st20~left-gastro-omental-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Common hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg","cap":"Distribution of the common hepatic artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6895,0.2981,0.9198,0.3411]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s301~common-hepatic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Common hepatic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Common hepatic artery.</b> The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Divides into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries above the first part of duodenum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, take its right-going branch, and stop at the point where it splits near the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s301.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 301"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9061,0.479,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.47643}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_337_347.jpg","cap":"Abdominal esophagus","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.8609,0.9914,0.9026]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s055.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 55","ov":[[0.0969,0.9401,0.3539,0.9952]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s317.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 317","ov":[[0.214,0.9001,0.471,0.9871]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s053~gastroduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Common hepatic artery","E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the common hepatic artery, then follow the branch that dives down behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s053.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 53"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0989,0.9146,0.3519,0.9876,"",1]],"ar":0.52571}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_375_410.jpg","cap":"Initial branching and relationships of the superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.115","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0843,0.3153,0.3349,0.3938]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s323~posterior-superior-pancreaticoduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery.</b> The posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery branches from the gastroduodenal artery behind the duodenum and descends on the back of the pancreatic head beside the bile duct.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery, behind the first part of the duodenum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Back of pancreatic head, duodenum and bile duct; joins the posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Head of pancreas, which covers it from the front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), with the bile duct beside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn the duodenum and pancreatic head forward to see their posterior surface, find the bile duct, and look for the artery running beside it from the gastroduodenal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s323.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 323"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9129,0.7361,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.515}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_375_410.jpg","cap":"Initial branching and relationships of the superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.115","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0063,0.5547,0.2569,0.6343]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s321~anterior-superior-pancreaticoduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery.</b> The anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, a branch of the gastroduodenal artery, runs down the front of the pancreatic head beside the duodenum to meet the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch, with the right gastro-omental)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Front of pancreatic head and duodenum; joins the anterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum, with the transverse colon and mesocolon in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the pancreatic head, near the duodenum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the gastroduodenal artery at the lower border of the first part of the duodenum, then follow the branch running down over the front of the pancreatic head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s321.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 321"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9137,0.7254,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.52}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply to the pancreas","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0448,0.4889,0.2714,0.5302]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s319~right-gastro-omental-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","C. Common hepatic artery","D. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; joins the left gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Head of pancreas at its start, then the greater omentum below the greater curvature</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the gastroduodenal artery emerging below the first part of the duodenum, then follow its branch to the left along the greater curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s319.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 319"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9118,0.5204,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.50857}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_337_347.jpg","cap":"Abdominal esophagus","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.8609,0.9914,0.9026]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s053.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 53","ov":[[0.0969,0.9126,0.3539,0.9896]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s317.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 317","ov":[[0.214,0.9001,0.471,0.9871]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s055~gastroduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Common hepatic artery","E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the common hepatic artery, then follow the branch that dives down behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s055.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 55"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.0989,0.9421,0.3519,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.795,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s053~gastroduodenal-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":33,"label":"Gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_337_347.jpg","cap":"Abdominal esophagus","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.8609,0.9914,0.9026]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s053.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 53","ov":[[0.0969,0.9126,0.3539,0.9896]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s055.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 55","ov":[[0.0969,0.9401,0.3539,0.9952]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s317~gastroduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Gastroduodenal artery","E. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the common hepatic artery, then follow the branch that dives down behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s317.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 317"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9021,0.469,0.9851,"",1]],"ar":0.45571,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s053~gastroduodenal-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Proper hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg","cap":"Distribution of the common hepatic artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6906,0.1586,0.901,0.2015]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s303~proper-hepatic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastric artery","B. Cystic artery","C. Left hepatic artery","D. Right hepatic artery","E. Proper hepatic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Proper hepatic artery.</b> The proper hepatic artery continues the common hepatic beyond the gastroduodenal branch, rising in the hepatoduodenal ligament left of the bile duct and in front of the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery, after the gastroduodenal artery leaves</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left hepatic arteries; usually gives the right gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein behind it; bile duct on its right</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) and sort its three structures: bile duct on the right, artery on the left, portal vein behind.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s303.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 303"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9061,0.4554,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.47643}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Right gastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply to the pancreas","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2165,0.1521,0.3796,0.1934]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s311.jpg","cap":"Right gastric artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 311","ov":[[0.2174,0.9301,0.4455,0.9926]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q26~right-gastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Cystic artery","B. Proper hepatic artery","C. Left gastric artery","D. Right hepatic artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right gastric artery.</b> The right gastric artery, a branch of the proper hepatic artery, runs left along the lesser curvature from the pylorus in the lesser omentum to join the left gastric artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Pyloric part and lesser curvature of stomach; joins the left gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery in the hepatoduodenal ligament, then look for the small branch heading down to the pylorus and along the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q26.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 26"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2017,0.935,0.4219,0.9917,"",1]],"ar":0.70143}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Right gastric vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg","cap":"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41","tb":1,"ov":[[0.205,0.2928,0.352,0.3429]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s339~right-gastric-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left gastric vein","B. Right gastric vein","C. Right hepatic artery","D. Right gastric artery","E. Proper hepatic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric vein","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right gastric vein.</b> The right gastric vein runs with the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature near the pylorus and drains directly into the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Pyloric part and lesser curvature of stomach; receives the prepyloric vein</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein, directly</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pylorus and the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature, then the accompanying vein running to the portal vein in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s339.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 339"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9146,0.4426,0.9876,"",1]],"ar":0.52571}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Left hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg","cap":"Distribution of the common hepatic artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6811,0.1161,0.8625,0.1591]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s307~left-hepatic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right hepatic artery","B. Right gastric artery","C. Left hepatic artery","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Cystic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left hepatic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left hepatic artery.</b> The left hepatic artery is the left end branch of the proper hepatic artery, running to the left side of the porta hepatis to supply the left liver.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery (left terminal branch); sometimes from the left gastric artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left half of the liver, including the quadrate lobe and part of the caudate lobe</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left hepatic duct, the most anterior structure at the porta hepatis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left branch of the portal vein, at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery and follow its left branch toward the left side of the porta hepatis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s307.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 307"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2444,0.9321,0.4805,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.77401}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Right hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg","cap":"Distribution of the common hepatic artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3903,0.0106,0.5854,0.0533]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s305~right-hepatic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastric artery","B. Left hepatic artery","C. Right hepatic artery","D. Cystic artery","E. Proper hepatic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hepatic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right hepatic artery.</b> The right hepatic artery is the right terminal branch of the proper hepatic artery, passing behind the common hepatic duct and giving off the cystic artery in the cystohepatic triangle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery (right terminal branch); sometimes from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right half of the liver; gives the cystic artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Common hepatic duct, which usually crosses in front of it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right branch of the portal vein, behind it at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery at the top of the hepatoduodenal ligament and follow its right branch behind the common hepatic duct toward the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s305.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 305"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2444,0.9308,0.4984,0.9903,"",1]],"ar":0.75895}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Cystic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1148,0.5698,0.2264,0.6182]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s357.jpg","cap":"Cystic artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 357","ov":[[0.214,0.9037,0.391,0.9878]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s309~cystic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastric artery","B. Cystic artery","C. Left hepatic artery","D. Right hepatic artery","E. Proper hepatic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cystic artery.</b> The cystic artery usually arises from the right hepatic artery in the cystohepatic triangle, between cystic duct, common hepatic duct and liver, and runs to the gallbladder.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right hepatic artery, in the cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gallbladder and cystic duct</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum covering the cystohepatic triangle (cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck of the gallbladder, reached above the cystic duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cystic duct leaving the gallbladder neck, then look just above it in the triangle under the liver for the small artery reaching the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s309.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 309"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9059,0.389,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.475}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Right gastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply to the pancreas","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2165,0.1521,0.3796,0.1934]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q26.jpg","cap":"Right gastric artery on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 26","ov":[[0.1997,0.933,0.4239,0.9937]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s311~right-gastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right hepatic artery","B. Left gastric artery","C. Right gastric artery","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Cystic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right gastric artery.</b> The right gastric artery, a branch of the proper hepatic artery, runs left along the lesser curvature from the pylorus in the lesser omentum to join the left gastric artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Pyloric part and lesser curvature of stomach; joins the left gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery in the hepatoduodenal ligament, then look for the small branch heading down to the pylorus and along the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s311.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 311"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2194,0.9321,0.4435,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.69602,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q26~right-gastric-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A2","u":34,"label":"Cystic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1148,0.5698,0.2264,0.6182]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s309.jpg","cap":"Cystic artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 309","ov":[[0.214,0.9039,0.391,0.9879]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s357~cystic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left hepatic artery","B. Proper hepatic artery","C. Right gastric artery","D. Cystic artery","E. Right hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Cystic artery.</b> The cystic artery usually arises from the right hepatic artery in the cystohepatic triangle, between cystic duct, common hepatic duct and liver, and runs to the gallbladder.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right hepatic artery, in the cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gallbladder and cystic duct</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum covering the cystohepatic triangle (cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck of the gallbladder, reached above the cystic duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cystic duct leaving the gallbladder neck, then look just above it in the triangle under the liver for the small artery reaching the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s357.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 357"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9057,0.389,0.9858,"",1]],"ar":0.47429,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s309~cystic-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":35,"label":"Right lobe of liver","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1699.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0325,0.0825,0.125,0.154]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s243~right-lobe-of-liver","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right lobe of liver","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Left branch of portal vein","D. Left lobe of liver","E. Coronary ligament (of liver)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right lobe of liver","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right lobe of liver.</b> The right lobe is the liver lying right of the falciform ligament and, underneath, right of the gallbladder and inferior vena cava, above the right kidney and colic flexure.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right dome of the diaphragm and the lower right ribs</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right kidney, right suprarenal gland, right colic flexure and duodenum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament on the front of the liver, then the gallbladder and IVC on the underside; liver to the right of these is the right lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s243.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 243"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.8874,0.4433,0.9822,"",1]],"ar":0.39429}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":35,"label":"Left lobe of liver","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1699.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8061,0.3034,0.9776,0.3755]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s245~left-lobe-of-liver","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Fundus of gallbladder","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Left lobe of liver","D. Body of gallbladder","E. Right lobe of liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left lobe of liver","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left lobe of liver.</b> The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall below the xiphoid</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Stomach (anterior surface) and abdominal esophagus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament and the round ligament in its free edge, then follow them to the fissure on the underside; liver to the left is the left lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s245.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 245"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.8878,0.404,0.9822,"",1]],"ar":0.39571}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":35,"label":"Quadrate lobe (of liver)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_358_385.jpg","cap":"Posterior view of the bare area of the liver and associated ligaments","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.96","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3693,0.8392,0.5033,0.8895]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s345~quadrate-lobe","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Quadrate lobe (of liver)","B. Coronary ligament (of liver)","C. Hepatic portal vein","D. Caudate lobe (of liver)","E. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Quadrate lobe (of liver)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Quadrate lobe (of liver).</b> The quadrate lobe is the square area under the liver in front of the porta hepatis, between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres fissure.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Pylorus and first part of the duodenum, below it; porta hepatis behind it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn the liver up and find the gallbladder and the round ligament; the square of liver between them, in front of the porta hepatis, is the quadrate lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s345.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 345"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9029,0.459,0.9853,"",1]],"ar":0.46}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":35,"label":"Caudate lobe (of liver)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1703.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1278,0.3746,0.3374,0.4741]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s343~caudate-lobe","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left triangular ligament","B. Caudate lobe (of liver)","C. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","D. Quadrate lobe (of liver)","E. Coronary ligament (of liver)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Caudate lobe (of liver)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Caudate lobe (of liver).</b> The caudate lobe is the small lobe on the back of the liver, just in front of the inferior vena cava, beside the fissure for the ligamentum venosum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Superior recess of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) on its right and behind, and the right crus of the diaphragm</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC on the back of the liver and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum; the lobe between them, above the porta hepatis, is the caudate lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s343.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 343"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9029,0.4497,0.9853,"",1]],"ar":0.46}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":36,"label":"Ligamentum venosum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg","cap":"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0064,0.0895,0.1719,0.1633]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s347~ligamentum-venosum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","B. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","C. Fundus of gallbladder","D. Fissure for ligamentum teres","E. Ligamentum venosum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ligamentum venosum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ligamentum venosum.</b> The ligamentum venosum lies in a fissure on the back of the liver between the caudate and left lobes, joining the left portal branch to the inferior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ductus venosus, the fetal shunt from umbilical vein to IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Left branch of the portal vein to the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lesser omentum, which attaches along it in the fissure</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Caudate lobe</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the left lobe and trace the lesser omentum up to the liver; follow its attachment into the deep fissure beside the caudate lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s347.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 347"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9117,0.4661,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.50786}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":37,"label":"Falciform ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_256_1611.jpg","cap":"Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0157,0.1555,0.1615,0.2354]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s239.jpg","cap":"Falciform ligament on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 239","ov":[[0.214,0.9114,0.431,0.9894]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q29~falciform-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Coronary ligament (of liver)","C. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","D. Gallbladder","E. Left triangular ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Falciform ligament.</b> The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery (ventral mesogastrium)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, above the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior and superior surface of the liver</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the upper abdomen in the midline; follow the fold from the umbilicus up onto the anterior surface of the liver: that fold is the falciform ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q29.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 29"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2124,0.9592,0.4254,0.9966,"",1]],"ar":1.16286}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":37,"label":"Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg","cap":"Parts of greater and lesser omenta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0047,0.108,0.1391,0.1494]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s271~round-ligament-of-liver","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Coronary ligament (of liver)","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Caudate lobe (of liver)","D. Left triangular ligament","E. Falciform ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis).</b> The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Obliterated left umbilical vein</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Umbilicus to the left branch of the portal vein, via the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which encloses it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the liver, in the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament between the anterior abdominal wall and the liver, then feel along its free lower edge for the firm cord running to the underside of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s271.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 271"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.8829,0.4761,0.9812,"",1]],"ar":0.37857}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":37,"label":"Coronary ligament (of liver)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1703.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7389,0.0805,0.9508,0.1805]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s273~coronary-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","B. Falciform ligament","C. Caudate lobe (of liver)","D. Coronary ligament (of liver)","E. Left triangular ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Coronary ligament (of liver)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Coronary ligament (of liver).</b> The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery; peritoneum reflecting from the diaphragm onto the liver</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Diaphragm to the superior and posterior surface of the liver, enclosing the bare area</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Superior and posterior surface of the right lobe, around the bare area</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Run a hand over the top of the right lobe toward the back until it is stopped by the peritoneal reflection to the diaphragm; that reflection is the coronary ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s273.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 273"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9007,0.4283,0.9848,"",1]],"ar":0.44929}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":37,"label":"Left triangular ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_358_385.jpg","cap":"Posterior view of the bare area of the liver and associated ligaments","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.96","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0065,0.17,0.2249,0.2174]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s275~left-triangular-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left triangular ligament","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Falciform ligament","D. Coronary ligament (of liver)","E. Ligamentum venosum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left triangular ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left triangular ligament.</b> The left triangular ligament is a thin peritoneal fold tying the left tip of the left liver lobe to the diaphragm, where the coronary ligament's layers meet.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery; two peritoneal layers meeting at the left end of the coronary ligament</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Left tip of the left lobe of the liver to the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Superior surface of the left lobe of the liver</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the upper surface of the left lobe to its left tip and pull it down gently to see the fold to the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s275.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 275"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9015,0.5004,0.985,"",1]],"ar":0.45286}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":37,"label":"Falciform ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_256_1611.jpg","cap":"Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0157,0.1555,0.1615,0.2354]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q29.jpg","cap":"Falciform ligament on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 29","ov":[[0.2104,0.9572,0.4274,0.9986]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s239~falciform-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Bile duct (common bile duct)","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Falciform ligament","D. Left triangular ligament","E. Coronary ligament (of liver)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Falciform ligament.</b> The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery (ventral mesogastrium)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, above the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior and superior surface of the liver</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the upper abdomen in the midline; follow the fold from the umbilicus up onto the anterior surface of the liver: that fold is the falciform ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s239.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 239"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9134,0.429,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.51857,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q29~falciform-ligament"}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":38,"label":"Hepatic portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg","cap":"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41","tb":1,"ov":[[0.128,0.3808,0.2314,0.4321]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s333~portal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Splenic vein","B. Left branch of portal vein","C. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","D. Hepatic portal vein","E. Right branch of portal vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepatic portal vein.</b> The portal vein forms behind the pancreatic neck from the superior mesenteric and splenic veins, then rises in the hepatoduodenal ligament behind the bile duct and hepatic artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>SMV (superior mesenteric vein) and splenic vein, joining behind the pancreatic neck</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Liver: divides at the porta hepatis into right and left branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Bile duct (right) and proper hepatic artery (left), in the hepatoduodenal ligament</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), separated by the omental (epiploic) foramen</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Put a finger through the omental foramen: the large soft vessel in the ligament in front of the finger is the portal vein, and the IVC is behind the finger.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s333.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 333"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9035,0.4304,0.9854,"",1]],"ar":0.46286}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":38,"label":"Right branch of portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_375_410.jpg","cap":"Initial branching and relationships of the superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.115","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2638,0.0996,0.3666,0.1484]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s337~right-branch-of-portal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right branch of portal vein","B. Left branch of portal vein","C. Inferior mesenteric vein","D. Hepatic portal vein","E. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right branch of portal vein","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right branch of portal vein.</b> The right branch of the portal vein is the short, wide branch entering the right liver at the porta hepatis, behind the right hepatic artery and duct.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein, as its short, wide right division</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Sinusoids of the right half of the liver, via segmental branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right hepatic artery and right hepatic duct, in front of it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Caudate lobe (caudate process), behind it at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the portal vein up to the porta hepatis; at its division, the branch passing straight into the right lobe is the right branch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s337.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 337"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9053,0.534,0.9858,"",1]],"ar":0.47214}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":38,"label":"Left branch of portal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8119,0.8858,0.9602,0.9339]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s335~left-branch-of-portal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatic portal vein","B. Left branch of portal vein","C. Splenic vein","D. Right branch of portal vein","E. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left branch of portal vein","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left branch of portal vein.</b> The left branch of the portal vein runs left across the porta hepatis to the ligamentum teres fissure, meeting the round ligament and the ligamentum venosum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein, as its longer, thinner left division</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left half of the liver; joined by the round ligament and ligamentum venosum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left hepatic artery and left hepatic duct; quadrate lobe in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Caudate lobe, behind it at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the round ligament in its fissure on the underside of the liver and follow it back to the vein it joins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s335.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 335"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9053,0.519,0.9858,"",1]],"ar":0.47214}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":38,"label":"Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_302_1711.jpg","cap":"Hepatic segmentation","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1855,0.0307,0.3763,0.0779]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s353.jpg","cap":"Hepatic veins (right, middle, left) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 353","ov":[[0.214,0.9032,0.4096,0.9877]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s351~hepatic-veins","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatic lymph nodes","B. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","C. Left branch of portal vein","D. Hepatic portal vein","E. Right branch of portal vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left).</b> The right, middle and left hepatic veins run between liver segments and empty into the inferior vena cava on the back of the liver, just below the diaphragm.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Central veins of the liver lobules, draining all the liver sinusoids</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), just below the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver tissue; they run between segments, unlike the portal triads within segments</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), which they join at the back of the liver</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC in its groove on the back of the liver just below the diaphragm and look for the large openings entering it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s351.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 351"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9052,0.4226,0.9857,"",1]],"ar":0.47143}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":38,"label":"Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_302_1711.jpg","cap":"Hepatic segmentation","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1855,0.0307,0.3763,0.0779]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s351.jpg","cap":"Hepatic veins (right, middle, left) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 351","ov":[[0.214,0.9032,0.4246,0.9877]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s353~hepatic-veins","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left gastric vein","B. Left branch of portal vein","C. Hepatic portal vein","D. Right branch of portal vein","E. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Hepatic veins (right, middle, left).</b> The right, middle and left hepatic veins run between liver segments and empty into the inferior vena cava on the back of the liver, just below the diaphragm.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Central veins of the liver lobules, draining all the liver sinusoids</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), just below the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver tissue; they run between segments, unlike the portal triads within segments</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), which they join at the back of the liver</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC in its groove on the back of the liver just below the diaphragm and look for the large openings entering it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s353.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 353"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9052,0.4076,0.9857,"",1]],"ar":0.47143,"kind":"repeat","like":"zs_S07-s351~hepatic-veins"}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":39,"label":"Gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg","cap":"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0047,0.5474,0.0993,0.5929]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s247~gallbladder","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatic lymph nodes","B. Right branch of portal vein","C. Ligamentum venosum","D. Gallbladder","E. Hepatic portal vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gallbladder.</b> The gallbladder is the pear-shaped sac on the liver's underside between the right and quadrate lobes, resting on the transverse colon and first part of the duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver (gallbladder fossa between right and quadrate lobes)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>First and second parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior border of the right lobe at the right 9th costal cartilage in the midclavicular line, then lift the liver; the sac on its underside is the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s247.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 247"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9057,0.389,0.9858,"",1]],"ar":0.47429}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":39,"label":"Fundus of gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1159,0.3439,0.1894,0.3919]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s359~fundus-of-gallbladder","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Fundus of gallbladder","B. Falciform ligament","C. Neck of gallbladder","D. Left branch of portal vein","E. Body of gallbladder"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Fundus of gallbladder","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Fundus of gallbladder.</b> The fundus is the rounded blind end of the gallbladder projecting below the liver's inferior border, touching the abdominal wall at the right ninth costal cartilage tip.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal wall at the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage (midclavicular line)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse colon</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior border of the right lobe, then the rounded sac projecting below it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s359.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 359"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.5774,0.8561,0.8226,0.9214,"",0],[0.2799,0.9243,0.5251,0.9896,"",0],[0.2799,0.8561,0.5251,0.9214,"",1]],"ar":0.59714}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":39,"label":"Body of gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1161,0.4803,0.1713,0.5287]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s359~body-of-gallbladder","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Quadrate lobe (of liver)","B. Body of gallbladder","C. Right branch of portal vein","D. Fundus of gallbladder","E. Neck of gallbladder"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Body of gallbladder","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Body of gallbladder.</b> The body is the broad middle part of the gallbladder, between fundus and neck, lying in its liver fossa against the transverse colon and duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver, in the gallbladder fossa</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse colon and the first part of the duodenum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the gallbladder from its rounded tip back toward the porta hepatis; the broad middle part in the liver fossa is the body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s359.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 359"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2799,0.8561,0.5251,0.9214,"",0],[0.2799,0.9243,0.5251,0.9896,"",0],[0.5774,0.8561,0.8226,0.9214,"",1]],"ar":0.59714}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":39,"label":"Neck of gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6434,0.1246,0.8068,0.1726]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s359~neck-of-gallbladder","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Hepatic portal vein","B. Left lobe of liver","C. Neck of gallbladder","D. Fundus of gallbladder","E. Body of gallbladder"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Neck of gallbladder","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Neck of gallbladder.</b> The neck of the gallbladder is its narrow tapered end pointing toward the porta hepatis, making an S-shaped bend before it continues as the cystic duct.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver, near the right end of the porta hepatis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the gallbladder from its fundus toward the porta hepatis; where it narrows and bends just before the cystic duct is the neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s359.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 359"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2799,0.8561,0.5251,0.9214,"",0],[0.5774,0.8561,0.8226,0.9214,"",0],[0.2799,0.9243,0.5251,0.9896,"",1]],"ar":0.59714}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":40,"label":"Cystic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3427,0.7682,0.4429,0.8166]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s361.jpg","cap":"Cystic duct on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 361","ov":[[0.214,0.9183,0.391,0.9908]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st19~cystic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Neck of gallbladder","B. Bile duct (common bile duct)","C. Cystic duct","D. Hepatic lymph nodes","E. Caudate lobe (of liver)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic duct","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cystic duct.</b> The cystic duct runs from the gallbladder neck to join the common hepatic duct, forming the bile duct, with the cystic artery just above it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Neck of the gallbladder</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the common hepatic duct to form the bile duct</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the hepatoduodenal ligament; the cystic artery runs just above it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right free edge of the lesser omentum, forming the lower side of the cystohepatic triangle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the gallbladder and follow the duct leaving it toward the hepatoduodenal ligament until it meets the common hepatic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st19.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 19"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1866,0.9402,0.3714,0.9923,"",1]],"ar":0.83037}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":40,"label":"Bile duct (common bile duct)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_364_394.jpg","cap":"Bile drainage","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.102","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0218,0.4657,0.1029,0.5121]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s297~bile-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatic portal vein","B. Cystic duct","C. Bile duct (common bile duct)","D. Main pancreatic duct","E. Hepatic lymph nodes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile duct (common bile duct)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Bile duct (common bile duct).</b> The bile duct runs in the lesser omentum's free edge, right of the hepatic artery and anterior to the portal vein, then behind the duodenum and pancreatic head.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Common hepatic duct and cystic duct joining</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla; opens at the major duodenal papilla</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Free edge of the lesser omentum, then the first part of duodenum and pancreatic head</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Portal vein in the ligament, then the IVC (inferior vena cava) behind the pancreatic head</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Put a finger in the omental foramen; the free edge in front of it is the hepatoduodenal ligament, and the duct on its right front is the bile duct, which you follow down behind the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s297.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 297"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.8964,0.389,0.984,"",1]],"ar":0.43}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":40,"label":"Hepatic lymph nodes","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_345_1792.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic vessels and lymph nodes of posterior abdominal wall and lymphatic trunks of abdomen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.100","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0132,0.2202,0.1846,0.2991]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s315~hepatic-lymph-nodes","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Hepatic portal vein","B. Hepatic lymph nodes","C. Cystic duct","D. Celiac lymph nodes","E. Bile duct (common bile duct)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic lymph nodes","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hepatic lymph nodes.</b> The hepatic lymph nodes lie along the hepatic arteries and bile duct in the lesser omentum and at the porta hepatis, draining onward to the celiac nodes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Liver, gallbladder and bile ducts (including the cystic node)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Celiac lymph nodes, then the intestinal lymph trunk and cisterna chyli</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Hepatic artery and bile duct, within the free edge of the lesser omentum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the hepatoduodenal ligament; the small nodes along the hepatic artery and bile duct are hepatic nodes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s315.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 315"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9122,0.454,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.51071}]},{"l":"VB-A3","u":40,"label":"Cystic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg","cap":"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3427,0.7682,0.4429,0.8166]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st19.jpg","cap":"Cystic duct on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 19","ov":[[0.1846,0.9382,0.3734,0.9943]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s361~cystic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Caudate lobe (of liver)","B. Bile duct (common bile duct)","C. Hepatic lymph nodes","D. Hepatic portal vein","E. Cystic duct"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic duct","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cystic duct.</b> The cystic duct runs from the gallbladder neck to join the common hepatic duct, forming the bile duct, with the cystic artery just above it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Neck of the gallbladder</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the common hepatic duct to form the bile duct</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the hepatoduodenal ligament; the cystic artery runs just above it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right free edge of the lesser omentum, forming the lower side of the cystohepatic triangle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the gallbladder and follow the duct leaving it toward the hepatoduodenal ligament until it meets the common hepatic duct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s361.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 361"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.9203,0.389,0.9888,"",1]],"ar":0.56571,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st19~cystic-duct"}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":41,"label":"Jejunum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8287,0.4081,0.919,0.4528]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s363~jejunum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sigmoid colon","B. Ileum","C. Inferior mesenteric artery","D. Jejunum","E. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Jejunum.</b> The jejunum is the first two-fifths of the small intestine, starting at the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 and coiling mostly in the upper left abdomen.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal wall over the left kidney, slung from the mesentery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 under the transverse mesocolon, then follow the loops away from it; hold the mesentery to the light to see the long vasa recta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s363.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 363"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9118,0.389,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.50857}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":41,"label":"Ileum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_267_1632.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.42","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0993,0.7928,0.1726,0.8347]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s365~ileum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Jejunum","B. Ileum","C. Sigmoid colon","D. Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","E. Superior rectal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ileum.</b> The ileum is the last three-fifths of the small bowel past the duodenum, coiled in the lower right abdomen and pelvis, ending at the cecum's medial side.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right posterior abdominal wall, iliac vessels and pelvic organs, slung from the mesentery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow backward the small intestine that enters its medial side; those terminal loops are ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s365.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 365"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9118,0.389,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.50857}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":42,"label":"Cecum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_347_366.jpg","cap":"Cecum and appendix","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.77","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0279,0.6632,0.1555,0.7128]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s251~cecum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Vermiform appendix","B. Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","C. Right colic (hepatic) flexure","D. Cecum","E. Rectum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cecum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Cecum.</b> The cecum is the blind-ended pouch of large bowel in the right iliac fossa, hanging below the ileocecal junction, with the appendix attached to its posteromedial side.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right iliacus and psoas major, with the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the terminal ileum and the ileocecal junction; the large sac hanging below that junction is the cecum, and its three teniae meet at the appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s251.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 251"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9129,0.389,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.515}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":42,"label":"Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_278_1658.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.53","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3702,0.2788,0.4587,0.3711]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s385~ileocecal-junction","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ascending colon","B. Cecum","C. Vermiform appendix","D. Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","E. Right colic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve).</b> The ileocecal junction is where the terminal ileum joins the medial side of the large bowel in the right iliac fossa, between cecum below and ascending colon above.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus and psoas major in the right iliac fossa</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow the terminal ileum to the point where it joins the large bowel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s385.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 385"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3126,0.9321,0.578,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.90728}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":42,"label":"Vermiform appendix","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_278_1658.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.53","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3185,0.6214,0.4062,0.6759]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s259~vermiform-appendix","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","B. Vermiform appendix","C. Rectum","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Cecum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vermiform appendix","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vermiform appendix.</b> The appendix is a narrow blind tube arising from the posteromedial wall of the cecum below the ileocecal junction, where the three teniae coli meet, hung on the mesoappendix.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Cecum when retrocecal (the usual position); otherwise ileal coils and the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus and psoas major in the right iliac fossa, usually tucked behind the cecum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum and follow any one of its teniae coli down to where the three bands converge; the appendix arises there.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s259.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 259"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9131,0.389,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.51643}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":43,"label":"Ascending colon","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_349_371.jpg","cap":"Colon","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.82","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0397,0.5513,0.3076,0.5836]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s253~ascending-colon","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Left colic (splenic) flexure","C. Ascending colon","D. Descending colon","E. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ascending colon.</b> The ascending colon runs up the right flank from the cecum to the right colic flexure under the liver, with the right paracolic gutter lateral to it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum, the greater omentum and the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus, quadratus lumborum, transversus abdominis aponeurosis and the lower pole of the right kidney</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow the large bowel upward along the right flank to the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s253.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 253"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.3903,0.9173,0.5969,0.9882,"",0],[0.6714,0.9173,0.8779,0.9882,"",0],[0.0935,0.9173,0.3001,0.9882,"",1]],"ar":0.54429}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":43,"label":"Right colic (hepatic) flexure","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg","cap":"Parts of greater and lesser omenta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0047,0.6006,0.1691,0.667]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s399~right-colic-flexure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Left colic (splenic) flexure","C. Descending colon","D. Ascending colon","E. Right colic (hepatic) flexure"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right colic (hepatic) flexure","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right colic (hepatic) flexure.</b> The right colic (hepatic) flexure is where the ascending colon turns left into the transverse colon, under the right lobe of the liver and in front of the right kidney.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right lobe of the liver and the gallbladder fundus, above and in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower anterior surface of the right kidney, with the descending duodenum medial to it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the gallbladder and right lobe of the liver, then follow the ascending colon up to the point where it turns left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s399.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 399"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.181,0.9076,0.389,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.48429}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":43,"label":"Transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg","cap":"Parts of greater and lesser omenta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8375,0.5608,0.9833,0.6022]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s253~transverse-colon","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Ascending colon","C. Left colic (splenic) flexure","D. Transverse colon","E. Right colic (hepatic) flexure"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Transverse colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Transverse colon.</b> The transverse colon hangs on the transverse mesocolon between the right and left colic flexures, below the stomach, with the greater omentum attached along its front.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, fused to its anterior surface, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Descending duodenum, head of the pancreas and small intestine coils, hung on the transverse mesocolon</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the greater omentum upward; the large bowel attached to its back and hanging below the stomach is the transverse colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s253.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 253"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0935,0.9173,0.3001,0.9882,"",0],[0.6714,0.9173,0.8779,0.9882,"",0],[0.3903,0.9173,0.5969,0.9882,"",1]],"ar":0.54429}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":43,"label":"Left colic (splenic) flexure","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_350_373.jpg","cap":"Right and left colic flexures","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6975,0.9191,0.9812,0.9759]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s401~left-colic-flexure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left colic (splenic) flexure","B. Transverse colon","C. Ascending colon","D. Right colic (hepatic) flexure","E. Descending colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left colic (splenic) flexure","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left colic (splenic) flexure.</b> The left colic flexure is where the transverse colon turns sharply down into the descending colon, beside the lower spleen and in front of the left kidney.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater curvature of the stomach and the lower spleen, under the left costal margin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower part of the left kidney; tied to the diaphragm by the phrenicocolic ligament</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the spleen, then follow the transverse colon to the left until it turns sharply down along the left flank.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s401.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 401"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.181,0.9076,0.374,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.48429}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":43,"label":"Descending colon","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_277_1657.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum and large intestine (including appendix)","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.52","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8681,0.3442,0.9935,0.411]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s253~descending-colon","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right colic (hepatic) flexure","B. Left colic (splenic) flexure","C. Ascending colon","D. Transverse colon","E. Descending colon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending colon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Descending colon.</b> The descending colon runs down the left flank from the left colic flexure by the spleen to the iliac fossa, in front of the left kidney's lateral edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of jejunum medially, then the greater omentum and anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lateral border of the left kidney, quadratus lumborum, transversus abdominis aponeurosis and iliacus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the spleen and the left colic flexure, then follow the large bowel down the left flank to the iliac fossa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s253.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 253"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0935,0.9173,0.3001,0.9882,"",0],[0.3903,0.9173,0.5969,0.9882,"",0],[0.6714,0.9173,0.8779,0.9882,"",1]],"ar":0.54429}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":44,"label":"Teniae coli","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_348_367.jpg","cap":"Positions of the appendix","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.79","tb":1,"ov":[[0.2382,0.0144,0.4391,0.0624]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s057~teniae-coli","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Vermiform appendix","B. Right colic artery","C. Sigmoid colon","D. Descending colon","E. Teniae coli"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Teniae coli","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Teniae coli.</b> The teniae coli are three flat bands of longitudinal muscle running along the cecum and colon that meet at the base of the appendix and spread out at the rectosigmoid junction.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Serosa (visceral peritoneum) of the colon</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Circular muscle layer of the colon wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find any part of the colon and look for the pale flat band running lengthwise; follow it on the cecum to where it meets the other two at the appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s057.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 57"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1586,0.9552,0.4318,0.9954,"",1]],"ar":1.70596}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":45,"label":"Sigmoid colon","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_399_1901.jpg","cap":"Sigmoid colon entering lesser pelvis and becoming rectum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8975,0.2025,0.9747,0.2457]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s255~sigmoid-colon","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sigmoid arteries","B. Sigmoid colon","C. Transverse colon","D. Rectum","E. Descending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sigmoid colon","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Sigmoid colon.</b> The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped loop from the end of the descending colon in the left iliac fossa to the rectum at S3, hanging free on the sigmoid mesocolon.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the lower anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left external iliac vessels, piriformis and sacral plexus; rests on the bladder or uterus below</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the descending colon down the left flank to the iliac fossa; the loop that swings free on a mesentery is the sigmoid colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s255.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 255"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9117,0.389,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.50786}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":45,"label":"Rectum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_277_1657.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum and large intestine (including appendix)","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.52","tb":1,"ov":[[0.615,0.7367,0.6975,0.778]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s257~rectum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ileum","B. Urinary bladder","C. Sigmoid colon","D. Middle colic artery","E. Rectum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Rectum.</b> The rectum continues the sigmoid colon from about S3 and follows the curve of the sacrum and coccyx down through the pelvis, with no teniae, haustra or mesentery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Bladder, seminal glands and prostate (male) or uterus and vagina (female), across the peritoneal pouch</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sacrum, coccyx and piriformis, with the mesorectal fat and superior rectal vessels between</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the sigmoid colon down into the pelvis; where its mesocolon ends and the teniae fan out in front of the sacrum, you are on the rectum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s257.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 257"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9137,0.389,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.52}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":46,"label":"Superior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116","tb":1,"ov":[[0.525,0.1147,0.8671,0.156]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s367~superior-mesenteric-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Superior mesenteric vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Superior mesenteric artery.</b> The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Midgut: duodenum below the major papilla to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the pancreas and the third part of the duodenum, then the artery emerging between them just left of the superior mesenteric vein; trace it up to the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s367.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 367"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0,0.0888,0.34,0.1778,"",0],[0.216,0.9252,0.5133,0.9898,"",1]],"ar":0.605}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":46,"label":"Superior mesenteric vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg","cap":"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41","tb":1,"ov":[[0.119,0.8825,0.3394,0.9341]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s383~superior-mesenteric-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric vein","B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Superior mesenteric vein.</b> The superior mesenteric vein climbs just right of the superior mesenteric artery and joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas at its end; the root of the mesentery below that</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Third part of the duodenum and uncinate process, right of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the superior mesenteric artery below the pancreas, then take the wider, thin-walled vessel immediately to its right and follow it up behind the pancreatic neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s383.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 383"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9069,0.4954,0.9861,"",1]],"ar":0.48071}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":46,"label":"Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_363_393.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply to the pancreas","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.101","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0195,0.656,0.4544,0.7348]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s325~inferior-pancreaticoduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Vasa recta (straight arteries)","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery.</b> The posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery branches from the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery off the superior mesenteric artery and climbs behind the pancreatic head beside the duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery), usually its first branch, or the first jejunal artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Head of the pancreas and lower duodenum; anterior and posterior branches join the superior pancreaticoduodenals</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Root of the transverse mesocolon (anterior branch); the pancreatic head covers the posterior branch</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Head of the pancreas, in the groove between it and the duodenal C</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the superior mesenteric artery where it emerges below the pancreatic neck, then follow its first small right-sided branch into the groove between the pancreatic head and the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s325.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 325"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9129,0.724,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.515}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":46,"label":"Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8287,0.473,0.978,0.518]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s369~intestinal-arteries","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Superior rectal artery","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries.</b> On a superior mesenteric angiogram the jejunal and ileal branches are the many arteries fanning from the left side of the superior mesenteric artery toward the small bowel.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), about 15–18 branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Jejunum and ileum, through arterial arcades and vasa recta</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the mesentery, then the greater omentum in the intact abdomen</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Between the two peritoneal layers of the mesentery, in its fat</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Spread the small-intestine mesentery and hold it up to the light; trace the branches nearest the root back to the left side of the superior mesenteric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s369.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 369"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.9636,0.8522,0.9938,0.8857,"",0],[0.216,0.9137,0.4876,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.52}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":46,"label":"Vasa recta (straight arteries)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8287,0.6342,0.9387,0.6789]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s371~vasa-recta","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sigmoid arteries","B. Superior mesenteric vein","C. Vasa recta (straight arteries)","D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Superior mesenteric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vasa recta (straight arteries)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vasa recta (straight arteries).</b> The vasa recta are the straight arteries running from the outermost arterial arcade in the mesentery to the small intestine wall, long in the jejunum and short in the ileum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Outermost (terminal) arterial arcade in the mesentery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Mesenteric border of the small intestine, alternating sides; long (jejunum), short (ileum)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the mesentery, with little fat over them by the jejunum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Between the two peritoneal layers of the mesentery, near the gut wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the outermost arterial arcade, then look at the fine straight vessels between it and the intestinal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s371.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 371"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9098,0.389,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49643}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":47,"label":"Middle colic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1662.jpg","cap":"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0139,0.2704,0.1818,0.3179]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s381~middle-colic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ileocolic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Left colic artery","D. Appendicular vein","E. Right colic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Middle colic artery.</b> The middle colic artery leaves the front of the superior mesenteric artery just below the pancreas and runs inside the transverse mesocolon to the transverse colon.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), just below the pancreas</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Transverse colon; right and left branches join the right and left colic arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Upper layer of the transverse mesocolon, the floor of the lesser sac under the stomach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower layer of the transverse mesocolon, over the duodenum and jejunum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the transverse colon upward to stretch the transverse mesocolon, then find the artery entering it from the SMA at the lower border of the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s381.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 381"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9073,0.4269,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.48286}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":47,"label":"Right colic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6887,0.5091,0.8167,0.5413]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s379.jpg","cap":"Right colic artery on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 379","ov":[[0.214,0.906,0.416,0.9883]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st17~right-colic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Appendicular vein","B. Middle colic artery","C. Ileocolic artery","D. Left colic artery","E. Right colic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right colic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right colic artery.</b> The right colic artery leaves the right side of the superior mesenteric artery, between the middle colic and ileocolic arteries, and runs behind the peritoneum to the ascending colon.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); may share a trunk with the middle colic or ileocolic</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Ascending colon; ascending and descending branches join the middle colic and ileocolic</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (right infracolic space)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels on the posterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Reflect the ascending colon medially or strip the posterior peritoneum right of the SMA; the right colic is the branch heading straight across to the ascending colon between the middle colic and ileocolic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st17.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 17"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1823,0.9508,0.3998,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.01839}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":47,"label":"Ileocolic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0068,0.5416,0.0992,0.5863]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s373~ileocolic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right colic artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Ileocolic artery","D. Appendicular vein","E. Middle colic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ileocolic artery.</b> The ileocolic artery is the last right-side branch of the superior mesenteric artery, running down and right to the ileocecal junction, where it gives the appendicular artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery), its lowest right-sided (terminal) branch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Terminal ileum, cecum, appendix (appendicular artery) and lower ascending colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum along the root of the mesentery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels, toward the right iliac fossa</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ileocecal junction in the right iliac fossa, then follow the artery that reaches it back up toward the superior mesenteric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s373.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 373"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9069,0.3904,0.9861,"",1]],"ar":0.48071}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":47,"label":"Appendicular vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_257_1614.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and venous drainage of abdominal parts of alimentary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.32","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0085,0.8146,0.1836,0.8593]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s377~appendicular-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric vein","B. Middle colic artery","C. Ileocolic artery","D. Appendicular vein","E. Right colic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendicular vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Appendicular vein.</b> The appendicular vein runs with the appendicular artery in the free edge of the mesoappendix and drains into the ileocolic vein, a tributary of the superior mesenteric vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Vermiform appendix, via the mesoappendix</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Ileocolic vein, a tributary of the SMV (superior mesenteric vein)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the mesoappendix; terminal ileum crosses in front near its origin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inside the free edge of the mesoappendix, which hangs behind the terminal ileum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the appendix and its mesoappendix; the vein running alongside the appendicular artery in the free edge is the appendicular vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s377.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 377"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9099,0.424,0.9867,"",1]],"ar":0.49714}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":47,"label":"Right colic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6887,0.5091,0.8167,0.5413]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st17.jpg","cap":"Right colic artery on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 17","ov":[[0.1803,0.9488,0.4018,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s379~right-colic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ileocolic artery","B. Left colic artery","C. Right colic artery","D. Middle colic artery","E. Appendicular vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right colic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right colic artery.</b> The right colic artery leaves the right side of the superior mesenteric artery, between the middle colic and ileocolic arteries, and runs behind the peritoneum to the ascending colon.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); may share a trunk with the middle colic or ileocolic</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Ascending colon; ascending and descending branches join the middle colic and ileocolic</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (right infracolic space)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels on the posterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Reflect the ascending colon medially or strip the posterior peritoneum right of the SMA; the right colic is the branch heading straight across to the ascending colon between the middle colic and ileocolic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s379.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 379"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.216,0.908,0.414,0.9863,"",1]],"ar":0.48643,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st17~right-colic-artery"}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":48,"label":"Inferior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1662.jpg","cap":"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8047,0.6375,0.9631,0.7159]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s387~inferior-mesenteric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior rectal artery","B. Sigmoid arteries","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Left colic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Inferior mesenteric artery.</b> The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L3, behind the third part of the duodenum, and runs down and left behind the peritoneum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L3, 3–4 cm above the bifurcation</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Hindgut: distal third of the transverse colon to the upper rectum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Third part of the duodenum at its origin, then the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta, then the left psoas major as it runs down and left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Push the small intestine to the right and find the aortic bifurcation, then look 3-4 cm above it on the front of the aorta for the artery heading down to the left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s387.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 387"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9132,0.499,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.51714}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":48,"label":"Inferior mesenteric vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1661.jpg","cap":"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8054,0.3243,0.9831,0.4371]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s395~inferior-mesenteric-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sigmoid arteries","B. Superior mesenteric vein","C. Inferior mesenteric artery","D. Left colic artery","E. Inferior mesenteric vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Inferior mesenteric vein.</b> The inferior mesenteric vein climbs the left posterior abdominal wall, just left of the duodenojejunal flexure, then passes behind the pancreas to usually drain into the splenic vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Continuation of the superior rectal vein; receives the sigmoid and left colic veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Splenic vein behind the pancreas (usual); sometimes the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) or their junction</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum (paraduodenal fold), then the body of the pancreas</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left psoas major, just left of the duodenojejunal flexure</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the transverse colon and push the small intestine to the right; find the duodenojejunal flexure and look just to its left for the vertical vein heading up under the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s395.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 395"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9107,0.4811,0.9868,"",1]],"ar":0.50214}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":48,"label":"Left colic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1662.jpg","cap":"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8047,0.5385,0.9437,0.586]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s389~left-colic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sigmoid arteries","B. Left colic artery","C. Inferior mesenteric artery","D. Right colic artery","E. Superior rectal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left colic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left colic artery.</b> The left colic artery is the first branch of the inferior mesenteric artery, running left behind the peritoneum toward the descending colon.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its first branch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Descending colon and distal transverse colon; ascending branch joins the middle colic via the marginal artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (left infracolic space)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left psoas major, left ureter and left gonadal vessels</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior mesenteric artery on the front of the aorta, then follow its first branch to the left across the posterior wall toward the descending colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s389.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 389"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.908,0.399,0.9863,"",1]],"ar":0.48643}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":48,"label":"Sigmoid arteries","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_378_412.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.117","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7407,0.8604,0.9849,0.8979]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s391~sigmoid-arteries","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior rectal artery","B. Left colic artery","C. Inferior mesenteric vein","D. Sigmoid arteries","E. Inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sigmoid arteries","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sigmoid arteries.</b> The sigmoid arteries are two to four branches of the inferior mesenteric artery that run down and left in the sigmoid mesocolon, between the left colic and superior rectal arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), usually 2–4 branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Sigmoid colon and the lowest descending colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left psoas major and left ureter, then between the layers of the sigmoid mesocolon</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the sigmoid colon to spread its mesocolon, then follow the vessels inside it back to the inferior mesenteric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s391.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 391"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9077,0.4069,0.9862,"",1]],"ar":0.485}]},{"l":"VB-A4","u":48,"label":"Superior rectal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_400_1903.jpg","cap":"Arteries and veins of rectum and anal canal","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.32","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0131,0.0184,0.2905,0.0724]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s393~superior-rectal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior mesenteric vein","C. Superior rectal artery","D. Left colic artery","E. Sigmoid arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior rectal artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Superior rectal artery.</b> The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, crossing the left common iliac vessels in the sigmoid mesocolon root to reach the back of the rectum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectum via two branches beside it; anastomoses with the middle rectal arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon root, then the rectum in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left common iliac vessels, then the sacrum (S3) behind the rectum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior mesenteric artery, then follow its main stem downward past the sigmoid branches over the pelvic brim to the back of the rectum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s393.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 393"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9098,0.4569,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49643}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":49,"label":"Psoas major muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_341_1787.jpg","cap":"Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.97","tb":1,"ov":[[0.775,0.4509,0.903,0.5005]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s465~psoas-major-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Psoas major muscle","B. Psoas minor muscle","C. Lumbosacral trunk","D. Iliacus muscle","E. Quadratus lumborum muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Psoas major muscle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Psoas major muscle.</b> Psoas major is the thick round muscle hugging the sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies, with the kidney in front and lateral and quadratus lumborum behind and lateral.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Sides of the T12–L5 vertebral bodies, intervening discs and lumbar transverse processes</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Lesser trochanter of the femur, joined with iliacus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney, ureter, gonadal vessels and genitofemoral nerve, behind the peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lumbar transverse processes and vertebral bodies; quadratus lumborum lies posterolateral</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find a lumbar vertebral body; the large round muscle hugging its side, anterior to the transverse process, is psoas major, with the kidney anterolateral and quadratus lumborum posterolateral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s465.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 465"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9088,0.4469,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.49071}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":49,"label":"Psoas minor muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_341_1787.jpg","cap":"Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.97","tb":1,"ov":[[0.775,0.6463,0.903,0.6962]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s467~psoas-minor-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Transversus abdominis muscle","B. Iliacus muscle","C. Psoas minor muscle","D. Psoas major muscle","E. Quadratus lumborum muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Psoas minor muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Psoas minor muscle.</b> Psoas minor is a small muscle from T12 and L1 whose long flat tendon runs down the front of psoas major; it is absent in many people.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Sides of the T12 and L1 vertebral bodies and the disc between</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Pectineal line and iliopubic eminence</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Psoas fascia and extraperitoneal fat beneath the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of psoas major</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas major and look on its anterior surface for a thin, glistening flat tendon running along its length.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s467.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 467"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9088,0.4476,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.49071}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":49,"label":"Quadratus lumborum muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_403_443.jpg","cap":"Organization of fat and fascia surrounding the kidney","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.140","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3727,0.939,0.6121,0.9863]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s471~quadratus-lumborum-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Quadratus lumborum muscle","B. Psoas minor muscle","C. Psoas major muscle","D. Pyramidalis muscle","E. Iliacus muscle"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Quadratus lumborum muscle","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Quadratus lumborum muscle.</b> Quadratus lumborum is the flat posterior abdominal wall muscle between the iliac crest and 12th rib, behind and lateral to psoas major and behind the kidney.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Iliolumbar ligament and internal lip of the iliac crest</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Medial half of the 12th rib's lower border and tips of the lumbar transverse processes</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior thoracolumbar fascia layer, then the kidney; subcostal, iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves cross it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Middle layer of the thoracolumbar fascia, then erector spinae</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas beside the vertebral bodies; the flat muscle lateral to it at the tips of the transverse processes, behind the kidney, is quadratus lumborum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s471.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 471"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9134,0.5361,0.9874,"",1]],"ar":0.51857}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":49,"label":"Iliacus muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_231_1574.jpg","cap":"Formations of inguinal region","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.14","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1387,0.3574,0.2012,0.4154]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s469~iliacus-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Psoas major muscle","B. Quadratus lumborum muscle","C. Psoas minor muscle","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. Iliacus muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliacus muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Iliacus muscle.</b> The iliacus lines the iliac fossa lateral to psoas major, with the femoral nerve in the groove between them, and joins psoas to insert on the lesser trochanter.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa, inner iliac crest and ala of the sacrum</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Lesser trochanter of the femur, with the psoas major tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliac fascia, crossed by the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve; cecum (right), descending colon (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliac fossa of the ilium</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the wing of the ilium; the muscle lining its inner (abdominal) surface, lateral to psoas, is iliacus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s469.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 469"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.906,0.389,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.47571}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":49,"label":"Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_341_1787.jpg","cap":"Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.97","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8555,0.8536,0.9512,0.9036]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s473~iliopsoas-muscle","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Psoas minor muscle","B. Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus)","C. Quadratus lumborum muscle","D. Internal oblique muscle","E. Rectus abdominis muscle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus).</b> The iliopsoas is psoas major and iliacus merged below the iliac fossa, passing under the inguinal ligament lateral to the femoral vessels to insert on the lesser trochanter.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Psoas major (T12–L5 vertebrae) and iliacus (iliac fossa)</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Lesser trochanter of the femur</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Inguinal ligament and iliac fascia, with the femoral nerve on its front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Hip joint capsule and superior pubic ramus, under the inguinal ligament</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow psoas major and iliacus down to the inguinal ligament; where they have merged into one muscle passing under it into the thigh, it is iliopsoas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s473.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 473"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.6652,0.1832,0.748,0.2175,"",0],[0.6168,0.2667,0.7053,0.3009,"",0],[0.201,0.9149,0.3961,0.9877,"",1]],"ar":0.52786}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":50,"label":"Kidney","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_403_443.jpg","cap":"Organization of fat and fascia surrounding the kidney","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.140","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4765,0.1324,0.548,0.1798]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s419~kidney","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Kidney","B. Obturator nerve","C. Perirenal (perinephric) fat","D. Subcostal nerve","E. Suprarenal (adrenal) gland"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kidney","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kidney.</b> Each kidney lies behind the peritoneum on psoas major and quadratus lumborum at T12 to L3, with the suprarenal gland on its upper pole and its hilum facing medially.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right: liver, duodenum, right colic flexure; left: stomach, spleen, pancreas, jejunum, left colic flexure</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm, psoas major, quadratus lumborum and transversus abdominis aponeurosis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas and quadratus lumborum; the bean-shaped organ lying on them in a bed of fat, with vessels entering its medial notch, is the kidney.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s419.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 419"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9091,0.389,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49286}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":50,"label":"Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg","cap":"External and internal appearance of kidneys","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3507,0.5172,0.4805,0.5599]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s443~fibrous-capsule-of-kidney","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal pelvis","B. Right kidney","C. Renal cortex","D. Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule)","E. Left kidney"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule).</b> The renal capsule is the thin, tough membrane on the kidney's surface, deep to the perirenal fat, turning inward at the hilum to line the renal sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Perirenal fat, then the renal fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal cortex (surface of the kidney)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Clean the perirenal fat off the kidney; the thin shiny membrane left on its surface, which can be peeled off, is the fibrous capsule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s443.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 443"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.92,0.389,0.9887,"",1]],"ar":0.56357}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Renal cortex","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg","cap":"External and internal appearance of kidneys","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8262,0.523,0.9327,0.5657]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s439~renal-cortex","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal pelvis","B. Renal pyramid (medulla)","C. Major calyx","D. Minor calyx","E. Renal cortex"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal cortex","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Renal cortex.</b> The renal cortex is the outer pale band of the kidney just under the capsule, dipping inward as renal columns between the darker striped medullary pyramids.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Fibrous capsule of the kidney</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal pyramids (medulla), between which it dips as renal columns</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the striated triangular pyramids; the uniform tissue between their bases and the capsule, and the columns between them, is cortex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s439.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 439"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2249,0.9296,0.4022,0.9904,"",1]],"ar":0.66368}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Renal pyramid (medulla)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg","cap":"External and internal appearance of kidneys","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8262,0.9065,0.9542,0.9494]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s441.jpg","cap":"Renal pyramid (medulla) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 441","ov":[[0.2229,0.9276,0.4042,0.9924]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s059~renal-pyramid","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal pyramid (medulla)","B. Renal cortex","C. Renal pelvis","D. Ureter","E. Minor calyx"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal pyramid (medulla)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Renal pyramid (medulla).</b> The renal pyramids make up the medulla: striped cones deep to the cortex, separated by renal columns, with their tips (papillae) pointing into the minor calyces.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal cortex at its base, beneath the capsule</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal sinus and minor calyx, into which its papilla projects</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal sinus and calyces in the centre; the striped triangles pointing at them are the pyramids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s059.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 59"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1158,0.9552,0.3279,0.9954,"",1]],"ar":1.25659}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Minor calyx","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_404_444.jpg","cap":"Internal structure of the kidney","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.141","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0563,0.678,0.1728,0.722]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s445~minor-calyx","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Renal pyramid (medulla)","B. Minor calyx","C. Renal pelvis","D. Major calyx","E. Ureter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Minor calyx","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Minor calyx.</b> Each minor calyx is a small cup in the renal sinus wrapped around the tip (papilla) of a renal pyramid, joining others to form a major calyx.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal papilla of one pyramid (sometimes two or three), draining its collecting ducts</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Major calyx (several minor calyces join each one)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find a pyramid's apex and the small cup wrapped around it; follow its stem to where it joins others to form a major calyx.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s445.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 445"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8584,0.4318,0.9757,0.4674,"",0],[0.216,0.9182,0.389,0.9883,"",1]],"ar":0.55}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Major calyx","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_404_444.jpg","cap":"Internal structure of the kidney","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.141","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6861,0.2434,0.8026,0.2873]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s447~major-calyx","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Ureter","B. Major calyx","C. Renal pelvis","D. Minor calyx","E. Renal pyramid (medulla)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Major calyx","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Major calyx.</b> The major calyces are the two or three main branches of the renal pelvis inside the renal sinus, each formed where several minor calyces join.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Union of several minor calyces in the renal sinus</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, inside the renal sinus</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow its two or three main branches outward; these are the major calyces, which then divide into minor calyces.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s447.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 447"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8894,0.1672,0.9553,0.2363,"",0],[0.216,0.9182,0.389,0.9883,"",1]],"ar":0.55}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Renal pelvis","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg","cap":"External and internal appearance of kidneys","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3507,0.265,0.4545,0.3077]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s431~renal-pelvis","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal cortex","B. Minor calyx","C. Renal pelvis","D. Renal pyramid (medulla)","E. Major calyx"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal pelvis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Renal pelvis.</b> The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped top of the ureter, formed in the renal sinus by the major calyces and lying behind the renal vessels at the hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Union of two or three major calyces in the renal sinus</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Ureter, at the ureteropelvic junction near the hilum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal artery, then renal vein (most anterior), in front of it at the hilum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior segmental artery, then psoas major, behind it at the hilum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter and follow it up into the hilum; the expansion where it widens and receives the major calyces is the renal pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s431.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 431"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9125,0.389,0.9872,"",1]],"ar":0.51286}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Ureter","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.809,0.7503,0.8727,0.7894]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s433~ureter","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ureter","B. Major calyx","C. Renal pelvis","D. Minor calyx","E. Renal pyramid (medulla)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ureter","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ureter.</b> Each ureter runs from the renal pelvis down the front of psoas major behind the peritoneum, crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels, and enters the pelvis over the common iliac bifurcation.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, at the ureteropelvic junction</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels; mesentery root (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow the tube down on psoas to the pelvic brim; check that the gonadal vessels cross in front of it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s433.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 433"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9142,0.389,0.9875,"",1]],"ar":0.52357}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":51,"label":"Renal pyramid (medulla)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg","cap":"External and internal appearance of kidneys","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8262,0.9065,0.9542,0.9494]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s059.jpg","cap":"Renal pyramid (medulla) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 59","ov":[[0.1138,0.9532,0.3299,0.9974]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s441~renal-pyramid","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Major calyx","B. Renal cortex","C. Renal pelvis","D. Renal pyramid (medulla)","E. Ureter"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal pyramid (medulla)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Renal pyramid (medulla).</b> The renal pyramids make up the medulla: striped cones deep to the cortex, separated by renal columns, with their tips (papillae) pointing into the minor calyces.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal cortex at its base, beneath the capsule</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal sinus and minor calyx, into which its papilla projects</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal sinus and calyces in the centre; the striped triangles pointing at them are the pyramids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s441.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 441"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2249,0.9296,0.4022,0.9904,"",1]],"ar":0.66368,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s059~renal-pyramid"}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":52,"label":"Renal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_325_1756.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply of kidneys and ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.84","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0178,0.5402,0.1994,0.6218]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s435~renal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal vein","B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","C. Renal artery","D. Segmental arteries of kidney","E. Left suprarenal vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Renal artery.</b> Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Kidney via five segmental arteries; also the inferior suprarenal artery and upper ureter</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal vein; the right also passes behind the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Crus of the diaphragm and psoas major; renal pelvis behind it at the hilum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta just below the SMA origin and follow the lateral branch to the kidney; at the hilum check that the vein is in front of it and the pelvis behind it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s435.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 435"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2292,0.9318,0.4143,0.9905,"",1]],"ar":0.72414}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":52,"label":"Segmental arteries of kidney","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg","cap":"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7071,0.5871,0.8823,0.6298]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s429~segmental-arteries-of-kidney","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal artery","B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","C. Renal vein","D. Segmental arteries of kidney","E. Left suprarenal vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Segmental arteries of kidney","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Segmental arteries of kidney.</b> The segmental arteries are the five end-arteries the renal artery splits into at the renal hilum, each feeding one kidney segment before dividing into interlobar arteries in the renal sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Renal artery, at or near the hilum, via its anterior and posterior divisions</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Five kidney segments as end-arteries; each divides into interlobar arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal vein and its tributaries, in the renal sinus fat</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal pelvis (anterior segmentals); the posterior segmental artery passes behind the pelvis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Follow the renal artery into the hilum; the first set of branches it divides into, before entering the kidney tissue, are segmental arteries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s429.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 429"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9125,0.5311,0.9872,"",1]],"ar":0.51286}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":52,"label":"Renal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg","cap":"External and internal appearance of kidneys","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3507,0.2216,0.4428,0.2642]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s425~renal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal artery","B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","C. Left suprarenal vein","D. Segmental arteries of kidney","E. Renal vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Renal vein.</b> The renal veins leave each hilum in front of the renal arteries and drain into the inferior vena cava; the left is longer and crosses in front of the aorta.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Kidney, via veins joining in the renal sinus</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), at about L1–L2</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Descending duodenum (right); SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and pancreas (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal artery behind it at the hilum; the left vein crosses in front of the aorta</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC; the large veins entering its sides from each kidney hilum are the renal veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s425.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 425"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2367,0.937,0.4274,0.9916,"",1]],"ar":0.81296}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":52,"label":"Left suprarenal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg","cap":"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7061,0.5044,0.8887,0.5471]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s427~left-suprarenal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal vein","B. Renal artery","C. Segmental arteries of kidney","D. Left suprarenal vein","E. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left suprarenal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left suprarenal vein.</b> The left suprarenal vein leaves the front of the left suprarenal gland and runs down to drain into the upper border of the left renal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland; often joined by the left inferior phrenic vein</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Upper border of the left renal vein</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Body of the pancreas and splenic artery, behind the stomach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the left suprarenal gland, then the left crus of the diaphragm</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left renal vein where it crosses in front of the aorta, then trace the vessel entering its upper border up to the suprarenal gland.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s427.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 427"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9107,0.4426,0.9868,"",1]],"ar":0.50214}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":52,"label":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8406,0.6989,0.9704,0.7608]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s423~gonadal-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Renal vein","B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","C. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","D. Renal artery","E. Segmental arteries of kidney"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian).</b> The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Testis via the spermatic cord, or ovary via the suspensory ligament of the ovary</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed by the duodenum and colic vessels (right) or left colic vessels (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Psoas major and the ureter; the right one also crosses in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter on psoas; the thin vessels crossing in front of it obliquely are the gonadal vessels. Follow the narrower one up to its origin on the aorta just below the renal arteries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s423.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 423"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0,0.011,0.6127,0.1069,"",0],[0.216,0.9215,0.4083,0.989,"",1]],"ar":0.575}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":53,"label":"Inferior phrenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0182,0.1032,0.1486,0.1425]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s457~inferior-phrenic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","B. Renal artery","C. Inferior phrenic artery","D. Celiac ganglion","E. Lumbar arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior phrenic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Inferior phrenic artery.</b> The inferior phrenic arteries, the abdominal aorta's first branches, climb onto the diaphragm's underside, the right behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta just below the aortic hiatus, or the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Undersurface of the diaphragm; gives the superior suprarenal arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right one; esophagus over the left one</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Crura and undersurface of the diaphragm</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Look at the underside of the diaphragm beside the crura; the artery climbing onto it from the top of the abdominal aorta is the inferior phrenic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s457.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 457"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9063,0.4797,0.9859,"",1]],"ar":0.47714}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":53,"label":"Celiac ganglion","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_424_469.jpg","cap":"Prevertebral ganglia associated with the prevertebral plexus","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.159","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8182,0.1487,0.9655,0.1867]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s501~celiac-ganglion","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Celiac ganglion","B. Iliacus muscle","C. Ureter","D. Inferior phrenic artery","E. Lumbar arteries"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac ganglion","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Celiac ganglion.</b> The celiac ganglia are paired firm nerve masses lying on the diaphragmatic crura on either side of the celiac trunk's origin, each receiving the greater splanchnic nerve.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Greater splanchnic nerves (T5–T9 presynaptic sympathetic); vagal fibers pass through without synapsing</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Foregut organs via periarterial plexuses on the celiac trunk branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right one; splenic artery and pancreas over the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Crura of the diaphragm, on either side of the celiac trunk origin</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk at the aorta and clear the tough fibrous tissue around its origin; the firm masses on each side are the ganglia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s501.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 501"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9094,0.3961,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49429}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":53,"label":"Lumbar arteries","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_532_2181.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply of spinal cord","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 4.46","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6495,0.6643,0.8867,0.6953]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s459~lumbar-arteries","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal artery","B. Celiac ganglion","C. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","D. Lumbar arteries","E. Inferior phrenic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar arteries","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lumbar arteries.</b> The lumbar arteries are four paired branches from the back of the abdominal aorta at L1 to L4, hugging the vertebral bodies behind the sympathetic trunks and psoas.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal aorta opposite the L1–L4 vertebral bodies (four pairs)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal wall, back muscles, vertebrae and spinal cord</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Sympathetic trunks and psoas major; right ones also behind the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the aorta off the lumbar spine; the paired arteries leaving its back and hugging the sides of the vertebral bodies are lumbar arteries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s459.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 459"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9115,0.4019,0.987,"",1]],"ar":0.50643}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":54,"label":"Common iliac artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_383_1878.jpg","cap":"Arteries of pelvis","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.17","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0069,0.1026,0.199,0.1626]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s461~common-iliac-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Internal iliac artery and vein","B. External iliac artery and vein","C. Common iliac artery","D. Renal artery","E. Inferior phrenic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Common iliac artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Common iliac artery.</b> Each common iliac artery runs from the L4 aortic bifurcation to the sacroiliac joint, splitting into external and internal iliacs; the right overlies the inferior vena cava's origin.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta bifurcation at L4, slightly left of midline</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>External and internal iliac arteries, in front of the sacroiliac joint (L5–S1)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum and ureter at its end; small intestine (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>L4–L5 bodies and common iliac veins; the right crosses the IVC (inferior vena cava) origin</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aortic bifurcation on L4 and follow either branch down to the pelvic brim, where it splits into external and internal iliac arteries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s461.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 461"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9085,0.4454,0.9864,"",1]],"ar":0.48929}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Subcostal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg","cap":"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0375,0.2026,0.1746,0.2406]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s485~subcostal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","C. Subcostal nerve","D. Iliohypogastric nerve","E. Obturator nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Subcostal nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Subcostal nerve.</b> The subcostal nerve is the T12 anterior ramus, running along the lower border of the 12th rib, behind the lateral arcuate ligament and across quadratus lumborum behind the kidney.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior ramus of T12</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterolateral abdominal muscles, suprapubic skin and skin over the hip</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney (posterior surface) and colon, then internal oblique in the anterior wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum, then transversus abdominis in the anterior wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the 12th rib on the posterior abdominal wall; the nerve just below its lower border crossing quadratus lumborum is the subcostal nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s485.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 485"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9094,0.4047,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49429}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Iliohypogastric nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg","cap":"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0054,0.2565,0.1753,0.2945]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s481~iliohypogastric-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","C. Obturator nerve","D. Iliohypogastric nerve","E. Subcostal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Iliohypogastric nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Iliohypogastric nerve.</b> The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) crosses in front of quadratus lumborum, then runs between internal oblique and transversus abdominis, just above the ilioinguinal nerve.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior ramus of L1, often with T12</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Internal oblique and transversus abdominis; suprapubic skin and upper lateral buttock skin</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lower pole of the kidney, then internal oblique, finally the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum, then transversus abdominis after piercing it above the iliac crest</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ilioinguinal nerve in the inguinal canal, then look just above it between internal oblique and transversus near the iliac crest; the parallel nerve that stays above the canal is the iliohypogastric.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s481.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 481"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2514,0.9321,0.5267,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.79574}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_427_473.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6695,0.2481,0.8685,0.2824]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s477.jpg","cap":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 477","ov":[[0.2383,0.9059,0.651,0.9883]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s479.jpg","cap":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 479","ov":[[0.2383,0.9059,0.6624,0.9883]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q23~genitofemoral-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Obturator nerve","B. Iliohypogastric nerve","C. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","D. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","E. Subcostal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch).</b> The genitofemoral nerve pierces psoas major and runs down its front; its genital branch enters the deep inguinal ring, its femoral branch passes under the inguinal ligament.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of L1–L2 (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Genital branch: cremaster and anterior scrotum or labium majus; femoral branch: skin over the femoral triangle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ureter and gonadal vessels crossing in front, under the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of psoas major</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas major; the nerve lying on its front surface, not at its borders, is the genitofemoral nerve. Follow it down to where it divides above the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q23.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 23"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1946,0.9526,0.4261,0.9953,"",1]],"ar":0.98643}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_231_1574.jpg","cap":"Formations of inguinal region","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.14","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0166,0.1794,0.1627,0.2776]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s483~lateral-femoral-cutaneous-nerve","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Obturator nerve","B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","C. Iliohypogastric nerve","D. Subcostal nerve","E. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh).</b> The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve leaves the lateral border of psoas major, crosses iliacus, and passes under the inguinal ligament just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of L2–L3 (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of the anterolateral thigh</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliac fascia, then the cecum on the right or descending colon on the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus, crossing obliquely toward the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior superior iliac spine from inside the abdomen; the thin nerve on iliacus heading toward it is the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s483.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 483"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.9799,0.8582,1,0.8921,"",0],[0.216,0.9129,0.5697,0.9873,"",1]],"ar":0.515}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Femoral nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_427_473.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.148,0.509,0.3271,0.5433]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s487~femoral-nerve","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Subcostal nerve","B. Lumbosacral trunk","C. Iliohypogastric nerve","D. Obturator nerve","E. Femoral nerve"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Femoral nerve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Femoral nerve.</b> The femoral nerve leaves psoas major's lateral border, runs in the groove between psoas and iliacus, and passes under the inguinal ligament lateral to the femoral artery.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Posterior divisions of the L2–L4 anterior rami (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Iliacus, anterior thigh muscles (quadriceps, sartorius, pectineus) and anterior thigh skin; saphenous nerve</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliac fascia, then the inguinal ligament as it enters the thigh</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus, in the groove between it and psoas major</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas and iliacus at the pelvic brim; the thick flat nerve lying in the groove between them is the femoral nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s487.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 487"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.9778,0.864,1,0.8978,"",0],[0.216,0.9121,0.389,0.9871,"",1]],"ar":0.51}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Obturator nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_427_473.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6691,0.3815,0.8548,0.4158]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s489~obturator-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Lumbosacral trunk","B. Obturator nerve","C. Iliohypogastric nerve","D. Femoral nerve","E. Subcostal nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Obturator nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Obturator nerve.</b> The obturator nerve leaves the medial border of psoas major at the pelvic brim, passes behind the common iliac vessels, and follows the pelvic wall to the obturator foramen.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior divisions of the L2–L4 anterior rami (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Medial thigh adductor muscles, obturator externus and skin of the medial thigh</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Common iliac vessels at the brim, then pelvic peritoneum and, in females, the ovary</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ala of the sacrum, then obturator internus on the lateral pelvic wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the medial border of psoas at the pelvic brim; the nerve slipping behind the common iliac vessels into the pelvis is the obturator nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s489.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 489"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.8958,0.4011,0.9838,"",1]],"ar":0.42714}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Lumbosacral trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_387_1884.jpg","cap":"Nerves and nerve plexuses of pelvis","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.21","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1102,0.2706,0.2604,0.3142]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s491~lumbosacral-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Iliohypogastric nerve","B. Obturator nerve","C. Subcostal nerve","D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","E. Lumbosacral trunk"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbosacral trunk","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lumbosacral trunk.</b> The lumbosacral trunk (L4 and L5) descends over the sacral ala, medial to psoas major and behind the common iliac vessels, to join the sacral plexus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Part of the L4 anterior ramus joining the L5 anterior ramus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Sacral plexus, joining the S1 anterior ramus; feeds the sciatic and gluteal nerves</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Common iliac vessels, then the internal iliac vessels at the pelvic brim</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ala of the sacrum, medial to psoas major</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Retract psoas major laterally at the pelvic brim; the thick nerve crossing the sacral ala into the pelvis is the lumbosacral trunk.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s491.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 491"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.216,0.9158,0.4319,0.9879,"",1]],"ar":0.53357}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_427_473.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6695,0.2481,0.8685,0.2824]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q23.jpg","cap":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 23","ov":[[0.1926,0.9506,0.4281,0.9973]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s479.jpg","cap":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 479","ov":[[0.2383,0.9059,0.6624,0.9883]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s477~genitofemoral-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Obturator nerve","B. Iliohypogastric nerve","C. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","D. Subcostal nerve","E. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch).</b> The genitofemoral nerve pierces psoas major and runs down its front; its genital branch enters the deep inguinal ring, its femoral branch passes under the inguinal ligament.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of L1–L2 (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Genital branch: cremaster and anterior scrotum or labium majus; femoral branch: skin over the femoral triangle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ureter and gonadal vessels crossing in front, under the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of psoas major</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas major; the nerve lying on its front surface, not at its borders, is the genitofemoral nerve. Follow it down to where it divides above the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s477.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 477"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9079,0.649,0.9863,"",1]],"ar":0.48571,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q23~genitofemoral-nerve"}]},{"l":"VB-A5","u":55,"label":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_427_473.jpg","cap":"","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6695,0.2481,0.8685,0.2824]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q23.jpg","cap":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 23","ov":[[0.1926,0.9506,0.4281,0.9973]]},{"src":"../img/zs_S07-s477.jpg","cap":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch) on another picture","where":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 477","ov":[[0.2383,0.9059,0.651,0.9883]]}],"q":[{"id":"zs_S07-s479~genitofemoral-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","B. Subcostal nerve","C. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","D. Iliohypogastric nerve","E. Obturator nerve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch).</b> The genitofemoral nerve pierces psoas major and runs down its front; its genital branch enters the deep inguinal ring, its femoral branch passes under the inguinal ligament.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of L1–L2 (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Genital branch: cremaster and anterior scrotum or labium majus; femoral branch: skin over the femoral triangle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ureter and gonadal vessels crossing in front, under the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of psoas major</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas major; the nerve lying on its front surface, not at its borders, is the genitofemoral nerve. Follow it down to where it divides above the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/zs_S07-s479.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 zSpace review","n":"2021 zSpace & bones review (Exam 2), slide 479"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9079,0.6604,0.9863,"",1]],"ar":0.48571,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q23~genitofemoral-nerve"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"VB-T1","h":"Front chest and neck strap muscles","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Serratus anterior muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Outer surfaces of the lateral parts of ribs 1-8, by fleshy digitations</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Anterior (costal) surface of the medial border of the scapula</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pectoralis major and minor in front; long thoracic nerve on its outer surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ribs 1-8 and the external intercostal muscles; subscapularis and scapula lie behind it</dd></dl><p>Serratus anterior arises by separate fleshy digitations from the outer surfaces of the upper 8-9 ribs on the lateral chest wall and passes posteriorly, deep to the scapula, to the anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula. Its slips lie on the outer surface of the ribs, and the long thoracic nerve runs vertically on its superficial surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the lateral chest wall below the axilla first; the muscle slips attached to the outer ribs that disappear deep to the scapula are serratus anterior.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_118_1347.jpg\" alt=\"Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:80.33%;top:61.38%;width:8.59%;height:7.56%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Serratus anterior muscle: Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.13</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Serratus anterior muscle</b>: arises from outer surfaces of the lateral parts of ribs 1-8, by fleshy digitations; deep to pectoralis major and minor in front; superficial to ribs 1-8 and the external intercostal muscles</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Serratus anterior muscle"]},{"l":"VB-T1","h":"Intercostal muscles and nerve","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>External intercostal muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower border of the rib above</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Upper border of the rib below; fibres run down and forward</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pectoral muscles, serratus anterior or latissimus dorsi, depending on the region</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle, whose fibres run at right angles to it</dd></dl><p>The external intercostal is the most superficial muscle within each intercostal space; its fibres pass from the lower border of one rib downward and forward (inferomedially, toward the sternum anteriorly) to the upper border of the rib below. It extends from the rib tubercles to the costochondral junctions, where it is replaced anteriorly by the external intercostal membrane. <i>Finding it:</i> Find two adjacent ribs on the lateral or posterior chest wall first; the first muscle layer between them, with fibres running down and forward, is the external intercostal.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Internal intercostal muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower border of the rib above, at the edge of the costal groove</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Upper border of the rib below; fibres run down and back</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External intercostal muscle (external intercostal membrane in front)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the innermost intercostal muscle</dd></dl><p>The internal intercostal lies deep to the external intercostal in each space; its fibres run downward and backward (inferoposteriorly), at right angles to the external fibres, from the sternum back to the rib angles, where it is replaced by the internal intercostal membrane. The intercostal vein, artery and nerve run on its deep surface, between it and the innermost intercostal. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the external intercostal first, then lift or cut it; the next muscle layer, with fibres crossing at right angles, is the internal intercostal (anteriorly near the sternum it is visible through the external intercostal membrane).</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_121_1354.jpg\" alt=\"Dissection of posterior aspect of thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:71.90%;top:83.97%;width:21.37%;height:4.34%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>External intercostal muscle: Dissection of posterior aspect of thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.17</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_180_138.jpg\" alt=\"Intercostal muscles\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.75%;top:70.56%;width:26.18%;height:4.16%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Internal intercostal muscle: Intercostal muscles <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.27</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Innermost intercostal muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surface of the rib above, near the costal groove</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inner surface of the rib below</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the internal intercostal muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</dd></dl><p>The innermost intercostal is the deepest layer of the intercostal space, best developed in the lateral part of the space; its fibres run in the same direction as the internal intercostal. It is separated from the internal intercostal by the intercostal vein, artery and nerve and lies directly against the endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the intercostal neurovascular bundle in the costal groove first (from inside the chest after removing pleura, or by deepening the space); the thin muscle on the pleural side of the bundle is the innermost intercostal.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Intercostal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of spinal nerves T1-T11</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Intercostal muscles, chest wall skin and parietal pleura; T7-T11 continue to the abdominal wall</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle; lowest in the bundle, below the intercostal vein and artery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal muscle (endothoracic fascia and pleura where it is absent)</dd></dl><p>Each intercostal nerve is the anterior ramus of a thoracic spinal nerve (T1-T11) and runs around the chest wall in the intercostal space, between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles, as the lowest structure of the bundle (vein above, artery, nerve below) near the costal groove. Medially it is connected to a sympathetic trunk ganglion by rami communicantes. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the costal groove on the inferior border of a rib from inside the chest first; the lowest, solid white cord of the bundle under it is the intercostal nerve.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>External intercostal muscle</b>: arises from lower border of the rib above; deep to pectoral muscles, serratus anterior or latissimus dorsi, depending on the region; superficial to internal intercostal muscle, whose fibres run at right angles to it</li><li><b>Internal intercostal muscle</b>: arises from lower border of the rib above, at the edge of the costal groove; deep to external intercostal muscle (external intercostal membrane in front); superficial to intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the innermost intercostal muscle</li><li><b>Innermost intercostal muscle</b>: arises from inner surface of the rib above, near the costal groove; deep to intercostal vein, artery and nerve, then the internal intercostal muscle; superficial to endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</li><li><b>Intercostal nerve</b>: comes from anterior rami of spinal nerves T1-T11; deep to internal intercostal muscle; superficial to innermost intercostal muscle (endothoracic fascia and pleura where it is absent)</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["External intercostal muscle","Internal intercostal muscle","Innermost intercostal muscle","Intercostal nerve"]},{"l":"VB-T1","h":"Internal thoracic artery and transversus thoracis","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Internal thoracic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>First part of the subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 1-6; ends as musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and pleura above; transversus thoracis separates it from pleura lower down</dd></dl><p>The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery and descends behind the costal cartilages about 1 cm lateral to the edge of the sternum, anterior to transversus thoracis and the parietal pleura, accompanied by internal thoracic veins. It gives anterior intercostal arteries and in the 6th intercostal space divides into the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn over the removed anterior chest wall and find the lateral edge of the sternum first; the vertical artery running parallel to it about a finger-width lateral, behind the cartilages, is the internal thoracic artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Musculophrenic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery (its lateral terminal branch, in the 6th intercostal space)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 7-9 and the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 7-9 along the costal margin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm, whose costal attachment it pierces along the costal margin</dd></dl><p>The musculophrenic artery is the lateral terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery, arising in the 6th intercostal space and running inferolaterally behind the costal cartilages along the costal margin. It gives anterior intercostal arteries to spaces 7-9 and supplies the diaphragm. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the internal thoracic artery and follow it down to its division at the 6th intercostal space; the branch that turns laterally along the costal margin is the musculophrenic artery.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries of thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.54%;top:27.14%;width:23.74%;height:4.89%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Internal thoracic artery: Arteries of thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_160_1444.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply and venous drainage of pericardium\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:2.47%;top:77.25%;width:23.17%;height:5.07%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Musculophrenic artery: Arterial supply and venous drainage of pericardium <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.47</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Transversus thoracis muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surface of the lower body of the sternum and the xiphoid process</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2-6</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal thoracic vessels, then the costal cartilages and internal intercostals</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</dd></dl><p>Transversus thoracis lies on the internal (posterior) surface of the anterior thoracic wall: its slips arise from the lower body of the sternum and xiphoid process and fan superolaterally to the inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2-6. The internal thoracic vessels run between it and the costal cartilages, so the muscle is deep (posterior) to them; it is continuous below with transversus abdominis. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn the removed anterior chest wall (sternum and cartilages) over and find the internal thoracic artery first; the thin muscle slips covering it and fanning out from the lower sternum are transversus thoracis.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Internal thoracic artery</b>: comes from first part of the subclavian artery; deep to costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum; superficial to endothoracic fascia and pleura above</li><li><b>Musculophrenic artery</b>: comes from internal thoracic artery (its lateral terminal branch, in the 6th intercostal space); deep to costal cartilages 7-9 along the costal margin; superficial to diaphragm, whose costal attachment it pierces along the costal margin</li><li><b>Transversus thoracis muscle</b>: arises from inner surface of the lower body of the sternum and the xiphoid process; deep to internal thoracic vessels, then the costal cartilages and internal intercostals; superficial to endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Internal thoracic artery","Musculophrenic artery","Transversus thoracis muscle"]},{"l":"VB-T2","h":"Lungs, lobes and fissures","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Horizontal fissure (right lung)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Middle lobe below it; meets the oblique fissure at the midaxillary line</dd></dl><p>The horizontal fissure is present in the right lung and absent from the left; it runs forward horizontally from the oblique fissure at about the midaxillary line along the level of the 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, separating the superior lobe from the middle lobe. It is often incomplete. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure on the lateral surface of the right lung first; the fissure that branches off it and runs forward horizontally is the horizontal fissure.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Oblique fissure</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; runs from the T2 spine to the 6th costal cartilage</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</dd></dl><p>The oblique fissure is present in both lungs; it runs from the posterior surface at about the level of the T2 spinous process downward and forward to about the 6th costal cartilage anteriorly, usually cutting completely through the lung to the hilum. It separates the inferior lobe (behind and below) from the superior lobe on the left and from the superior and middle lobes on the right. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the long cleft that starts high on the back of the lung and reaches the inferior border anteriorly; on the right confirm the horizontal fissure branches from it.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_141_1401.jpg\" alt=\"Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.24%;top:58.07%;width:13.64%;height:7.92%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Horizontal fissure (right lung): Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.34</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_264_259.jpg\" alt=\"Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:84.25%;top:34.87%;width:15.08%;height:4.71%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Oblique fissure: Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.103</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Horizontal fissure (right lung)</b>: deep to right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course; superficial to middle lobe below it</li><li><b>Oblique fissure</b>: deep to superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; superficial to inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Horizontal fissure (right lung)","Oblique fissure"]},{"l":"VB-T2","h":"Lung borders, cardiac notch and lingula","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Cardiac notch (left lung)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left 4th and 5th costal cartilages and the left costomediastinal recess</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Pericardium and heart (bare area), just left of the sternum</dd></dl><p>The cardiac notch is the deep concavity in the anterior border of the superior lobe of the left lung where the lung is displaced by the heart and pericardium; the lingula lies just below it. Through this gap the pericardium is less covered by lung, which is why the left costomediastinal recess is larger. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior border of the left lung first; the scooped-out gap in that border above the lingula is the cardiac notch.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lingula (of left lung)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior chest wall at the left 5th-6th costal cartilages</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left ventricle (left heart border) medially; inferior lobe behind across the oblique fissure</dd></dl><p>The lingula is the thin, tongue-shaped inferior projection of the superior lobe of the left lung, below the cardiac notch, lying over the left side of the heart. It is part of the superior lobe (no fissure separates it) and corresponds to the middle lobe of the right lung. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cardiac notch on the anterior border of the left lung first; the tongue of lung tissue curling below and in front of it is the lingula.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_138_1395.jpg\" alt=\"Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.05%;top:39.30%;width:13.17%;height:4.53%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Cardiac notch (left lung): Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.31</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Cardiac notch (left lung)</b>: deep to left 4th and 5th costal cartilages and the left costomediastinal recess; superficial to pericardium and heart (bare area), just left of the sternum</li><li><b>Lingula (of left lung)</b>: deep to anterior chest wall at the left 5th-6th costal cartilages; superficial to left ventricle (left heart border) medially</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Cardiac notch (left lung)","Lingula (of left lung)"]},{"l":"VB-T2","h":"Diaphragm: domes, central tendon and crura","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Central tendon of diaphragm</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium fused to it, with the heart's inferior surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Liver's upper surface; IVC (inferior vena cava) passes through its right part at T8</dd></dl><p>The central tendon is the pale, three-lobed aponeurosis forming the centre of the diaphragm dome, into which all the muscle fibres insert; its upper surface is fused to the fibrous pericardium. The caval opening for the IVC lies in it, to the right of the midline, at about T8. <i>Finding it:</i> Remove the heart in its pericardium and look at the floor it sat on first; the pale tendinous area fused to the pericardium is the central tendon.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right crus of diaphragm</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Anterior bodies of L1-L3 and their discs</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Central tendon; its fibres split around the esophageal hiatus at T10</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) and right suprarenal gland, with the caudate lobe in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Bodies of L1-L3, with the aorta passing behind the median arcuate ligament on its left</dd></dl><p>The right crus is the larger, longer musculotendinous attachment of the diaphragm to the anterior bodies of L1-L3 (the left crus reaches L1-L2); its fibres ascend and split to form the esophageal hiatus, and it joins the left crus over the front of the aorta by the median arcuate ligament. It lies to the right of and partly in front of the aorta, behind the IVC and caudate lobe. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal aorta at the level of the celiac trunk first; the thick muscle band rising on its right side from the vertebral bodies is the right crus.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_396_432.jpg\" alt=\"Diaphragm\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:65.78%;top:8.46%;width:13.54%;height:4.26%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Central tendon of diaphragm: Diaphragm <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.132</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_335_1778.jpg\" alt=\"Attachments, disposition, and features of the abdominal aspect of the diaphragm\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:89.86%;top:62.96%;width:5.59%;height:5.03%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left crus of diaphragm: Attachments, disposition, and features of the abdominal aspect of the diaphragm <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.91</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left crus of diaphragm</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Anterior bodies of L1-L2 and their disc</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Central tendon; joins the right crus by the median arcuate ligament over the aorta</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left gastric artery, then the lesser sac and stomach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Bodies of L1-L2, with the aorta to its right</dd></dl><p>The left crus is the smaller, shorter musculotendinous attachment of the diaphragm to the front of the L1-L2 vertebral bodies, ascending on the left side of the aorta. It joins the right crus in front of the aorta through the median arcuate ligament; the esophageal hiatus lies mainly in the right crus, to its right. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal aorta at the level of the celiac trunk; the muscle band rising from the vertebral bodies on its left side is the left crus.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Central tendon of diaphragm</b>: deep to fibrous pericardium fused to it, with the heart's inferior surface; superficial to liver's upper surface</li><li><b>Right crus of diaphragm</b>: arises from anterior bodies of L1-L3 and their discs; deep to IVC (inferior vena cava) and right suprarenal gland, with the caudate lobe in front; superficial to bodies of L1-L3, with the aorta passing behind the median arcuate ligament on its left</li><li><b>Left crus of diaphragm</b>: arises from anterior bodies of L1-L2 and their disc; deep to left suprarenal gland and left gastric artery, then the lesser sac and stomach; superficial to bodies of L1-L2, with the aorta to its right</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Central tendon of diaphragm","Right crus of diaphragm","Left crus of diaphragm"]},{"l":"VB-T2","h":"Larynx to the main bronchi","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right main (primary) bronchus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</dd></dl><p>The right main bronchus is shorter (about 2.5 cm), wider and more vertical than the left; it gives off the superior lobar bronchus before entering the hilum and continues as the bronchus intermedius. The arch of the azygos vein passes over it, and at the hilum it lies posterior to the right pulmonary artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina at the tracheal bifurcation first; the short, steep, wide bronchus going right, under the azygos arch, is the right main bronchus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left main (primary) bronchus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Aortic arch hooking over it; left pulmonary artery in front, then above</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus and descending aorta behind it</dd></dl><p>The left main bronchus is longer (about 5 cm), narrower and more horizontal than the right; it passes inferior to the aortic arch and anterior to the esophagus and descending aorta to reach the left hilum, where it lies below and behind the left pulmonary artery. It divides into superior and inferior lobar bronchi. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina first; the long bronchus running left under the aortic arch, below the left pulmonary artery, is the left main bronchus.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg\" alt=\"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.71%;top:57.71%;width:9.25%;height:4.21%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right main (primary) bronchus: Deep dissection of superior mediastinum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_190_1499.jpg\" alt=\"Relationships of structures in superior mediastinum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.91%;top:36.16%;width:18.88%;height:7.69%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Tracheobronchial lymph nodes: Relationships of structures in superior mediastinum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.65</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Tracheobronchial lymph nodes</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Bronchopulmonary (hilar) nodes, which drain the lungs</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Paratracheal nodes, then the bronchomediastinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Deep cardiac plexus, right pulmonary artery and aortic arch in front of the bifurcation</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus behind; the subcarinal group rests on the pericardium over the left atrium</dd></dl><p>Lymph nodes clustered around the tracheal bifurcation: superior groups in the angles between trachea and main bronchi and an inferior (subcarinal) group below the carina. They receive lymph from the bronchopulmonary nodes and drain to the paratracheal nodes and bronchomediastinal trunks. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina; the small, often carbon-blackened nodes in the angles beside it and below the bifurcation are tracheobronchial nodes.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right main (primary) bronchus</b>: deep to right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front; superficial to right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</li><li><b>Left main (primary) bronchus</b>: deep to aortic arch hooking over it; superficial to esophagus and descending aorta behind it</li><li><b>Tracheobronchial lymph nodes</b>: drains into paratracheal nodes, then the bronchomediastinal lymph trunks; deep to deep cardiac plexus, right pulmonary artery and aortic arch in front of the bifurcation; superficial to esophagus behind</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Right main (primary) bronchus","Left main (primary) bronchus","Tracheobronchial lymph nodes"]},{"l":"VB-T2","h":"Lung root at the hilum","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hilum of the lung</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels, in front of the root</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Vagus nerve and posterior pulmonary plexus behind the root; descending aorta behind the left</dd></dl><p>The hilum is the area on the mediastinal surface of each lung where the structures of the root (main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, lymphatics) enter and leave; it is outlined by the reflection of visceral into parietal pleura, which extends downward as the pulmonary ligament. On the right the bronchus is posterior and superior with the artery in front; on the left the artery is uppermost. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cut bronchus and pulmonary vessels on the medial surface of the removed lung first; the pleura-bounded area containing them is the hilum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right pulmonary artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ascending aorta and SVC (superior vena cava), in front of it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Carina and right main bronchus, directly behind it</dd></dl><p>The right pulmonary artery leaves the bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk in the concavity of the aortic arch and runs horizontally to the right, posterior to the ascending aorta and SVC and anterior to the right main bronchus, to reach the right hilum. It is longer than the left and at the hilum lies anterior to the bronchus and below the superior lobar (eparterial) bronchus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk first and follow its right branch; the vessel that disappears behind the ascending aorta and SVC toward the right hilum is the right pulmonary artery.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_145_1414.jpg\" alt=\"Pulmonary circulation\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:38.07%;top:1.22%;width:12.33%;height:4.71%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Hilum of the lung: Pulmonary circulation <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.37</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_202_165.jpg\" alt=\"Pulmonary vessels\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:12.58%;top:62.87%;width:15.30%;height:6.07%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pulmonary veins: Pulmonary vessels <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.45</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pulmonary veins</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Each lung, as superior and inferior veins per side; right middle lobe vein joins the superior</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve in front; right veins pass behind the SVC (superior vena cava) and right atrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Main bronchus behind them in the root; descending aorta behind the left veins</dd></dl><p>There are usually two pulmonary veins on each side (superior and inferior), forming the most anterior and most inferior structures of the root of the lung; they pass medially to open into the posterior wall of the left atrium. The inferior pulmonary vein lies at the lowest point of the hilum, just above the pulmonary ligament. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left atrium on the back of the heart (or the lowest part of the hilum) first; the vessels entering the left atrium from each side are the pulmonary veins.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Hilum of the lung</b>: deep to phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels, in front of the root; superficial to vagus nerve and posterior pulmonary plexus behind the root</li><li><b>Right pulmonary artery</b>: comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch; deep to ascending aorta and SVC (superior vena cava), in front of it; superficial to carina and right main bronchus, directly behind it</li><li><b>Pulmonary veins</b>: drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings; deep to phrenic nerve in front; superficial to main bronchus behind them in the root</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Hilum of the lung","Right pulmonary artery","Pulmonary veins"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Trachea and esophagus","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Trachea</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein and brachiocephalic trunk in front; aortic arch on its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus, directly behind its membranous posterior wall</dd></dl><p>The trachea descends in the midline (slightly to the right) from the neck through the superior mediastinum to the level of the sternal angle, where it divides into the right and left main bronchi; its wall has C-shaped cartilage rings open posteriorly, where the flat membranous wall rests on the esophagus. In the superior mediastinum it lies posterior to the left brachiocephalic vein and the origins of the arch branches, with the brachiocephalic trunk anterior and to its right and the arch of the aorta curving along its left side. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the esophagus on the front of the vertebral column first; the ringed airway directly in front of it is the trachea.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Esophagus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Trachea, then the left main bronchus, then pericardium over the left atrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebrae, thoracic duct and azygos vein; descending aorta behind it lower down</dd></dl><p>Muscular tube running from the neck through the superior mediastinum (posterior to the trachea, anterior to T1–T4, slightly left of midline) into the posterior mediastinum, where it lies posterior to the pericardium and left atrium, first to the right of the descending aorta and then anterior and to the left of it as it reaches the esophageal hiatus at T10. The vagal (esophageal) plexus covers it, and it is narrowed where the aortic arch, left main bronchus and diaphragm cross it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the trachea and look directly behind it; below the carina follow the same tube down behind the left atrium, with the descending aorta on its left.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_193_1505.jpg\" alt=\"Great vessels and nerves\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:20.51%;top:3.12%;width:7.87%;height:6.35%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Trachea: Great vessels and nerves <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.68</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg\" alt=\"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:69.74%;top:7.85%;width:10.90%;height:4.24%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Esophagus: Deep dissection of superior mediastinum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Trachea</b>: deep to left brachiocephalic vein and brachiocephalic trunk in front; superficial to esophagus, directly behind its membranous posterior wall</li><li><b>Esophagus</b>: deep to trachea, then the left main bronchus, then pericardium over the left atrium; superficial to thoracic vertebrae, thoracic duct and azygos vein</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Trachea","Esophagus"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Thymus and great veins","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Thymus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Manubrium and upper body of the sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein, aortic arch and upper fibrous pericardium, behind it</dd></dl><p>Lies in the anterior part of the superior mediastinum (extending down into the anterior mediastinum), posterior to the manubrium and anterior to the left brachiocephalic vein, aortic arch and upper pericardium. In adults it is mostly replaced by fat, so expect a pale fatty mass with little gland tissue rather than a discrete organ. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the posterior surface of the manubrium first; the tissue between it and the left brachiocephalic vein is the thymus (or its fatty remnant).</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left brachiocephalic vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left internal jugular and left subclavian veins, joining behind the left sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the right brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC (superior vena cava), behind the right 1st costal cartilage</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Manubrium and the thymus (or its fatty remnant), in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Origins of the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, behind it</dd></dl><p>Formed behind the left sternoclavicular joint by the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins, it runs obliquely to the right and downward behind the manubrium, anterior to the origins of the left subclavian, left common carotid and brachiocephalic trunk, to join the right brachiocephalic vein behind the right first costal cartilage. It is about twice as long as the right vein and thin-walled. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the manubrium and look deep to it; the first large vessel, crossing from upper left to lower right above the aortic arch, is the left brachiocephalic vein.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_191_1501.jpg\" alt=\"Dissections of superior mediastinum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:10.14%;top:61.15%;width:8.78%;height:4.57%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Thymus: Dissections of superior mediastinum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.66</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_160_1444.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply and venous drainage of pericardium\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.37%;top:8.67%;width:19.39%;height:8.50%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left brachiocephalic vein: Arterial supply and venous drainage of pericardium <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.47</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right brachiocephalic vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right internal jugular and right subclavian veins, joining behind the right sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the left brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC (superior vena cava), behind the right 1st costal cartilage</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right edge of the manubrium and 1st costal cartilage; right phrenic nerve on its lateral side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve behind it; brachiocephalic trunk behind and medial</dd></dl><p>Formed behind the right sternoclavicular joint by the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins, it descends almost vertically to the right of the brachiocephalic trunk and joins the left brachiocephalic vein behind the right first costal cartilage to form the SVC. The right phrenic nerve lies on its lateral side. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the SVC and follow it upward; above the right first costal cartilage it divides into the short vertical right and the long oblique left brachiocephalic veins.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Thymus</b>: deep to manubrium and upper body of the sternum; superficial to left brachiocephalic vein, aortic arch and upper fibrous pericardium, behind it</li><li><b>Left brachiocephalic vein</b>: drains into joins the right brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC (superior vena cava), behind the right 1st costal cartilage; deep to manubrium and the thymus (or its fatty remnant), in front; superficial to origins of the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, behind it</li><li><b>Right brachiocephalic vein</b>: drains into joins the left brachiocephalic vein to form the SVC (superior vena cava), behind the right 1st costal cartilage; deep to right edge of the manubrium and 1st costal cartilage; superficial to right vagus nerve behind it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Thymus","Left brachiocephalic vein","Right brachiocephalic vein"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Aortic arch and its branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Arch of aorta (aortic arch)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ascending aorta, at the sternal angle (behind the 2nd right sternocostal joint)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries; becomes descending aorta at T4</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left phrenic and vagus nerves and mediastinal pleura on its left; thymus remnant in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea and esophagus behind and right; it arches over the left main bronchus</dd></dl><p>The continuation of the ascending aorta, beginning and ending at the level of the sternal angle: it runs posteriorly and to the left, in front of and then to the left of the trachea and esophagus, over the root of the left lung, and gives the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries from its upper convexity. The ligamentum arteriosum attaches to its concave inferior surface, and the left phrenic and left vagus nerves cross its left side. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ascending aorta leaving the pericardium; follow it up to the sternal angle where it curves backward and to the left, then confirm the three branches rising from its top.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Brachiocephalic trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right subclavian and right common carotid arteries, behind the right sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein crossing in front; then the manubrium and thymus remnant</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea, crossing in front of it to lie on its right side</dd></dl><p>Arises from the beginning of the arch behind the manubrium, ascends to the right, first anterior to and then on the right side of the trachea, passing posterior to the left brachiocephalic vein, and divides behind the right sternoclavicular joint into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries. It usually has no branches before it divides. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left brachiocephalic vein and lift it; the large artery directly behind it, running from the arch up toward the right sternoclavicular joint, is the brachiocephalic trunk.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_196_1509.jpg\" alt=\"(Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:79.01%;top:41.35%;width:10.68%;height:3.99%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Arch of aorta (aortic arch): (Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_192_1503.jpg\" alt=\"Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:50.14%;top:13.21%;width:36.74%;height:5.54%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Brachiocephalic trunk: Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.67</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left common carotid artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its second branch, just left of the brachiocephalic trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Internal and external carotid arteries at the upper thyroid cartilage border (C4); no thoracic branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein, crossing in front of its origin; then the manubrium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea behind and medial, with the left subclavian artery behind and lateral</dd></dl><p>The second branch of the arch, arising just to the left of the brachiocephalic trunk; it ascends along the left side of the trachea, anterior and medial to the left subclavian artery, and passes behind the left sternoclavicular joint into the neck without branching in the thorax. The left vagus and left phrenic nerves descend in the gap between it and the left subclavian artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the aortic arch and count its branches from right to left: brachiocephalic trunk, then the left common carotid in the middle, then the left subclavian.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left subclavian artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its third and last branch, from the back of the arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Vertebral, internal thoracic, thyrocervical and costocervical branches; becomes axillary at the 1st rib</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left common carotid artery in front; left vagus and phrenic nerves descend between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left lung and pleura behind; trachea, esophagus and thoracic duct on its medial side</dd></dl><p>The third and last branch of the arch, arising from its posterior part; it ascends on the left side of the trachea and esophagus, lateral and posterior to the left common carotid, grooving the mediastinal surface of the left lung, then arches over the left lung apex to the first rib. The left vagus and left phrenic nerves descend between it and the left common carotid. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch and follow its last, most posterior branch; it is the vessel that curves over the apex of the left lung rather than going straight up the neck.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Arch of aorta (aortic arch)</b>: comes from ascending aorta, at the sternal angle (behind the 2nd right sternocostal joint); deep to left phrenic and vagus nerves and mediastinal pleura on its left; superficial to trachea and esophagus behind and right</li><li><b>Brachiocephalic trunk</b>: comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium; deep to left brachiocephalic vein crossing in front; superficial to trachea, crossing in front of it to lie on its right side</li><li><b>Left common carotid artery</b>: comes from arch of the aorta, its second branch, just left of the brachiocephalic trunk; deep to left brachiocephalic vein, crossing in front of its origin; superficial to trachea behind and medial, with the left subclavian artery behind and lateral</li><li><b>Left subclavian artery</b>: comes from arch of the aorta, its third and last branch, from the back of the arch; deep to left common carotid artery in front; superficial to left lung and pleura behind</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Arch of aorta (aortic arch)","Brachiocephalic trunk","Left common carotid artery","Left subclavian artery"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Phrenic and vagus nerves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Phrenic nerve (right/left)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd></dl><p>Each phrenic nerve (C3–C5) descends between the fibrous pericardium and the mediastinal pleura, anterior to the root of the lung and accompanied by the pericardiacophrenic artery and vein, to the diaphragm. The right one runs along the right brachiocephalic vein, SVC, right atrium and IVC; the left one crosses the left side of the aortic arch anterior to the left vagus and descends over the left ventricle. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pericardiacophrenic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery, near the thoracic inlet</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Pericardium, mediastinal pleura and diaphragm, running with the phrenic nerve</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root, beside the phrenic nerve</dd></dl><p>A slender branch of the internal thoracic artery that descends with the phrenic nerve, between the fibrous pericardium and the mediastinal pleura and anterior to the root of the lung, to the diaphragm. A pericardiacophrenic vein accompanies it, so nerve, artery and vein usually run together as one bundle. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the phrenic nerve on the side of the pericardium; the thin vessel hugging it is the pericardiacophrenic artery, with its vein.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg\" alt=\"Costal surfaces of lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:75.09%;top:22.87%;width:11.68%;height:4.39%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Phrenic nerve (right/left): Costal surfaces of lungs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_209_178.jpg\" alt=\"Phrenic nerves and pericardiacophrenic vessels\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:32.86%;top:80.53%;width:21.61%;height:6.14%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pericardiacophrenic artery: Phrenic nerves and pericardiacophrenic vessels <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.54</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right vagus nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the posterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front; azygos arch lateral to it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root</dd></dl><p>Enters the thorax anterior to the right subclavian artery (where it gives the right recurrent laryngeal nerve), then descends on the right side of the trachea, posterior to the right brachiocephalic vein and SVC and medial to the arch of the azygos vein, passes posterior to the root of the right lung and spreads onto the esophagus as the esophageal plexus. Below the plexus its fibres mainly form the posterior vagal trunk. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch of the azygos vein; the nerve passing medial to the arch on the side of the trachea and then behind the right lung root is the right vagus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left vagus nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the anterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root</dd></dl><p>Descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the left side of the aortic arch posterior to the left phrenic nerve, gives the left recurrent laryngeal nerve at the inferior border of the arch, then passes posterior to the root of the left lung onto the esophagus (esophageal plexus). Below the plexus its fibres mainly form the anterior vagal trunk. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left side of the aortic arch; of the two nerves crossing it, follow the posterior one: if it gives a branch that hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, it is the left vagus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right recurrent laryngeal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve, as it crosses in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid; laryngeal mucosa below the vocal folds</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right common carotid artery and right thyroid lobe, in front as it ascends</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Loops under the right subclavian artery, then lies in the tracheoesophageal groove</dd></dl><p>Branches from the right vagus as it crosses anterior to the right subclavian artery, loops inferior and posterior to that artery, and ascends in the groove between the trachea and esophagus to the larynx. Its loop is at the root of the neck, higher than the left nerve, and it has no relation to the aortic arch. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the right subclavian artery where the right vagus crosses in front of it; trace the branch that curves under the artery and turns upward.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid; laryngeal mucosa below the vocal folds</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum, around which it hooks; left thyroid lobe higher up</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Trachea and esophagus, in the left tracheoesophageal groove</dd></dl><p>Arises from the left vagus at the inferior border of the aortic arch, hooks under the arch immediately lateral (and posterior) to the ligamentum arteriosum, and ascends to the larynx in the groove between the trachea and esophagus. Its loop lies at sternal-angle level, lower than the loop of the right recurrent laryngeal nerve. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk bifurcation and the underside of the arch; the nerve curving under the arch just lateral to it is the left recurrent laryngeal.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Anterior vagal trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Esophageal plexus, mainly left vagus fibers, at the esophageal hiatus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior stomach (anterior gastric branches); hepatic branches in the lesser omentum to liver and pylorus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum over the esophagus; left lobe of the liver in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the abdominal esophagus</dd></dl><p>The anterior vagal trunk is the single nerve re-formed from the esophageal plexus on the anterior surface of the esophagus at the esophageal hiatus, carrying mainly left vagus fibres; it gives gastric branches to the anterior stomach and hepatic branches that run in the lesser omentum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal esophagus at the hiatus and look on its front surface for the nerve running onto the stomach.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Posterior vagal trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Esophageal plexus, mainly right vagus fibers, at the esophageal hiatus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior stomach (posterior gastric branches); celiac branch to the celiac plexus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Posterior surface of the abdominal esophagus</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right crus of the diaphragm, behind the esophagus</dd></dl><p>The posterior vagal trunk forms from the esophageal plexus on the posterior surface of the esophagus at the hiatus, carrying mainly right vagus fibres; it gives gastric branches to the posterior stomach and a celiac branch to the celiac plexus. <i>Finding it:</i> Pull the abdominal esophagus forward and look on its back surface, near the right crus.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Phrenic nerve (right/left)</b>: comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4); deep to mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it; superficial to fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root</li><li><b>Pericardiacophrenic artery</b>: comes from internal thoracic artery, near the thoracic inlet; deep to mediastinal pleura; superficial to fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root, beside the phrenic nerve</li><li><b>Right vagus nerve</b>: comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); deep to SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front; superficial to right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root</li><li><b>Left vagus nerve</b>: comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); deep to left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root; superficial to left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root</li><li><b>Right recurrent laryngeal nerve</b>: comes from right vagus nerve, as it crosses in front of the right subclavian artery; deep to right common carotid artery and right thyroid lobe, in front as it ascends; superficial to loops under the right subclavian artery, then lies in the tracheoesophageal groove</li><li><b>Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</b>: comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch; deep to aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum, around which it hooks; superficial to trachea and esophagus, in the left tracheoesophageal groove</li><li><b>Anterior vagal trunk</b>: comes from esophageal plexus, mainly left vagus fibers, at the esophageal hiatus; deep to peritoneum over the esophagus; superficial to anterior surface of the abdominal esophagus</li><li><b>Posterior vagal trunk</b>: comes from esophageal plexus, mainly right vagus fibers, at the esophageal hiatus; deep to posterior surface of the abdominal esophagus; superficial to right crus of the diaphragm, behind the esophagus</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Phrenic nerve (right/left)","Pericardiacophrenic artery","Right vagus nerve","Left vagus nerve","Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Anterior vagal trunk","Posterior vagal trunk"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Descending aorta and intercostal vessels","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Thoracic (descending) aorta</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior intercostal (3rd–11th), subcostal, bronchial and esophageal arteries; becomes abdominal aorta at T12</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lung root, pericardium over the left atrium, then the esophagus in front lower down</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the thoracic vertebral bodies; hemiazygos veins cross behind it</dd></dl><p>Continues the aortic arch from about T4, descends in the posterior mediastinum on the left side of the vertebral bodies, gradually moving toward the midline and lying posterior to the root of the left lung, the left atrium and (lower down) the esophagus, and leaves through the aortic hiatus at T12 as the abdominal aorta. It gives the 3rd–11th posterior intercostal and the subcostal arteries from its back, plus small bronchial, esophageal and pericardial branches. <i>Finding it:</i> Pull the left lung forward and look behind its root; the large artery on the left side of the vertebral column, with paired intercostal branches, is the thoracic aorta.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Posterior intercostal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Intercostal space, plus a dorsal branch from the back muscles and vertebral venous plexus</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein (right); hemiazygos or accessory hemiazygos (left); upper spaces to superior intercostal veins</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle; highest in the costal groove (vein, artery, nerve)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal muscle, then endothoracic fascia and costal pleura</dd></dl><p>Each posterior intercostal vein runs in the costal groove as the highest member of the intercostal bundle (vein, artery, nerve from above down) and drains medially into the azygos vein on the right and the hemiazygos or accessory hemiazygos vein on the left; the upper few drain into the superior intercostal veins. Follow it medially to a longitudinal vein on the vertebral bodies, not to the aorta. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein (right) or hemiazygos vein (left) on the vertebral bodies first; the segmental veins entering it from each intercostal space are the posterior intercostal veins.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg\" alt=\"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.79%;top:70.36%;width:14.79%;height:6.36%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Thoracic (descending) aorta: Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_125_1361.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.93%;top:42.35%;width:19.37%;height:4.74%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Posterior intercostal vein: Veins of thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.20</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Thoracic (descending) aorta</b>: comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle); deep to left lung root, pericardium over the left atrium, then the esophagus in front lower down; superficial to left side of the thoracic vertebral bodies</li><li><b>Posterior intercostal vein</b>: drains into azygos vein (right); deep to internal intercostal muscle; superficial to innermost intercostal muscle, then endothoracic fascia and costal pleura</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Thoracic (descending) aorta","Posterior intercostal vein"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Azygos system and thoracic duct","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Azygos vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right ascending lumbar and subcostal veins; then right posterior intercostals, hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Esophagus and right lung root in front; costal pleura on its lateral side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right side of the thoracic vertebral bodies, crossing the right posterior intercostal arteries</dd></dl><p>Ascends through the posterior mediastinum on the right side of the bodies of the lower eight thoracic vertebrae, to the right of the thoracic duct and aorta and posterior to the esophagus and right lung root, receiving the right posterior intercostal veins and the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins. At about T4 it arches forward over the root of the right lung to enter the posterior side of the SVC. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the SVC first, then the vein that arches over the right main bronchus into its back; follow that vein down along the right side of the vertebral bodies.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hemiazygos vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left ascending lumbar and left subcostal veins, plus the lowest left posterior intercostals</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies</dd></dl><p>Formed on the left from the left ascending lumbar and subcostal veins, it ascends on the left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies, receiving the lowest left posterior intercostal veins (about 9th–11th), and usually at about T9 crosses to the right, posterior to the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct, to join the azygos vein. Its crossing level and its connection with the accessory hemiazygos vary. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein on the right; look for a vein joining it from the left across the front of the lower vertebral bodies behind the aorta, and follow it back to the left side.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg\" alt=\"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.72%;top:39.00%;width:12.16%;height:4.03%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Azygos vein: Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_200_1515.jpg\" alt=\"Azygos system of veins\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:60.63%;top:45.33%;width:25.91%;height:4.90%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Hemiazygos vein: Azygos system of veins <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.74</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Accessory hemiazygos vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left posterior intercostal veins of about the 4th–8th spaces; sometimes left bronchial veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta and thoracic duct</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies</dd></dl><p>Begins at about the fourth or fifth left intercostal space and descends on the left side of the vertebral bodies from about T5 to T8, receiving the left posterior intercostal veins of about the 4th–8th spaces (sometimes the left bronchial veins), then crosses posterior to the aorta and thoracic duct at about T7–T8 to the azygos vein. It may join the hemiazygos before crossing and may connect above with the left superior intercostal vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein, then look for veins joining it from the left: the higher crossing (about T7–T8), fed from the upper left intercostal spaces, is the accessory hemiazygos.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Thoracic duct</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Cisterna chyli at about L1–L2, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Esophagus in front; azygos vein on its right and descending aorta on its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</dd></dl><p>Begins at the cisterna chyli in the abdomen, enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus and ascends on the front of the vertebral bodies posterior to the esophagus, between the azygos vein (right) and descending aorta (left); at about T4–T6 it crosses to the left and ascends along the left side of the esophagus into the neck to end at the left venous angle. It is thin-walled, often collapsed and beaded by its valves. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein and the descending aorta; gently separate the tissue between them behind the esophagus to find the thin whitish duct.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Azygos vein</b>: drains into back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4; deep to esophagus and right lung root in front; superficial to right side of the thoracic vertebral bodies, crossing the right posterior intercostal arteries</li><li><b>Hemiazygos vein</b>: drains into azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9; deep to descending thoracic aorta in front; superficial to left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies</li><li><b>Accessory hemiazygos vein</b>: drains into azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos); deep to descending thoracic aorta in front; superficial to left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies</li><li><b>Thoracic duct</b>: drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins); deep to esophagus in front; superficial to thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Azygos vein","Hemiazygos vein","Accessory hemiazygos vein","Thoracic duct"]},{"l":"VB-T3","h":"Sympathetic trunk and splanchnic nerves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Sympathetic trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gray rami to every spinal nerve; cardiac, pulmonary and splanchnic branches to the viscera</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura (thorax); IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right lumbar trunk, aorta beside the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Heads of the ribs in the thorax; sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies in the abdomen</dd></dl><p>The sympathetic trunk is a paired vertical chain of ganglia linked by nerve segments, lying behind the costal pleura on the heads of the upper ribs (and lower down on the sides of the vertebral bodies), joined to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes; in the abdomen it passes posterior to the medial arcuate ligament and descends on the anterolateral lumbar vertebral bodies along the medial border of psoas major. <i>Finding it:</i> Strip the costal pleura from the posterior thoracic wall beside the vertebral bodies and look for the cord running vertically over the rib heads.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Celiac ganglion (foregut), after piercing the crus; some fibers reach the suprarenal medulla</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies, medial to the sympathetic trunk</dd></dl><p>The greater splanchnic nerve is formed by roots from the sympathetic ganglia of about T5-T9 (or T10) that leave the trunk medially and unite into a nerve descending obliquely over the sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies; it pierces the crus of the diaphragm and ends in the celiac ganglion, carrying preganglionic sympathetic fibres. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the sympathetic trunk on the rib heads, then follow the medial branches from its middle ganglia as they join into one nerve heading down onto the vertebral bodies.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg\" alt=\"Innervation of small intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.58%;top:5.20%;width:20.43%;height:4.12%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Sympathetic trunk: Innervation of small intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_423_467.jpg\" alt=\"Nerve fibers passing through the abdominal prevertebral plexus and ganglia\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:66.61%;top:66.78%;width:20.38%;height:3.75%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: Nerve fibers passing through the abdominal prevertebral plexus and ganglia <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.158</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglia T10–T11</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Aorticorenal ganglion (midgut and kidney), after piercing the crus of the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sides of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies, lateral to the greater splanchnic nerve</dd></dl><p>The lesser splanchnic nerve arises from the T10-T11 sympathetic ganglia, descends usually lateral to the greater splanchnic nerve, pierces the crus of the diaphragm and ends in the aorticorenal ganglion, carrying preganglionic sympathetic fibres. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the sympathetic trunk down to the T10-T11 ganglia and look for the small nerve leaving them medially, below the greater splanchnic.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglion T12 (small and inconstant)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Renal plexus (kidney)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Side of the T12 vertebral body, beside the sympathetic trunk</dd></dl><p>The least splanchnic nerve is a small, inconstant branch from the T12 sympathetic ganglion that passes into the abdomen to end in the renal plexus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the lowest thoracic ganglion (T12) on the sympathetic trunk and look for a fine medial branch.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Sympathetic trunk</b>: comes from preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes; deep to costal pleura (thorax); superficial to heads of the ribs in the thorax</li><li><b>Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b>: comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially); deep to costal pleura and endothoracic fascia; superficial to sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies, medial to the sympathetic trunk</li><li><b>Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b>: comes from sympathetic ganglia T10–T11; deep to costal pleura and endothoracic fascia; superficial to sides of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies, lateral to the greater splanchnic nerve</li><li><b>Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b>: comes from sympathetic ganglion T12 (small and inconstant); deep to costal pleura and endothoracic fascia; superficial to side of the T12 vertebral body, beside the sympathetic trunk</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"7","ks":["Sympathetic trunk","Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Heart in the chest","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Parietal serous pericardium, fused to its inner surface; pericardial cavity beyond</dd></dl><p>The tough outer sac that encloses the heart and the roots of the great vessels, fused below to the central tendon of the diaphragm and blending above with the outer coat of the aorta, pulmonary trunk and superior vena cava. The phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels run on its lateral surface, under the mediastinal pleura. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the phrenic nerve on the side of the heart; the membrane it lies on, deep to the mediastinal pleura, is the fibrous pericardium.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg\" alt=\"Costal surfaces of lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:81.33%;top:54.67%;width:16.05%;height:4.42%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pericardium (fibrous pericardium): Costal surfaces of lungs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)</b>: deep to mediastinal pleura, with the phrenic nerve and pericardiacophrenic vessels between them; superficial to parietal serous pericardium, fused to its inner surface</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Pericardium (fibrous pericardium)"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Atria and auricles","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right atrium</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium with the right phrenic nerve; right lung edge and right 3rd–6th costal cartilages</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right pulmonary veins and left atrium behind it; IVC (inferior vena cava) enters below</dd></dl><p>The chamber forming the right border of the heart and the right part of its anterior surface, receiving the superior vena cava above, the inferior vena cava below and the coronary sinus. Its auricle overlaps the root of the ascending aorta, and inside it has pectinate muscles anteriorly and the smooth sinus venarum posteriorly. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the superior or inferior vena cava to the heart; the chamber they open into is the right atrium.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left atrium</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk in front (transverse pericardial sinus between)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Esophagus directly behind it (oblique pericardial sinus between), then the descending aorta</dd></dl><p>The chamber forming most of the base (posterior surface) of the heart, receiving the four pulmonary veins, with its auricle projecting forward along the left side of the pulmonary trunk. It lies directly anterior to the esophagus, with the oblique pericardial sinus between them, and its wall is smooth except inside the auricle. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary veins at the lung roots and follow them medially to the chamber they enter on the back of the heart.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg\" alt=\"Interior of right ventricle of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.68%;top:38.10%;width:11.30%;height:4.85%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right atrium: Interior of right ventricle of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_171_1463.jpg\" alt=\"Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:74.21%;top:26.19%;width:11.38%;height:4.52%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left atrium: Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.56</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right auricle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the sternum and anterior edge of the right lung</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Root of the ascending aorta; right coronary artery runs in the groove beneath it</dd></dl><p>A broad triangular muscular pouch projecting from the upper anterior part of the right atrium and overlapping the root of the ascending aorta. Its inner surface is ridged by pectinate muscles, and the right coronary artery emerges just below it into the coronary sulcus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the ascending aorta; the flap of right atrium lying against its right-anterior side is the right auricle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left auricle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium with the left phrenic nerve, then the left lung's anterior edge</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left side of the pulmonary trunk root, over the left coronary and circumflex arteries</dd></dl><p>A narrow, hooked muscular pouch extending forward from the upper left part of the left atrium, lying against the left side of the pulmonary trunk root and forming a small part of the left border of the heart. The left coronary artery passes out between it and the pulmonary trunk, and its interior holds the left atrium's pectinate muscles. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk; the small flap of atrium curling around its left side is the left auricle.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right atrium</b>: deep to pericardium with the right phrenic nerve; superficial to right pulmonary veins and left atrium behind it</li><li><b>Left atrium</b>: deep to ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk in front (transverse pericardial sinus between); superficial to esophagus directly behind it (oblique pericardial sinus between), then the descending aorta</li><li><b>Right auricle</b>: deep to pericardium, then the sternum and anterior edge of the right lung; superficial to root of the ascending aorta</li><li><b>Left auricle</b>: deep to pericardium with the left phrenic nerve, then the left lung's anterior edge; superficial to left side of the pulmonary trunk root, over the left coronary and circumflex arteries</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Right atrium","Left atrium","Right auricle","Left auricle"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Ventricles and interventricular septum","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right ventricle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the body of the sternum and left costal cartilages</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left ventricle (across the septum) behind; central tendon of the diaphragm below</dd></dl><p>The chamber forming most of the sternocostal (anterior) surface and the inferior border of the heart, lying between the right atrium and the pulmonary trunk. Its wall is thinner than the left ventricle's, and inside it has coarse trabeculae carneae, the septomarginal trabecula and a smooth conus arteriosus leading up to the pulmonary valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and follow it down to the chamber it leaves; that anterior chamber is the right ventricle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left ventricle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right ventricle in front; pericardium, left phrenic nerve and left lung on its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm under its diaphragmatic surface; left lung against its left surface</dd></dl><p>The chamber forming the apex, most of the left surface and left border, and most of the diaphragmatic surface of the heart. Its wall is usually two to three times thicker than the right ventricle's, its cavity is round in cross section, and it holds two large papillary muscles and leads to the aortic valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the apex and the anterior interventricular groove; the thick-walled chamber to the left of the groove is the left ventricle.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_166_1454.jpg\" alt=\"Shape, orientation, surfaces, and borders of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.17%;top:87.67%;width:16.71%;height:4.66%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right ventricle: Shape, orientation, surfaces, and borders of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.52</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_171_1463.jpg\" alt=\"Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:74.44%;top:52.64%;width:13.32%;height:4.55%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left ventricle: Interior of left atrium and ventricle of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.56</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right ventricle</b>: deep to pericardium, then the body of the sternum and left costal cartilages; superficial to left ventricle (across the septum) behind</li><li><b>Left ventricle</b>: deep to right ventricle in front; superficial to diaphragm under its diaphragmatic surface</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Great vessels","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior vena cava</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right and left common iliac veins at L5, behind the right common iliac artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium, lower part, after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>3rd part of duodenum, head of pancreas, portal vein (epiploic foramen), then bare area of liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lumbar vertebral bodies, right psoas and right crus; right renal artery passes behind it</dd></dl><p>Formed at L5 by the two common iliac veins posterior to the right common iliac artery, it ascends on the right side of the lumbar vertebral bodies to the right of the aorta, passes behind the duodenum, head of pancreas and liver, and pierces the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8. Its short thoracic part enters the lower posterior right atrium; tributaries include the renal veins, the right gonadal and right suprarenal veins, and the hepatic veins just below the diaphragm. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta on the vertebral body; the larger, flatter vein immediately to its right is the IVC. Higher up, follow it into the groove on the back of the liver to the right of the caudate lobe and on through the diaphragm into the right atrium.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pulmonary trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left pulmonary arteries, dividing under the aortic arch at sternal-angle level</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage; left auricle curls around its left side</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ascending aorta to its right; transverse pericardial sinus and left atrium behind</dd></dl><p>The thick vessel leaving the conus arteriosus of the right ventricle, starting anterior and to the left of the ascending aorta and passing posteriorly along its left side. It divides in the concavity of the aortic arch into the right and left pulmonary arteries, with the ligamentum arteriosum attached near the fork. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the most anterior great vessel leaving the front of the heart, to the left of the ascending aorta; follow it up to where it forks under the arch.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg\" alt=\"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.69%;top:1.21%;width:16.69%;height:4.25%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior vena cava: Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg\" alt=\"Interior of right ventricle of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.80%;top:8.70%;width:14.69%;height:4.88%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pulmonary trunk: Interior of right ventricle of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ascending aorta</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left ventricle, through the aortic valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left coronary arteries (its only branches); continues as the arch at the sternal angle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right auricle and pulmonary trunk overlap its root; then pericardium and sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse pericardial sinus, then the right pulmonary artery and left atrium behind it</dd></dl><p>The first part of the aorta, about 5 cm long and inside the pericardium, running superiorly and to the right from the aortic valve to become the arch at the level of the sternal angle. Its branches are the right and left coronary arteries, which arise from the aortic sinuses just above the valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk leaving the front of the heart; the larger vessel just to its right, between it and the superior vena cava, is the ascending aorta.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Inferior vena cava</b>: drains into right atrium, lower part, after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8; deep to 3rd part of duodenum, head of pancreas, portal vein (epiploic foramen), then bare area of liver; superficial to lumbar vertebral bodies, right psoas and right crus</li><li><b>Pulmonary trunk</b>: comes from right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve; deep to pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage; superficial to ascending aorta to its right</li><li><b>Ascending aorta</b>: comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve; deep to right auricle and pulmonary trunk overlap its root; superficial to transverse pericardial sinus, then the right pulmonary artery and left atrium behind it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Inferior vena cava","Pulmonary trunk","Ascending aorta"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Right coronary artery and branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right coronary artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right aortic sinus of the ascending aorta</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right atrium and ventricle, usually the SA (sinoatrial) and AV (atrioventricular) nodes, and posterior interventricular artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right auricle at its origin; epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; anterior cardiac veins cross it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the right atrium and right ventricle</dd></dl><p>Arises from the right aortic sinus, passes between the right auricle and the pulmonary trunk, and runs in the right part of the coronary sulcus around the inferior border onto the diaphragmatic surface. Its usual branches are the sinoatrial nodal artery, right marginal artery, atrioventricular nodal artery and, in most hearts, the posterior interventricular artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the right auricle and lift it; the artery in the fat of the groove beneath it, coming from the aortic root, is the right coronary artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right coronary artery near its origin (about 60%); otherwise the circumflex artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>SA (sinoatrial) node, where the SVC (superior vena cava) joins the right atrium</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; right auricle near its origin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right atrial wall between right auricle and aorta, curving behind the SVC (superior vena cava)</dd></dl><p>Usually (about 60% of hearts) a branch of the first part of the right coronary artery, otherwise of the circumflex artery, that ascends over the right atrium toward the opening of the superior vena cava. It supplies the sinoatrial node where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the first centimetre of the right coronary artery; the small branch climbing up the right atrium toward the superior vena cava is the sinoatrial nodal artery.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_175_1470.jpg\" alt=\"Coronary arteries\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:75.28%;top:57.89%;width:10.88%;height:4.96%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right coronary artery: Coronary arteries <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.59</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_187_1496.jpg\" alt=\"\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:25.55%;top:1.99%;width:15.31%;height:9.78%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery:  <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right marginal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right coronary artery, at the inferior (acute) border of the heart</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right ventricle, along the inferior border toward the apex</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium, with the small cardiac vein beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right ventricle surface along the acute (inferior) margin</dd></dl><p>A branch of the right coronary artery that leaves the coronary sulcus and runs along the inferior (acute) border of the heart toward, but usually not reaching, the apex. The small cardiac vein usually accompanies it. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the right coronary artery to the sharp inferior edge of the heart; the branch turning along that edge toward the apex is the right marginal artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Posterior interventricular artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Usually the right coronary artery at the crux (right-dominant); circumflex in left-dominant hearts</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior third of the interventricular septum (septal branches) and adjacent ventricular walls</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat, with the middle cardiac vein beside it; diaphragm below the heart</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular sulcus, over the posterior interventricular septum</dd></dl><p>Usually the terminal branch of the right coronary artery (right-dominant hearts), arising at the crux and running in the posterior interventricular sulcus toward the apex; in left-dominant hearts it comes from the circumflex. It gives septal branches to the posterior part of the interventricular septum and travels with the middle cardiac vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn the heart up onto its apex; follow the right coronary artery to the crux, then follow its branch down the posterior interventricular groove.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right coronary artery</b>: comes from right aortic sinus of the ascending aorta; deep to right auricle at its origin; superficial to right coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the right atrium and right ventricle</li><li><b>Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery</b>: comes from right coronary artery near its origin (about 60%); deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to right atrial wall between right auricle and aorta, curving behind the SVC (superior vena cava)</li><li><b>Right marginal artery</b>: comes from right coronary artery, at the inferior (acute) border of the heart; deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium, with the small cardiac vein beside it; superficial to right ventricle surface along the acute (inferior) margin</li><li><b>Posterior interventricular artery</b>: comes from usually the right coronary artery at the crux (right-dominant); deep to epicardial fat, with the middle cardiac vein beside it; superficial to posterior interventricular sulcus, over the posterior interventricular septum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Right coronary artery","Sinoatrial (SA) nodal artery","Right marginal artery","Posterior interventricular artery"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Left coronary artery and branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left coronary artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left aortic sinus of the ascending aorta</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular (LAD, left anterior descending) and circumflex arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk in front and left auricle to its left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left atrial wall, running behind the pulmonary trunk to the coronary sulcus</dd></dl><p>Arises from the left aortic sinus and passes between the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle toward the left part of the coronary sulcus. After a short course it divides into the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and lift the left auricle; the short artery coming from the aortic root between them is the left coronary artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior two-thirds of septum, front of both ventricles and apex, via diagonal and septal branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; great cardiac vein runs beside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum</dd></dl><p>A terminal branch of the left coronary artery that runs in the anterior interventricular sulcus from the left side of the pulmonary trunk down to the apex, often turning around it to meet the posterior interventricular artery. It gives diagonal branches onto the left ventricle and septal branches into the anterior part of the interventricular septum, and it runs with the great cardiac vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle; trace the left coronary artery from between them to the branch that turns straight down the front of the heart.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_175_1470.jpg\" alt=\"Coronary arteries\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.99%;top:30.59%;width:15.53%;height:4.95%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left coronary artery: Coronary arteries <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.59</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_176_1474.jpg\" alt=\"Variations in distribution of coronary arteries\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:3.01%;top:72.10%;width:34.87%;height:16.46%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Anterior interventricular artery (LAD): Variations in distribution of coronary arteries <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.60</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Circumflex artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left atrium and lateral and posterior left ventricle; left marginal artery; SA (sinoatrial) nodal artery in about 40%</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left auricle at its start; great cardiac vein and coronary sinus beside it in the groove</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the left atrium and left ventricle</dd></dl><p>A terminal branch of the left coronary artery that runs in the left part of the coronary sulcus, around the left border of the heart onto its posterior surface. It gives the left marginal artery, usually ends before reaching the crux, and runs near the great cardiac vein and coronary sinus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left coronary artery between the pulmonary trunk and left auricle; follow the branch that turns left under the auricle into the atrioventricular groove.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left marginal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Circumflex artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left ventricle, along the left (obtuse) border toward the apex</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left ventricle surface along the obtuse (left) margin</dd></dl><p>A branch of the circumflex artery that descends along the left (obtuse) border of the heart over the left ventricle toward the apex. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the circumflex artery around the left border of the heart; the branch leaving it to run down that border is the left marginal artery.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Left coronary artery</b>: comes from left aortic sinus of the ascending aorta; deep to pulmonary trunk in front and left auricle to its left; superficial to left atrial wall, running behind the pulmonary trunk to the coronary sulcus</li><li><b>Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)</b>: comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches; deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum</li><li><b>Circumflex artery</b>: comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches; deep to left auricle at its start; superficial to left coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, between the left atrium and left ventricle</li><li><b>Left marginal artery</b>: comes from circumflex artery; deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to left ventricle surface along the obtuse (left) margin</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Left coronary artery","Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","Circumflex artery","Left marginal artery"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Cardiac veins and coronary sinus","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Great cardiac vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Apex and front of both ventricles, ascending beside the LAD (left anterior descending) artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus, where the oblique vein of the left atrium joins on the back of the heart</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; left auricle where it turns left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, then the left coronary sulcus</dd></dl><p>Begins near the apex and ascends in the anterior interventricular sulcus beside the anterior interventricular artery, then turns left in the coronary sulcus around the left border with the circumflex artery. On the posterior surface it becomes the coronary sinus, at about the point where the oblique vein of the left atrium joins. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior interventricular artery; the thin-walled vein running beside it up the groove and then turning left is the great cardiac vein.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Middle cardiac vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Diaphragmatic surfaces of both ventricles, from near the apex</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus, near its right end</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; diaphragm below the heart</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular sulcus, beside the posterior interventricular artery</dd></dl><p>Begins near the apex and ascends in the posterior interventricular sulcus beside the posterior interventricular artery, ending in the coronary sinus near its right end. It is the vein of the groove between the two ventricles on the diaphragmatic surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the coronary sinus on the back of the heart; the vein entering it from the groove that runs down toward the apex is the middle cardiac vein.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_232_215.jpg\" alt=\"Major cardiac veins\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:74.45%;top:68.85%;width:24.85%;height:4.48%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Great cardiac vein: Major cardiac veins <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.75</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_177_1477.jpg\" alt=\"Cardiac veins\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:88.45%;top:80.80%;width:10.29%;height:10.34%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Middle cardiac vein: Cardiac veins <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.61</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Small cardiac vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Inferior (acute) margin of the right ventricle, running with the right marginal artery</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus near its right end (sometimes the middle cardiac vein)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right coronary sulcus posteriorly, after running along the acute margin</dd></dl><p>Runs with the right marginal artery along the inferior border of the heart and then in the right part of the coronary sulcus posteriorly, usually ending in the coronary sinus (sometimes in the middle cardiac vein). It is thin and can be hard to find. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the right marginal artery along the inferior border; the vein beside it, curving back into the right coronary groove toward the coronary sinus, is the small cardiac vein.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Anterior cardiac veins</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium directly, not the coronary sinus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the right ventricle, then over the right coronary artery in its sulcus</dd></dl><p>Two to four small veins on the anterior surface of the right ventricle that pass upward across the right coronary sulcus, usually over the right coronary artery, and usually open directly into the right atrium. Unlike the great, middle and small cardiac veins, they do not drain into the coronary sinus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the right coronary artery in the right coronary groove; the small veins crossing over it from the front of the right ventricle into the right atrium are anterior cardiac veins.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Great cardiac vein</b>: drains into coronary sinus, where the oblique vein of the left atrium joins on the back of the heart; deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to anterior interventricular sulcus, then the left coronary sulcus</li><li><b>Middle cardiac vein</b>: drains into coronary sinus, near its right end; deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to posterior interventricular sulcus, beside the posterior interventricular artery</li><li><b>Small cardiac vein</b>: drains into coronary sinus near its right end (sometimes the middle cardiac vein); deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to right coronary sulcus posteriorly, after running along the acute margin</li><li><b>Anterior cardiac veins</b>: drains into right atrium directly, not the coronary sinus; deep to epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; superficial to front of the right ventricle, then over the right coronary artery in its sulcus</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"7","ks":["Great cardiac vein","Middle cardiac vein","Small cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Inside the right atrium","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Opening of the coronary sinus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Thin valve of the coronary sinus, guarding its edge</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower septal wall of the right atrium, between the IVC (inferior vena cava) opening and tricuspid orifice</dd></dl><p>The orifice in the right atrium through which the coronary sinus empties, lying between the opening of the inferior vena cava and the right atrioventricular (tricuspid) orifice, often guarded by a thin valve fold. The atrioventricular node lies in the septum near it. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium, find the inferior vena cava opening, then move toward the tricuspid valve; the smaller hole between them is the coronary sinus opening.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Atrioventricular (AV) node</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right atrial endocardium in the triangle of Koch, near the coronary sinus opening</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower interatrial septum on the right atrial side, above the septal tricuspid cusp</dd></dl><p>Conducting tissue in the lower part of the interatrial septum on the right atrial side, near the opening of the coronary sinus and just above the attachment of the septal cusp of the tricuspid valve. It continues as the atrioventricular bundle into the top of the interventricular septum; 3-D models show it, but it usually cannot be seen on a dissected heart. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the coronary sinus opening in the right atrium; the node sits in the septum next to it, where the conduction pathway enters the top of the interventricular septum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_168_1458.jpg\" alt=\"Right atrium of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:77.52%;top:47.76%;width:21.97%;height:4.76%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Opening of the coronary sinus: Right atrium of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.53</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_178_1478.jpg\" alt=\"Conducting system of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:78.15%;top:17.83%;width:20.72%;height:9.26%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Atrioventricular (AV) node: Conducting system of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.62</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pectinate muscles</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis</dd></dl><p>Parallel muscular ridges on the inner surface of the anterior wall of the right atrium and inside both auricles, running forward from the crista terminalis. In the left atrium they are confined to the auricle, and the rest of that chamber is smooth. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the crista terminalis; the ridges running forward from it across the anterior wall and into the auricle are the pectinate muscles.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Opening of the coronary sinus</b>: deep to thin valve of the coronary sinus, guarding its edge; superficial to lower septal wall of the right atrium, between the IVC (inferior vena cava) opening and tricuspid orifice</li><li><b>Atrioventricular (AV) node</b>: deep to right atrial endocardium in the triangle of Koch, near the coronary sinus opening; superficial to lower interatrial septum on the right atrial side, above the septal tricuspid cusp</li><li><b>Pectinate muscles</b>: deep to endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity; superficial to anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"8","ks":["Opening of the coronary sinus","Atrioventricular (AV) node","Pectinate muscles"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Inside the ventricles","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Trabeculae carneae</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts)</dd></dl><p>Irregular muscular ridges, bridges and columns on the inner walls of both ventricles, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left. The outflow parts (conus arteriosus and aortic vestibule) are smooth. <i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle; the rough, interlacing muscle on the walls below the valves, apart from the large papillary muscles, is the trabeculae carneae.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd></dl><p>A curved muscular band in the right ventricle running from the lower part of the interventricular septum across the cavity to the base of the anterior papillary muscle. It carries the right bundle branch of the conducting system; a comparable band is usually absent in the left ventricle. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg\" alt=\"Internal view of the right ventricle\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:44.11%;top:94.98%;width:18.09%;height:4.00%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Trabeculae carneae: Internal view of the right ventricle <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_223_199.jpg\" alt=\"Internal view of the left ventricle\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.62%;top:57.85%;width:16.37%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Papillary muscle: Internal view of the left ventricle <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.67</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Papillary muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner ventricular wall (right: anterior, posterior, septal; left: anterior, posterior)</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Chordae tendineae, to the cusps of the tricuspid or mitral valve</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Endocardium; chordae tendineae attach at its tip</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ventricular wall muscle, from which it projects into the cavity</dd></dl><p>Conical muscular projections from the ventricular wall whose tips give attachment to the chordae tendineae of the atrioventricular cusps. The right ventricle usually has anterior (largest), posterior and septal papillary muscles; the left ventricle has two large ones, anterior and posterior. <i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle and follow the chordae tendineae down from a valve cusp; the muscle mound they attach to is a papillary muscle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Chordae tendineae</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Fibrous collagen cords covered by endocardium</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Papillary muscle tips to the free edges and ventricular surfaces of tricuspid and mitral cusps</dd></dl><p>Thin fibrous cords running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the free edges and ventricular surfaces of the tricuspid and mitral cusps, present in both ventricles. The semilunar (pulmonary and aortic) valves have no chordae. <i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle, find a cusp of the atrioventricular valve, and follow the threads hanging from its edge down to a papillary muscle.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Trabeculae carneae</b>: deep to endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity; superficial to inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts)</li><li><b>Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)</b>: arises from lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side; deep to endocardium; superficial to free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</li><li><b>Papillary muscle</b>: arises from inner ventricular wall (right: anterior, posterior, septal; deep to endocardium; superficial to ventricular wall muscle, from which it projects into the cavity</li><li><b>Chordae tendineae</b>: The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"9","ks":["Trabeculae carneae","Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","Papillary muscle","Chordae tendineae"]},{"l":"VB-T4","h":"Heart valves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right half of the sternal body opposite the 4th intercostal space</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Mitral valve behind and to its left; septal cusp against the interventricular septum</dd></dl><p>The right atrioventricular valve, with anterior, posterior and septal cusps attached to the fibrous ring around the right atrioventricular orifice and tethered by chordae tendineae to three papillary muscles. It lies posterior to the body of the sternum at about the 4th and 5th intercostal spaces. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and look into the opening that leads forward into the right ventricle; the cusps around it are the tricuspid valve.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left half of the sternum at the 4th costal cartilage; right ventricle in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left atrium behind it, then the esophagus</dd></dl><p>The left atrioventricular valve, with anterior and posterior cusps tethered by chordae tendineae to the anterior and posterior papillary muscles of the left ventricle. Its anterior cusp lies between the mitral and aortic orifices, and the valve lies posterior to the sternum at about the level of the 4th costal cartilage. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the left atrium from behind and look into the opening leading forward into the left ventricle; the two cusps around it are the mitral valve.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg\" alt=\"Interior of right ventricle of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.75%;top:90.16%;width:15.01%;height:4.89%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve: Interior of right ventricle of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_170_1462.jpg\" alt=\"Valves of heart and great vessels\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.79%;top:93.96%;width:15.30%;height:3.81%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve: Valves of heart and great vessels <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.55</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pulmonary valve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Medial end of the left 3rd costal cartilage and adjacent sternum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Aortic valve, behind, below and to its right</dd></dl><p>The semilunar valve at the junction of the conus arteriosus and the pulmonary trunk, with anterior, right and left cusps and no chordae tendineae. It lies anterior, superior and to the left of the aortic valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the pulmonary trunk down to the heart and open it; the three pocket-shaped cusps at its base are the pulmonary valve.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Aortic valve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pulmonary valve and right ventricular outflow in front; left sternum at the 3rd intercostal space</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left atrium behind it; anterior mitral cusp continuous with its base</dd></dl><p>The semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the ascending aorta, with right, left and posterior cusps and no chordae; the right and left coronary arteries open from the aortic sinuses above the right and left cusps. It lies near the center of the heart, posterior to the left half of the sternum at about the 3rd intercostal space, posterior and to the right of the pulmonary valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the ascending aorta just above the heart and look down; the three cusps at its base, with coronary openings above two of them, form the aortic valve.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve</b>: deep to right half of the sternal body opposite the 4th intercostal space; superficial to mitral valve behind and to its left</li><li><b>Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve</b>: deep to left half of the sternum at the 4th costal cartilage; superficial to left atrium behind it, then the esophagus</li><li><b>Pulmonary valve</b>: deep to medial end of the left 3rd costal cartilage and adjacent sternum; superficial to aortic valve, behind, below and to its right</li><li><b>Aortic valve</b>: deep to pulmonary valve and right ventricular outflow in front; superficial to left atrium behind it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"10","ks":["Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve","Pulmonary valve","Aortic valve"]},{"l":"VB-A1","h":"Flat abdominal muscles, outside in","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>External oblique muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>External surfaces of ribs 5–12</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, pubic tubercle and anterior half of the iliac crest</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Investing fascia, then subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa's and Camper's layers) and skin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Internal oblique muscle</dd></dl><p>Outermost of the three flat abdominal muscles: it arises by fleshy digitations from the external surfaces of ribs 5-12 and its fibres run inferomedially (downward and forward), becoming aponeurosis anterior to rectus abdominis and ending inferiorly in the inguinal ligament. <i>Finding it:</i> Clear the superficial fascia over the side of the abdomen; the first muscle layer, whose upper digitations interlock with serratus anterior on the lower ribs, is external oblique.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Internal oblique muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inferior borders of ribs 10–12, linea alba, and pubic crest via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>External oblique muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd></dl><p>Middle of the three flat muscles, directly deep to external oblique: its fibres fan out from the thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of the iliac crest and lateral part of the inguinal ligament, running mostly superomedially, and its aponeurosis splits around rectus abdominis above the arcuate line. <i>Finding it:</i> Cut and reflect external oblique; the next fleshy layer, with fibres crossing those of external oblique, is internal oblique; its lowest fibres arch over the spermatic cord and continue onto it as cremaster.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_220_1563.jpg\" alt=\"Inferior abdominal wall and inguinal region of a male\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.59%;top:15.24%;width:14.87%;height:4.35%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>External oblique muscle: Inferior abdominal wall and inguinal region of a male <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.8</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_218_1560.jpg\" alt=\"Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:40.49%;top:5.69%;width:17.85%;height:4.87%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Internal oblique muscle: Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.6</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Transversus abdominis muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 7–12, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral third of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, and pubic crest and pecten pubis via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Internal oblique muscle, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</dd></dl><p>Deepest of the three flat abdominal muscles: its fibres run horizontally from the internal surfaces of costal cartilages 7-12, the thoracolumbar fascia and the iliac crest, and its aponeurosis passes posterior to rectus abdominis above the arcuate line and anterior to it below. <i>Finding it:</i> Reflect internal oblique; the deepest fleshy layer, with horizontal fibres and the lower thoracic and L1 nerves lying on its outer surface, is transversus abdominis.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>External oblique muscle</b>: arises from external surfaces of ribs 5–12; deep to investing fascia, then subcutaneous tissue (Scarpa's and Camper's layers) and skin; superficial to internal oblique muscle</li><li><b>Internal oblique muscle</b>: arises from thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament; deep to external oblique muscle; superficial to transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</li><li><b>Transversus abdominis muscle</b>: arises from inner surfaces of costal cartilages 7–12, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral third of inguinal ligament; deep to internal oblique muscle, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them; superficial to transversalis fascia, then extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["External oblique muscle","Internal oblique muscle","Transversus abdominis muscle"]},{"l":"VB-A1","h":"Rectus abdominis and rectus sheath","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Rectus abdominis muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Pubic symphysis and pubic crest</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5–7</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior rectus sheath, fused to its tendinous intersections</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line; transversalis fascia below it</dd></dl><p>Paired vertical strap muscle on either side of the linea alba, from the pubic crest and symphysis to the xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5-7, enclosed in the rectus sheath and crossed by tendinous intersections; the epigastric vessels run on its deep surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the linea alba in the midline, open the anterior rectus sheath beside it, and the vertical-fibred muscle inside is rectus abdominis.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior rectus sheath, to which they are firmly fused</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Deeper rectus abdominis fibres and the posterior rectus sheath, to which they do not attach</dd></dl><p>Transverse fibrous bands that interrupt rectus abdominis, usually three (at about the level of the umbilicus, near the xiphoid process and midway between), fused to the anterior layer of the rectus sheath but not to the posterior layer. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the anterior rectus sheath; the horizontal lines where the sheath stays stuck to the muscle are the tendinous intersections.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_219_1561.jpg\" alt=\"Anterolateral abdominal wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.79%;top:29.74%;width:14.24%;height:3.87%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Rectus abdominis muscle: Anterolateral abdominal wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.7</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_220_1563.jpg\" alt=\"Inferior abdominal wall and inguinal region of a male\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:71.32%;top:22.95%;width:10.14%;height:4.37%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Linea alba: Inferior abdominal wall and inguinal region of a male <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.8</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Linea alba</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Interlacing aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Subcutaneous tissue and skin; the umbilicus lies within it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</dd></dl><p>Midline fibrous band from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis, formed by the interlacing aponeuroses of the three flat muscles of both sides; it separates the two rectus abdominis muscles and contains the umbilicus. <i>Finding it:</i> Start at the umbilicus; the white vertical seam running above and below it between the two recti is the linea alba.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pyramidalis muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the pubis and the anterior pubic ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Linea alba, below the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior rectus sheath</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lowest part of rectus abdominis</dd></dl><p>Small triangular muscle anterior to the lowest part of rectus abdominis, inside the rectus sheath, running from the front of the pubis up to the linea alba; it is absent in about 20% of people. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the anterior rectus sheath just above the pubis; a small triangle of muscle lying on the front of the lowest rectus and pointing up toward the linea alba is pyramidalis.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Rectus abdominis, which lies directly on transversalis fascia below this line</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia and extraperitoneal fat; inferior epigastric vessels pass up in front of it</dd></dl><p>Curved lower edge of the posterior layer of the rectus sheath, usually about one-third of the way from the umbilicus to the pubic crest; the inferior epigastric vessels enter the rectus sheath at this line. <i>Finding it:</i> Look at the back of rectus abdominis from inside (or lift the muscle); follow the posterior sheath downward until it stops in a curved edge, where the inferior epigastric artery passes up into the sheath.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Rectus abdominis muscle</b>: arises from pubic symphysis and pubic crest; deep to anterior rectus sheath, fused to its tendinous intersections; superficial to posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line</li><li><b>Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)</b>: deep to anterior rectus sheath, to which they are firmly fused; superficial to deeper rectus abdominis fibres and the posterior rectus sheath, to which they do not attach</li><li><b>Linea alba</b>: deep to subcutaneous tissue and skin; superficial to transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum</li><li><b>Pyramidalis muscle</b>: arises from anterior surface of the pubis and the anterior pubic ligament; deep to anterior rectus sheath; superficial to lowest part of rectus abdominis</li><li><b>Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)</b>: deep to rectus abdominis, which lies directly on transversalis fascia below this line; superficial to transversalis fascia and extraperitoneal fat</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Rectus abdominis muscle","Tendinous intersections (of rectus abdominis)","Linea alba","Pyramidalis muscle","Arcuate line (of rectus sheath)"]},{"l":"VB-A1","h":"Epigastric arteries and inguinal region","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior epigastric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the inferior epigastric artery near the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Rectus abdominis muscle</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior rectus sheath</dd></dl><p>The superior epigastric artery is the medial terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery; it passes down from the 6th intercostal space, between the sternal and costal attachments of the diaphragm, into the rectus sheath, and descends on the posterior surface of rectus abdominis. It anastomoses with the inferior epigastric artery within the sheath around the umbilicus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the internal thoracic artery and follow it below the 6th intercostal space; the medial branch that continues straight down behind rectus abdominis inside its sheath is the superior epigastric artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior epigastric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>External iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lower rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the superior epigastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then rectus abdominis once inside the rectus sheath</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), then posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line</dd></dl><p>Branch of the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament that ascends medial to the deep inguinal ring beneath the peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line and runs on the deep surface of rectus abdominis, usually with two companion veins. <i>Finding it:</i> From inside the lower abdominal wall, find the deep inguinal ring with the ductus deferens or round ligament entering it; the vessel climbing along its medial edge toward rectus is the inferior epigastric artery.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_182_140.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries of the thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:71.67%;top:76.94%;width:20.50%;height:4.89%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior epigastric artery: Arteries of the thoracic wall <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.29</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_376_1869.jpg\" alt=\"Pelvic fascial ligaments\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:70.76%;top:16.07%;width:13.49%;height:4.66%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior epigastric artery: Pelvic fascial ligaments <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.14</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inguinal ligament</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Rolled-under lower free edge of the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) to the pubic tubercle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Skin and subcutaneous fat (Camper's and Scarpa's layers) at the groin crease; the spermatic cord rests on its upper grooved surface</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliopsoas, femoral nerve and femoral vessels, passing beneath it into the thigh</dd></dl><p>Thick, rolled-under inferior border of the external oblique aponeurosis running from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle; its grooved upper surface forms the floor of the inguinal canal, and the femoral vessels and nerve pass deep to it into the thigh. <i>Finding it:</i> Locate the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle; the firm band connecting them along the groin is the inguinal ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ilioinguinal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of medial thigh, penile root or mons, anterior scrotum or labium majus; lower internal oblique</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney at first; in the inguinal canal, the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; then the spermatic cord (or round ligament) in the inguinal canal</dd></dl><p>Branch of L1 that emerges from the lateral border of psoas, crosses quadratus lumborum and iliacus just below the iliohypogastric nerve, and pierces transversus abdominis and internal oblique near the anterior iliac crest. It then runs through the inguinal canal alongside (outside) the spermatic cord or round ligament and exits through the superficial inguinal ring. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the external oblique aponeurosis over the inguinal canal; the thin white nerve lying on the front of the spermatic cord and emerging with it at the superficial ring is the ilioinguinal.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Superior epigastric artery</b>: comes from internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch); deep to rectus abdominis muscle; superficial to posterior rectus sheath</li><li><b>Inferior epigastric artery</b>: comes from external iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament; deep to transversalis fascia, then rectus abdominis once inside the rectus sheath; superficial to parietal peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), then posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line</li><li><b>Inguinal ligament</b>: deep to skin and subcutaneous fat (Camper's and Scarpa's layers) at the groin crease; superficial to iliopsoas, femoral nerve and femoral vessels, passing beneath it into the thigh</li><li><b>Ilioinguinal nerve</b>: comes from L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve); deep to kidney at first; superficial to quadratus lumborum behind the kidney</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Superior epigastric artery","Inferior epigastric artery","Inguinal ligament","Ilioinguinal nerve"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Parts of the stomach","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Stomach</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lobe of liver, diaphragm and anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Stomach bed: lesser sac, pancreas, left kidney, left suprarenal gland, spleen, transverse mesocolon</dd></dl><p>The stomach is the dilated J-shaped part of the gut between the esophagus (at the cardia, left of T11) and the duodenum (at the pylorus, near L1 just right of the midline); its short lesser curvature faces up and right toward the liver and its long greater curvature carries the greater omentum. Its wall is thick, its mucosa forms rugae, and it has no teniae coli, haustra or omental appendices. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the esophagus passing through the esophageal hiatus, then follow it into the cardia and along the curvatures to the firm pyloric ring.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_267_1632.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:87.83%;top:5.23%;width:10.81%;height:4.15%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Stomach: Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.42</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Stomach</b>: deep to left lobe of liver, diaphragm and anterior abdominal wall; superficial to stomach bed: lesser sac, pancreas, left kidney, left suprarenal gland, spleen, transverse mesocolon</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Stomach"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Peritoneum and omenta","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Parietal peritoneum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Extraperitoneal fat, then transversalis fascia and transversus abdominis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Peritoneal cavity, with the greater omentum and intestines against its inner surface</dd></dl><p>Thin, glistening serous membrane lining the inner surface of the abdominal walls and the inferior surface of the diaphragm, separated from transversalis fascia by extraperitoneal fat; where it leaves the walls as mesenteries, omenta and ligaments it becomes the visceral peritoneum covering the organs. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the anterior abdominal wall and look at its inner surface; the shiny lining carrying the five umbilical folds below the umbilicus is parietal peritoneum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Greater omentum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Dorsal mesogastrium (a four-layered peritoneal fold)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Greater curvature of stomach to the transverse colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse colon and coils of small intestine</dd></dl><p>Fat-laden, apron-like peritoneal fold that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum, drapes anterior to the transverse colon and small intestine, and is attached to the transverse colon; its parts include the gastrocolic, gastrosplenic and gastrophrenic ligaments. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the abdomen; the fatty sheet covering the intestines is the greater omentum; lift it upward and confirm it stays attached along the greater curvature and the transverse colon.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_249_1601.jpg\" alt=\"Principal formations of peritoneum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.67%;top:68.87%;width:29.25%;height:4.88%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Parietal peritoneum: Principal formations of peritoneum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.25</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_334_343.jpg\" alt=\"Greater omentum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:40.20%;top:95.73%;width:30.08%;height:3.58%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Greater omentum: Greater omentum <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.56</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lesser omentum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesogastrium (ventral mesentery)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Lesser curvature and first part of duodenum to porta hepatis and fissure for ligamentum venosum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lobe of the liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac (omental bursa), with the caudate lobe and pancreas behind it</dd></dl><p>Thin double layer of peritoneum from the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum to the liver (fissure for the ligamentum venosum and porta hepatis), made of the hepatogastric ligament and the thick free hepatoduodenal ligament; it forms the anterior wall of the upper lesser sac. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the inferior border of the liver and pull the stomach down; the sheet between the lesser curvature and the liver, ending on the right in a thick free edge, is the lesser omentum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hepatogastric ligament</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesogastrium; it is the thin left part of the lesser omentum</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of stomach to the fissure for ligamentum venosum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left lobe of the liver</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac (omental bursa), with the caudate lobe and celiac trunk behind it</dd></dl><p>Thin, often translucent left part of the lesser omentum, running from the lesser curvature of the stomach to the fissure for the ligamentum venosum on the liver; the left and right gastric vessels run in it along the lesser curvature. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the liver and pull the stomach down; the thin membrane spanning from the lesser curvature up to the liver is the hepatogastric ligament, and the thick right free edge beyond it is the hepatoduodenal ligament.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Parietal peritoneum</b>: deep to extraperitoneal fat, then transversalis fascia and transversus abdominis; superficial to peritoneal cavity, with the greater omentum and intestines against its inner surface</li><li><b>Greater omentum</b>: deep to parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to transverse colon and coils of small intestine</li><li><b>Lesser omentum</b>: deep to left lobe of the liver; superficial to lesser sac (omental bursa), with the caudate lobe and pancreas behind it</li><li><b>Hepatogastric ligament</b>: deep to left lobe of the liver; superficial to lesser sac (omental bursa), with the caudate lobe and celiac trunk behind it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Parietal peritoneum","Greater omentum","Lesser omentum","Hepatogastric ligament"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Duodenum, pancreas and spleen","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Head of pancreas</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pylorus and transverse colon, with the transverse mesocolon root crossing it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), right renal vessels and left renal vein; bile duct grooves its back</dd></dl><p>The head of the pancreas lies within the C-shaped curve of the duodenum, to the right of the superior mesenteric vessels and anterior to the IVC, right renal vessels and left renal vein; the bile duct runs in a groove on, or embedded in, its posterior surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the descending duodenum and look medial to it: the gland tissue in the concavity of the C is the head.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Uncinate process of pancreas</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Superior mesenteric vein and artery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd></dl><p>The uncinate process is the projection from the lower part of the head of the pancreas that extends to the left posterior to the superior mesenteric vein and artery, anterior to the aorta and IVC, just above the third part of the duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMV as it passes behind the pancreas, then look behind and below it for the tongue of gland coming from the head.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_271_1643.jpg\" alt=\"Relationships of duodenum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.61%;top:56.52%;width:16.40%;height:4.22%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Head of pancreas: Relationships of duodenum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.45</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_295_1691.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply and venous drainage of pancreas\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:58.60%;top:73.54%;width:28.12%;height:5.19%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Uncinate process of pancreas: Arterial supply and venous drainage of pancreas <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.60</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Neck of pancreas</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pylorus of the stomach, with the lesser sac between</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>SMV (superior mesenteric vein) joining the splenic vein to form the portal vein</dd></dl><p>The neck of the pancreas is the short narrowed part connecting head and body, lying anterior to the superior mesenteric vein and the start of the portal vein (formed behind it), next to the pylorus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find where the SMV and splenic vein join; the gland lying directly in front of that junction is the neck.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Body of pancreas</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Aorta, SMA (superior mesenteric artery), left suprarenal, left kidney and renal vessels; splenic vein behind</dd></dl><p>The body of the pancreas runs to the left from the neck, crossing anterior to the aorta and L2 vertebra, the SMA, left suprarenal gland, left kidney and renal vessels; it forms part of the stomach bed in the floor of the lesser sac, with the splenic vein behind it and the splenic artery along its upper border. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the lesser sac and look at its floor behind the stomach: the gland lying over the aorta and left kidney is the body.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Tail of pancreas</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach (across the lesser sac) and the left colic flexure</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left kidney, then the splenorenal ligament with the splenic vessels to the splenic hilum</dd></dl><p>The tail of the pancreas is the narrow left end, lying anterior to the left kidney and passing with the splenic vessels between the layers of the splenorenal ligament to reach the splenic hilum; it is closely related to the left colic flexure. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic hilum and follow the splenic vessels medially; the gland lying among them is the tail.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Main pancreatic duct</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Small lobular ducts joining along the gland from tail to head</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla; opens at the major duodenal papilla</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Pancreatic tissue, which must be dissected away from the front to expose it</dd></dl><p>The main pancreatic duct runs through the substance of the pancreas from the tail to the head; in the head it turns down and joins the bile duct to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla. <i>Finding it:</i> Scrape away pancreatic tissue along the middle of the body and follow the white duct to the right into the head.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Spleen</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach anteriorly (gastric impression); left colic flexure below</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm against ribs 9–11 posterolaterally; left kidney medially</dd></dl><p>The spleen is a solid oval organ in the left hypochondrium lying against the diaphragm opposite ribs 9-11, posterolateral to the stomach, lateral to the left kidney and above the left colic flexure; its concave visceral surface has the hilum where the splenic vessels enter, and its superior border is usually notched. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the fundus of the stomach and follow the gastrosplenic ligament laterally to the solid organ tucked under the left dome of the diaphragm.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Head of pancreas</b>: deep to pylorus and transverse colon, with the transverse mesocolon root crossing it; superficial to IVC (inferior vena cava), right renal vessels and left renal vein</li><li><b>Uncinate process of pancreas</b>: deep to superior mesenteric vein and artery; superficial to aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava)</li><li><b>Neck of pancreas</b>: deep to pylorus of the stomach, with the lesser sac between; superficial to SMV (superior mesenteric vein) joining the splenic vein to form the portal vein</li><li><b>Body of pancreas</b>: deep to stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa); superficial to aorta, SMA (superior mesenteric artery), left suprarenal, left kidney and renal vessels</li><li><b>Tail of pancreas</b>: deep to stomach (across the lesser sac) and the left colic flexure; superficial to left kidney, then the splenorenal ligament with the splenic vessels to the splenic hilum</li><li><b>Main pancreatic duct</b>: empties into joins the bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla; deep to pancreatic tissue, which must be dissected away from the front to expose it</li><li><b>Spleen</b>: deep to stomach anteriorly (gastric impression); superficial to diaphragm against ribs 9–11 posterolaterally</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Head of pancreas","Uncinate process of pancreas","Neck of pancreas","Body of pancreas","Tail of pancreas","Main pancreatic duct","Spleen"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Celiac trunk and left gastric artery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Celiac trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta, anterior surface at T12, just below the aortic hiatus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries (foregut supply)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac and the lesser omentum; body of pancreas just below</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the abdominal aorta, just below the median arcuate ligament</dd></dl><p>The celiac trunk is the first unpaired anterior branch of the abdominal aorta, arising at T12 just below the median arcuate ligament; it is short (about 1.25 cm), wrapped in the celiac plexus and ganglia, and divides almost at once into the left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the abdominal aorta emerging through the aortic hiatus, then follow its anterior surface down to the first large anterior branch above the pancreas.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Celiac lymph nodes</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Gastric, hepatic, splenic and pancreatic lymph nodes</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Intestinal lymph trunk, then the cisterna chyli</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the abdominal aorta, around the celiac trunk root</dd></dl><p>Lymph nodes around the root of the celiac trunk in front of the aorta. They receive lymph from the gastric, hepatic, splenic and pancreatic nodes and drain to the intestinal lymph trunk and cisterna chyli. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk leaving the aorta; the small soft nodes clustered around its root are celiac nodes.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg\" alt=\"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.69%;top:13.77%;width:11.64%;height:4.27%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Celiac trunk: Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_306_1718.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries supplying the biliary duct and lymphatic drainage of gallbladder and bile duct\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.82%;top:57.29%;width:12.62%;height:11.12%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Celiac lymph nodes: Arteries supplying the biliary duct and lymphatic drainage of gallbladder and bile duct <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.71</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left gastric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its smallest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of the stomach and the abdominal esophagus; joins the right gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left crus of the diaphragm and the left suprarenal area, as it rises in the posterior wall</dd></dl><p>The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk; it ascends retroperitoneally toward the esophagus, gives esophageal branches, then turns down along the lesser curvature within the lesser omentum to anastomose with the right gastric artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then take the branch that heads up to the esophagogastric junction before curving down the lesser curvature.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left gastric vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of stomach and lower esophagus (esophageal veins)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein; its esophageal tributaries anastomose with azygos veins (portocaval site)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lesser omentum along the lesser curvature, then lesser sac peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior wall of the lesser sac, beside the left gastric artery</dd></dl><p>Runs with the left gastric artery along the upper lesser curvature and receives esophageal veins from the lower esophagus (which connect with azygos tributaries), then turns down and to the right behind the lesser sac to drain into the portal vein, sometimes into the splenic vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left gastric artery rising from the celiac trunk to the lesser curvature, then the vein beside it.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Celiac trunk</b>: comes from abdominal aorta, anterior surface at T12, just below the aortic hiatus; deep to peritoneum of the lesser sac and the lesser omentum; superficial to front of the abdominal aorta, just below the median arcuate ligament</li><li><b>Celiac lymph nodes</b>: drains into intestinal lymph trunk, then the cisterna chyli; deep to peritoneum of the lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum; superficial to front of the abdominal aorta, around the celiac trunk root</li><li><b>Left gastric artery</b>: comes from celiac trunk (its smallest branch); deep to peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature; superficial to left crus of the diaphragm and the left suprarenal area, as it rises in the posterior wall</li><li><b>Left gastric vein</b>: drains into hepatic portal vein; deep to lesser omentum along the lesser curvature, then lesser sac peritoneum; superficial to posterior wall of the lesser sac, beside the left gastric artery</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Celiac trunk","Celiac lymph nodes","Left gastric artery","Left gastric vein"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Splenic artery and its branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Splenic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its largest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Spleen; also pancreatic, short gastric, left gastro-omental and posterior gastric branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas</dd></dl><p>The splenic artery is the largest branch of the celiac trunk; it follows a tortuous course to the left along the superior border of the pancreas, posterior to the stomach and lesser sac and anterior to the left kidney, then passes in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then follow its left branch along the top of the pancreas toward the spleen.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Splenic vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Veins at the splenic hilum; receives short gastric, pancreatic and IMV (inferior mesenteric vein)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Body and tail of pancreas, which it grooves from behind</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left kidney, then the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and aorta as it runs right</dd></dl><p>The splenic vein forms at the splenic hilum and runs to the right in a groove on the posterior surface of the body and tail of the pancreas, below the splenic artery; it usually receives the inferior mesenteric vein and joins the superior mesenteric vein posterior to the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the body of the pancreas and look on its back surface for the straight, wide vein heading right toward the pancreatic neck.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_296_1694.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:39.44%;top:27.04%;width:13.00%;height:6.08%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Splenic artery: Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.61</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:58.75%;top:58.28%;width:11.58%;height:5.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Splenic vein: Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery, near the splenic hilum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; anastomoses with the right gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature; lesser sac behind</dd></dl><p>The left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery arises from the splenic artery near the splenic hilum, passes through the gastrosplenic ligament to the greater curvature, and runs to the right within the greater omentum, about a finger-breadth from the curvature, to anastomose with the right gastro-omental artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic artery at the hilum of the spleen, then follow the branch that turns onto the greater curvature heading toward the pylorus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Short gastric arteries</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery near the hilum, or its terminal splenic branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Fundus of the stomach</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front peritoneal layer of the gastrosplenic ligament</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Splenic recess of the lesser sac, as they run in the gastrosplenic ligament</dd></dl><p>The short gastric arteries are usually four or five small branches of the splenic artery (or its terminal branches) near the splenic hilum that pass in the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus of the stomach. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic hilum, then look in the gastrosplenic ligament for small vessels running to the fundus and upper greater curvature.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Splenic artery</b>: comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch); deep to stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa); superficial to left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas</li><li><b>Splenic vein</b>: drains into joins the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein; deep to body and tail of pancreas, which it grooves from behind; superficial to left kidney, then the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and aorta as it runs right</li><li><b>Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b>: comes from splenic artery, near the splenic hilum; deep to peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum; superficial to greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature</li><li><b>Short gastric arteries</b>: comes from splenic artery near the hilum, or its terminal splenic branches; deep to front peritoneal layer of the gastrosplenic ligament; superficial to splenic recess of the lesser sac, as they run in the gastrosplenic ligament</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Splenic artery","Splenic vein","Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","Short gastric arteries"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Common hepatic and gastroduodenal arteries","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Common hepatic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Divides into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries above the first part of duodenum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac</dd></dl><p>The common hepatic artery is the branch of the celiac trunk that runs to the right, retroperitoneally along the upper border of the pancreas, toward the superior part of the duodenum, where it divides into the gastroduodenal artery (down, behind the duodenum) and the proper hepatic artery (up, into the hepatoduodenal ligament). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, take its right-going branch, and stop at the point where it splits near the first part of the duodenum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Gastroduodenal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</dd></dl><p>The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery and descends posterior to the superior (first) part of the duodenum, in front of the neck/head of the pancreas, with the bile duct usually to its right; at the lower border of the duodenum it divides into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the common hepatic artery, then follow the branch that dives down behind the first part of the duodenum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg\" alt=\"Distribution of the common hepatic artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:68.95%;top:29.81%;width:23.03%;height:4.30%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Common hepatic artery: Distribution of the common hepatic artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_337_347.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal esophagus\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.79%;top:86.09%;width:22.35%;height:4.17%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Gastroduodenal artery: Abdominal esophagus <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.59</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery, behind the first part of the duodenum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Back of pancreatic head, duodenum and bile duct; joins the posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Head of pancreas, which covers it from the front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), with the bile duct beside it</dd></dl><p>The posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery behind the first part of the duodenum and descends on the posterior surface of the head of the pancreas, close to the bile duct, to anastomose with the posterior branch of the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn the duodenum and pancreatic head forward to see their posterior surface, find the bile duct, and look for the artery running beside it from the gastroduodenal.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch, with the right gastro-omental)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Front of pancreatic head and duodenum; joins the anterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum, with the transverse colon and mesocolon in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the pancreatic head, near the duodenum</dd></dl><p>The anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that descends on the anterior surface of the head of the pancreas, near its groove with the descending duodenum, and anastomoses with the anterior branch of the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the gastroduodenal artery at the lower border of the first part of the duodenum, then follow the branch running down over the front of the pancreatic head.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; joins the left gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Head of pancreas at its start, then the greater omentum below the greater curvature</dd></dl><p>The right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs to the left along the greater curvature between the layers of the greater omentum, supplying stomach and omentum and anastomosing with the left gastro-omental artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the gastroduodenal artery emerging below the first part of the duodenum, then follow its branch to the left along the greater curvature.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Common hepatic artery</b>: comes from celiac trunk; deep to peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum; superficial to upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac</li><li><b>Gastroduodenal artery</b>: comes from common hepatic artery; deep to first part of the duodenum; superficial to neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</li><li><b>Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b>: comes from gastroduodenal artery, behind the first part of the duodenum; deep to head of pancreas, which covers it from the front; superficial to IVC (inferior vena cava), with the bile duct beside it</li><li><b>Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b>: comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch, with the right gastro-omental); deep to peritoneum, with the transverse colon and mesocolon in front; superficial to anterior surface of the pancreatic head, near the duodenum</li><li><b>Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b>: comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus; deep to peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum; superficial to head of pancreas at its start, then the greater omentum below the greater curvature</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Common hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"]},{"l":"VB-A2","h":"Proper hepatic artery and its branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Proper hepatic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery, after the gastroduodenal artery leaves</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left hepatic arteries; usually gives the right gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein behind it; bile duct on its right</dd></dl><p>The proper hepatic artery is the continuation of the common hepatic artery beyond the gastroduodenal branch; it ascends in the hepatoduodenal ligament to the left of the bile duct and anterior to the portal vein, and divides near the porta hepatis into the right and left hepatic arteries (the right gastric artery usually arises from it). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) and sort its three structures: bile duct on the right, artery on the left, portal vein behind.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right gastric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Pyloric part and lesser curvature of stomach; joins the left gastric artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</dd></dl><p>The right gastric artery is a small artery that usually arises from the proper hepatic artery (its origin varies), descends to the pylorus, and runs to the left along the lesser curvature in the lesser omentum to anastomose with the left gastric artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery in the hepatoduodenal ligament, then look for the small branch heading down to the pylorus and along the lesser curvature.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg\" alt=\"Distribution of the common hepatic artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:69.06%;top:15.86%;width:21.04%;height:4.29%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Proper hepatic artery: Distribution of the common hepatic artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply to the pancreas\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:21.65%;top:15.21%;width:16.31%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right gastric artery: Arterial supply to the pancreas <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right gastric vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Pyloric part and lesser curvature of stomach; receives the prepyloric vein</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein, directly</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</dd></dl><p>Runs with the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature near the pylorus and drains directly into the portal vein; a small prepyloric vein ascends over the front of the pylorus into it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pylorus and the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature, then the accompanying vein running to the portal vein in the hepatoduodenal ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left hepatic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery (left terminal branch); sometimes from the left gastric artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left half of the liver, including the quadrate lobe and part of the caudate lobe</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left hepatic duct, the most anterior structure at the porta hepatis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left branch of the portal vein, at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><p>The left hepatic artery is the left terminal branch of the proper hepatic artery, running to the left side of the porta hepatis to supply the left part of the liver (it sometimes arises from the left gastric artery instead). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery and follow its left branch toward the left side of the porta hepatis.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right hepatic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Proper hepatic artery (right terminal branch); sometimes from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right half of the liver; gives the cystic artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Common hepatic duct, which usually crosses in front of it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right branch of the portal vein, behind it at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><p>The right hepatic artery is the right terminal branch of the proper hepatic artery; it usually passes posterior to the common hepatic duct to the right side of the porta hepatis and gives the cystic artery in the cystohepatic triangle (it sometimes arises from the SMA instead). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the proper hepatic artery at the top of the hepatoduodenal ligament and follow its right branch behind the common hepatic duct toward the gallbladder.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Cystic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right hepatic artery, in the cystohepatic triangle (of Calot)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gallbladder and cystic duct</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum covering the cystohepatic triangle (cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Neck of the gallbladder, reached above the cystic duct</dd></dl><p>The cystic artery usually arises from the right hepatic artery in the cystohepatic triangle (between the cystic duct, common hepatic duct and liver) and runs to the neck of the gallbladder, where it divides into superficial and deep branches; its origin varies. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cystic duct leaving the gallbladder neck, then look just above it in the triangle under the liver for the small artery reaching the gallbladder.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Proper hepatic artery</b>: comes from common hepatic artery, after the gastroduodenal artery leaves; deep to peritoneum of the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament); superficial to hepatic portal vein behind it</li><li><b>Right gastric artery</b>: comes from proper hepatic artery; deep to front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature; superficial to lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</li><li><b>Right gastric vein</b>: drains into hepatic portal vein, directly; deep to front layer of the lesser omentum, along the lesser curvature; superficial to lesser sac, behind the lesser omentum near the pylorus</li><li><b>Left hepatic artery</b>: comes from proper hepatic artery (left terminal branch); deep to left hepatic duct, the most anterior structure at the porta hepatis; superficial to left branch of the portal vein, at the porta hepatis</li><li><b>Right hepatic artery</b>: comes from proper hepatic artery (right terminal branch); deep to common hepatic duct, which usually crosses in front of it; superficial to right branch of the portal vein, behind it at the porta hepatis</li><li><b>Cystic artery</b>: comes from right hepatic artery, in the cystohepatic triangle (of Calot); deep to peritoneum covering the cystohepatic triangle (cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver); superficial to neck of the gallbladder, reached above the cystic duct</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"7","ks":["Proper hepatic artery","Right gastric artery","Right gastric vein","Left hepatic artery","Right hepatic artery","Cystic artery"]},{"l":"VB-A3","h":"Liver and its lobes","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right lobe of liver</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right dome of the diaphragm and the lower right ribs</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right kidney, right suprarenal gland, right colic flexure and duodenum</dd></dl><p>The largest anatomical lobe: everything to the right of the falciform ligament on the diaphragmatic surface and, on the visceral surface, to the right of the gallbladder fossa and the groove for the IVC. It extends lowest on the right and is related below to the right colic flexure, right kidney and duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament on the front of the liver, then the gallbladder and IVC on the underside; liver to the right of these is the right lobe.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left lobe of liver</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall below the xiphoid</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Stomach (anterior surface) and abdominal esophagus</dd></dl><p>The smaller, thinner anatomical lobe, to the left of the falciform ligament on the diaphragmatic surface and to the left of the fissures for the ligamentum teres and ligamentum venosum on the visceral surface. It extends into the left upper abdomen anterior to the stomach and lesser omentum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament and the round ligament in its free edge, then follow them to the fissure on the underside; liver to the left is the left lobe.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1699.jpg\" alt=\"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:3.25%;top:8.25%;width:9.25%;height:7.15%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right lobe of liver: Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_358_385.jpg\" alt=\"Posterior view of the bare area of the liver and associated ligaments\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:36.93%;top:83.92%;width:13.40%;height:5.03%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Quadrate lobe (of liver): Posterior view of the bare area of the liver and associated ligaments <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.96</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Quadrate lobe (of liver)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Pylorus and first part of the duodenum, below it; porta hepatis behind it</dd></dl><p>A small lobe on the visceral surface, in front of and below the porta hepatis, bounded by the gallbladder fossa on the right and the fissure for the ligamentum teres on the left. It lies above and in front of the superior part of the duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn the liver up and find the gallbladder and the round ligament; the square of liver between them, in front of the porta hepatis, is the quadrate lobe.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Caudate lobe (of liver)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Superior recess of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) on its right and behind, and the right crus of the diaphragm</dd></dl><p>A small lobe on the posterior part of the visceral surface, between the groove for the IVC on its right and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum on its left, lying above and behind the porta hepatis. On axial slabs it is a tongue of liver directly anterior or anteromedial to the IVC. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC on the back of the liver and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum; the lobe between them, above the porta hepatis, is the caudate lobe.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right lobe of liver</b>: deep to right dome of the diaphragm and the lower right ribs; superficial to right kidney, right suprarenal gland, right colic flexure and duodenum</li><li><b>Left lobe of liver</b>: deep to diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall below the xiphoid; superficial to stomach (anterior surface) and abdominal esophagus</li><li><b>Quadrate lobe (of liver)</b>: superficial to pylorus and first part of the duodenum, below it</li><li><b>Caudate lobe (of liver)</b>: deep to superior recess of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum in front; superficial to IVC (inferior vena cava) on its right and behind, and the right crus of the diaphragm</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Right lobe of liver","Left lobe of liver","Quadrate lobe (of liver)","Caudate lobe (of liver)"]},{"l":"VB-A3","h":"Fissures and ligaments of the liver","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ligamentum venosum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ductus venosus, the fetal shunt from umbilical vein to IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Left branch of the portal vein to the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lesser omentum, which attaches along it in the fissure</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Caudate lobe</dd></dl><p>The fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus venosus, lying in a fissure on the posterior visceral surface between the caudate lobe and the left lobe and running from the left branch of the portal vein to the IVC (or left hepatic vein). The hepatogastric part of the lesser omentum attaches in the depth of this fissure. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the left lobe and trace the lesser omentum up to the liver; follow its attachment into the deep fissure beside the caudate lobe.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg\" alt=\"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.64%;top:8.95%;width:16.55%;height:7.38%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ligamentum venosum: Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Ligamentum venosum</b>: deep to lesser omentum, which attaches along it in the fissure; superficial to caudate lobe</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Ligamentum venosum"]},{"l":"VB-A3","h":"Peritoneal ligaments of the liver","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Falciform ligament</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery (ventral mesogastrium)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, above the umbilicus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior and superior surface of the liver</dd></dl><p>Sickle-shaped, sagittal double fold of peritoneum attaching the anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm above the umbilicus; its free inferior edge contains the round ligament of the liver, and it marks the surface division between the anatomical right and left lobes. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the upper abdomen in the midline; follow the fold from the umbilicus up onto the anterior surface of the liver: that fold is the falciform ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Obliterated left umbilical vein</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Umbilicus to the left branch of the portal vein, via the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which encloses it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the liver, in the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd></dl><p>The fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein, running from the umbilicus in the free inferior edge of the falciform ligament to the fissure for the ligamentum teres on the visceral surface, where it joins the left branch of the portal vein. On the underside it separates the left lobe from the quadrate lobe. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament between the anterior abdominal wall and the liver, then feel along its free lower edge for the firm cord running to the underside of the liver.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_256_1611.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.57%;top:15.55%;width:14.58%;height:7.99%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Falciform ligament: Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.31</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg\" alt=\"Parts of greater and lesser omenta\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.47%;top:10.80%;width:13.44%;height:4.14%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): Parts of greater and lesser omenta <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Coronary ligament (of liver)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery; peritoneum reflecting from the diaphragm onto the liver</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Diaphragm to the superior and posterior surface of the liver, enclosing the bare area</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Superior and posterior surface of the right lobe, around the bare area</dd></dl><p>The peritoneal reflection from the diaphragm onto the posterosuperior surface of the liver, with an anterior (superior) and a posterior (inferior) layer that enclose the bare area. The anterior layer is continuous with the falciform ligament, and the two layers meet at the right and left triangular ligaments. <i>Finding it:</i> Run a hand over the top of the right lobe toward the back until it is stopped by the peritoneal reflection to the diaphragm; that reflection is the coronary ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left triangular ligament</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery; two peritoneal layers meeting at the left end of the coronary ligament</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Left tip of the left lobe of the liver to the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the diaphragm</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Superior surface of the left lobe of the liver</dd></dl><p>A thin double fold of peritoneum at the left extremity of the left lobe, attaching it to the diaphragm, formed where the two layers of the coronary ligament meet on the left. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the upper surface of the left lobe to its left tip and pull it down gently to see the fold to the diaphragm.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Falciform ligament</b>: deep to parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm; superficial to anterior and superior surface of the liver</li><li><b>Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)</b>: deep to free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which encloses it; superficial to inferior surface of the liver, in the fissure for ligamentum teres</li><li><b>Coronary ligament (of liver)</b>: deep to inferior surface of the diaphragm; superficial to superior and posterior surface of the right lobe, around the bare area</li><li><b>Left triangular ligament</b>: deep to inferior surface of the diaphragm; superficial to superior surface of the left lobe of the liver</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Falciform ligament","Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","Coronary ligament (of liver)","Left triangular ligament"]},{"l":"VB-A3","h":"Blood vessels of the liver","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hepatic portal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>SMV (superior mesenteric vein) and splenic vein, joining behind the pancreatic neck</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Liver: divides at the porta hepatis into right and left branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Bile duct (right) and proper hepatic artery (left), in the hepatoduodenal ligament</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), separated by the omental (epiploic) foramen</dd></dl><p>Formed posterior to the neck of the pancreas (about L1-L2) by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins, it ascends behind the superior part of the duodenum and then in the hepatoduodenal ligament posterior to the bile duct and proper hepatic artery to the porta hepatis, where it divides into right and left branches. It lies anterior to the IVC, separated from it by the omental foramen. <i>Finding it:</i> Put a finger through the omental foramen: the large soft vessel in the ligament in front of the finger is the portal vein, and the IVC is behind the finger.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right branch of portal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein, as its short, wide right division</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Sinusoids of the right half of the liver, via segmental branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right hepatic artery and right hepatic duct, in front of it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Caudate lobe (caudate process), behind it at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><p>The short, wide branch from the division of the portal vein at the porta hepatis, entering the right part of the liver posterior to the right hepatic artery and duct. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the portal vein up to the porta hepatis; at its division, the branch passing straight into the right lobe is the right branch.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:12.80%;top:38.08%;width:10.34%;height:5.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Hepatic portal vein: Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_375_410.jpg\" alt=\"Initial branching and relationships of the superior mesenteric artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:26.38%;top:9.96%;width:10.28%;height:4.88%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right branch of portal vein: Initial branching and relationships of the superior mesenteric artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.115</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left branch of portal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Hepatic portal vein, as its longer, thinner left division</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left half of the liver; joined by the round ligament and ligamentum venosum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Left hepatic artery and left hepatic duct; quadrate lobe in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Caudate lobe, behind it at the porta hepatis</dd></dl><p>The longer branch from the division of the portal vein, running horizontally to the left across the porta hepatis to the fissure for the ligamentum teres, where the round ligament joins it and the ligamentum venosum connects it to the IVC. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the round ligament in its fissure on the underside of the liver and follow it back to the vein it joins.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Central veins of the liver lobules, draining all the liver sinusoids</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), just below the diaphragm</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver tissue; they run between segments, unlike the portal triads within segments</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), which they join at the back of the liver</dd></dl><p>Usually three (right, middle, left) large thin-walled veins that run within the liver between its segments and open into the IVC on the posterior surface just below the diaphragm. They do not pass through the porta hepatis and, unlike portal vein branches, are not wrapped in a sheath with an artery and a bile duct. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC in its groove on the back of the liver just below the diaphragm and look for the large openings entering it.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Hepatic portal vein</b>: drains into liver: divides at the porta hepatis into right and left branches; deep to bile duct (right) and proper hepatic artery (left), in the hepatoduodenal ligament; superficial to IVC (inferior vena cava), separated by the omental (epiploic) foramen</li><li><b>Right branch of portal vein</b>: drains into sinusoids of the right half of the liver, via segmental branches; deep to right hepatic artery and right hepatic duct, in front of it; superficial to caudate lobe (caudate process), behind it at the porta hepatis</li><li><b>Left branch of portal vein</b>: drains into left half of the liver; deep to left hepatic artery and left hepatic duct; superficial to caudate lobe, behind it at the porta hepatis</li><li><b>Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)</b>: drains into IVC (inferior vena cava), just below the diaphragm; deep to liver tissue; superficial to IVC (inferior vena cava), which they join at the back of the liver</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Hepatic portal vein","Right branch of portal vein","Left branch of portal vein","Hepatic veins (right, middle, left)"]},{"l":"VB-A3","h":"Gallbladder and its parts","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Gallbladder</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver (gallbladder fossa between right and quadrate lobes)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>First and second parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon</dd></dl><p>A pear-shaped, thin-walled sac in the gallbladder fossa on the visceral surface of the liver, between the right lobe and the quadrate lobe, with its fundus projecting beyond the inferior border and its neck pointing toward the porta hepatis to continue as the cystic duct. Its body is related to the transverse colon and the superior part of the duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior border of the right lobe at the right 9th costal cartilage in the midclavicular line, then lift the liver; the sac on its underside is the gallbladder.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Fundus of gallbladder</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal wall at the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage (midclavicular line)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse colon</dd></dl><p>The rounded blind end of the gallbladder projecting beyond the inferior border of the liver, usually touching the anterior abdominal wall at the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage in the midclavicular line. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior border of the right lobe, then the rounded sac projecting below it.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg\" alt=\"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.47%;top:54.74%;width:9.46%;height:4.55%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Gallbladder: Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg\" alt=\"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:11.59%;top:34.39%;width:7.35%;height:4.80%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Fundus of gallbladder: Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Body of gallbladder</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver, in the gallbladder fossa</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Transverse colon and the first part of the duodenum</dd></dl><p>The main part of the gallbladder between the fundus and the neck. It lies in the gallbladder fossa on the visceral surface of the liver and touches the transverse colon and the first and second parts of the duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the gallbladder from its rounded tip back toward the porta hepatis; the broad middle part in the liver fossa is the body.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Neck of gallbladder</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Liver, near the right end of the porta hepatis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum</dd></dl><p>The narrow, tapered end of the gallbladder pointing toward the porta hepatis, usually making an S-shaped bend before continuing as the cystic duct. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the gallbladder from its fundus toward the porta hepatis; where it narrows and bends just before the cystic duct is the neck.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Gallbladder</b>: deep to liver (gallbladder fossa between right and quadrate lobes); superficial to first and second parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon</li><li><b>Fundus of gallbladder</b>: deep to anterior abdominal wall at the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage (midclavicular line); superficial to transverse colon</li><li><b>Body of gallbladder</b>: deep to liver, in the gallbladder fossa; superficial to transverse colon and the first part of the duodenum</li><li><b>Neck of gallbladder</b>: deep to liver, near the right end of the porta hepatis; superficial to first part of the duodenum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Gallbladder","Fundus of gallbladder","Body of gallbladder","Neck of gallbladder"]},{"l":"VB-A3","h":"Biliary ducts and hepatic nodes","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Cystic duct</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Neck of the gallbladder</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the common hepatic duct to form the bile duct</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the hepatoduodenal ligament; the cystic artery runs just above it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right free edge of the lesser omentum, forming the lower side of the cystohepatic triangle</dd></dl><p>A duct about 3-4 cm long running from the neck of the gallbladder in the edge of the lesser omentum to join the common hepatic duct (usually on its right side) and form the bile duct; inside it has a spiral fold. The cystic artery runs nearby in the triangle formed by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct and liver. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the gallbladder and follow the duct leaving it toward the hepatoduodenal ligament until it meets the common hepatic duct.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Bile duct (common bile duct)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Common hepatic duct and cystic duct joining</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla; opens at the major duodenal papilla</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Free edge of the lesser omentum, then the first part of duodenum and pancreatic head</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Portal vein in the ligament, then the IVC (inferior vena cava) behind the pancreatic head</dd></dl><p>Formed by the union of the cystic and common hepatic ducts, it descends in the free edge of the lesser omentum to the right of the proper hepatic artery and anterior to the portal vein, then passes behind the superior part of the duodenum and in a groove on the posterior head of the pancreas. It joins the main pancreatic duct to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla. <i>Finding it:</i> Put a finger in the omental foramen; the free edge in front of it is the hepatoduodenal ligament, and the duct on its right front is the bile duct, which you follow down behind the duodenum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_307_1719.jpg\" alt=\"Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:34.27%;top:76.82%;width:10.02%;height:4.84%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Cystic duct: Nerves and veins of liver and biliary system <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.72</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_364_394.jpg\" alt=\"Bile drainage\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:2.18%;top:46.57%;width:8.11%;height:4.64%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Bile duct (common bile duct): Bile drainage <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.102</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hepatic lymph nodes</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Liver, gallbladder and bile ducts (including the cystic node)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Celiac lymph nodes, then the intestinal lymph trunk and cisterna chyli</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Hepatic artery and bile duct, within the free edge of the lesser omentum</dd></dl><p>Lymph nodes along the hepatic arteries and bile duct in the lesser omentum and at the porta hepatis. They drain the liver, gallbladder and parts of the stomach and duodenum and pass lymph to the celiac nodes. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the hepatoduodenal ligament; the small nodes along the hepatic artery and bile duct are hepatic nodes.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Cystic duct</b>: empties into joins the common hepatic duct to form the bile duct; deep to peritoneum of the hepatoduodenal ligament; superficial to right free edge of the lesser omentum, forming the lower side of the cystohepatic triangle</li><li><b>Bile duct (common bile duct)</b>: empties into joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla; deep to free edge of the lesser omentum, then the first part of duodenum and pancreatic head; superficial to portal vein in the ligament, then the IVC (inferior vena cava) behind the pancreatic head</li><li><b>Hepatic lymph nodes</b>: drains into celiac lymph nodes, then the intestinal lymph trunk and cisterna chyli; deep to peritoneum of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament); superficial to hepatic artery and bile duct, within the free edge of the lesser omentum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Cystic duct","Bile duct (common bile duct)","Hepatic lymph nodes"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Small intestine: jejunum, ileum and mesentery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Jejunum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal wall over the left kidney, slung from the mesentery</dd></dl><p>The first two-fifths of the small intestine beyond the duodenum, starting at the duodenojejunal flexure and lying mostly in the upper left abdomen; it is wider, thicker-walled and redder than the ileum, with tall, closely packed circular folds inside. Its mesentery has little fat, usually 1-2 rows of large arcades and long vasa recta. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 under the transverse mesocolon, then follow the loops away from it; hold the mesentery to the light to see the long vasa recta.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ileum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right posterior abdominal wall, iliac vessels and pelvic organs, slung from the mesentery</dd></dl><p>The distal three-fifths of the small intestine beyond the duodenum, lying mostly in the lower right abdomen and pelvis and ending at the ileocecal junction. Compared with the jejunum it is narrower, thinner-walled and paler, with low, sparse circular folds, Peyer patches, more mesenteric fat, many rows of small arcades and short vasa recta. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow backward the small intestine that enters its medial side; those terminal loops are ileum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:82.87%;top:40.81%;width:9.03%;height:4.47%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Jejunum: Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_267_1632.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:9.93%;top:79.28%;width:7.33%;height:4.19%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ileum: Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.42</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Jejunum</b>: deep to greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to posterior abdominal wall over the left kidney, slung from the mesentery</li><li><b>Ileum</b>: deep to greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to right posterior abdominal wall, iliac vessels and pelvic organs, slung from the mesentery</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Jejunum","Ileum"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Cecum, ileocecal junction and appendix","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Cecum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right iliacus and psoas major, with the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves</dd></dl><p>The first and widest part of the large intestine: a blind-ended sac in the right iliac fossa below the level of the ileocecal junction, with teniae coli and haustra and the appendix attached to its posteromedial side. It is usually covered by peritoneum but has no mesentery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the terminal ileum and the ileocecal junction; the large sac hanging below that junction is the cecum, and its three teniae meet at the appendix.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus and psoas major in the right iliac fossa</dd></dl><p>The junction of the terminal ileum with the large intestine on its medial (posteromedial) side, at the boundary between the cecum below and the ascending colon above. Opened, it shows the ileocecal orifice with upper and lower lips (the ileocecal valve), and the orifice of the appendix lies a little below it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow the terminal ileum to the point where it joins the large bowel.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_347_366.jpg\" alt=\"Cecum and appendix\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:2.79%;top:66.32%;width:12.76%;height:4.96%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Cecum: Cecum and appendix <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.77</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_278_1658.jpg\" alt=\"Terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:37.02%;top:27.88%;width:8.85%;height:9.23%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve): Terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.53</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Vermiform appendix</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Cecum when retrocecal (the usual position); otherwise ileal coils and the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus and psoas major in the right iliac fossa, usually tucked behind the cecum</dd></dl><p>A narrow blind-ended tube, usually 6-10 cm long, arising from the posteromedial wall of the cecum below the ileocecal junction, with its own triangular mesentery (mesoappendix) carrying the appendicular artery. Its position varies; it usually lies behind the cecum (retrocecal). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum and follow any one of its teniae coli down to where the three bands converge; the appendix arises there.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Cecum</b>: deep to coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to right iliacus and psoas major, with the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves</li><li><b>Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)</b>: deep to coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to iliacus and psoas major in the right iliac fossa</li><li><b>Vermiform appendix</b>: deep to cecum when retrocecal (the usual position); superficial to iliacus and psoas major in the right iliac fossa, usually tucked behind the cecum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Cecum","Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","Vermiform appendix"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Colon, ascending to descending","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ascending colon</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum, the greater omentum and the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus, quadratus lumborum, transversus abdominis aponeurosis and the lower pole of the right kidney</dd></dl><p>Runs upward on the right side of the posterior abdominal wall from the cecum (above the ileocecal junction) to the right colic flexure under the right lobe of the liver. It is secondarily retroperitoneal, with peritoneum on its front and sides and the right paracolic gutter lateral to it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow the large bowel upward along the right flank to the liver.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right colic (hepatic) flexure</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right lobe of the liver and the gallbladder fundus, above and in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower anterior surface of the right kidney, with the descending duodenum medial to it</dd></dl><p>The bend where the ascending colon turns medially and forward into the transverse colon, under the visceral surface of the right lobe of the liver and anterior to the lower part of the right kidney. It is usually lower, less acute and more mobile than the left colic flexure. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the gallbladder and right lobe of the liver, then follow the ascending colon up to the point where it turns left.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_349_371.jpg\" alt=\"Colon\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:3.97%;top:55.13%;width:26.79%;height:3.23%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ascending colon: Colon <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.82</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg\" alt=\"Parts of greater and lesser omenta\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.47%;top:60.06%;width:16.44%;height:6.64%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right colic (hepatic) flexure: Parts of greater and lesser omenta <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Transverse colon</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater omentum, fused to its anterior surface, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Descending duodenum, head of the pancreas and small intestine coils, hung on the transverse mesocolon</dd></dl><p>The longest and most mobile part of the colon, running from the right to the left colic flexure and hanging on the transverse mesocolon, whose root runs along the pancreas. The greater omentum is attached along its anterior surface, and it has teniae coli, haustra and omental appendices. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the greater omentum upward; the large bowel attached to its back and hanging below the stomach is the transverse colon.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left colic (splenic) flexure</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Greater curvature of the stomach and the lower spleen, under the left costal margin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower part of the left kidney; tied to the diaphragm by the phrenicocolic ligament</dd></dl><p>The bend where the transverse colon turns downward into the descending colon, on the left beside the inferior part of the spleen and anterior to the left kidney. It is usually higher, more acute and less mobile than the right colic flexure and is attached to the diaphragm by the phrenicocolic ligament. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the spleen, then follow the transverse colon to the left until it turns sharply down along the left flank.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Descending colon</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of jejunum medially, then the greater omentum and anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lateral border of the left kidney, quadratus lumborum, transversus abdominis aponeurosis and iliacus</dd></dl><p>Runs downward on the left side of the posterior abdominal wall from the left colic flexure to the left iliac fossa, where it becomes the sigmoid colon, passing anterior to the lateral border of the left kidney. It is secondarily retroperitoneal, usually without a mesentery, and usually narrower than the ascending colon. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the spleen and the left colic flexure, then follow the large bowel down the left flank to the iliac fossa.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Ascending colon</b>: deep to coils of ileum, the greater omentum and the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to iliacus, quadratus lumborum, transversus abdominis aponeurosis and the lower pole of the right kidney</li><li><b>Right colic (hepatic) flexure</b>: deep to right lobe of the liver and the gallbladder fundus, above and in front; superficial to lower anterior surface of the right kidney, with the descending duodenum medial to it</li><li><b>Transverse colon</b>: deep to greater omentum, fused to its anterior surface, then the anterior abdominal wall; superficial to descending duodenum, head of the pancreas and small intestine coils, hung on the transverse mesocolon</li><li><b>Left colic (splenic) flexure</b>: deep to greater curvature of the stomach and the lower spleen, under the left costal margin; superficial to lower part of the left kidney</li><li><b>Descending colon</b>: deep to coils of jejunum medially, then the greater omentum and anterior abdominal wall; superficial to lateral border of the left kidney, quadratus lumborum, transversus abdominis aponeurosis and iliacus</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Ascending colon","Right colic (hepatic) flexure","Transverse colon","Left colic (splenic) flexure","Descending colon"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Teniae coli, haustra and semilunar folds","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Teniae coli</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Serosa (visceral peritoneum) of the colon</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Circular muscle layer of the colon wall</dd></dl><p>Three narrow ribbons of thickened longitudinal muscle running along the cecum and colon, about equally spaced around the circumference; they converge on the base of the appendix and spread out at the rectosigmoid junction, so the rectum has none. On the transverse colon one band carries the greater omentum and one the transverse mesocolon. <i>Finding it:</i> Find any part of the colon and look for the pale flat band running lengthwise; follow it on the cecum to where it meets the other two at the appendix.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_348_367.jpg\" alt=\"Positions of the appendix\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:23.82%;top:1.44%;width:20.09%;height:4.80%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Teniae coli: Positions of the appendix <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.79</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Teniae coli</b>: deep to serosa (visceral peritoneum) of the colon; superficial to circular muscle layer of the colon wall</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Teniae coli"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Sigmoid colon, rectum and neighbours","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Sigmoid colon</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the lower anterior abdominal wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left external iliac vessels, piriformis and sacral plexus; rests on the bladder or uterus below</dd></dl><p>S-shaped loop of colon from the left iliac fossa (end of the descending colon) to the third sacral vertebra, where it continues as the rectum. It hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon, is very mobile and still has teniae coli, haustra and omental appendices. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the descending colon down the left flank to the iliac fossa; the loop that swings free on a mesentery is the sigmoid colon.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Rectum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Bladder, seminal glands and prostate (male) or uterus and vagina (female), across the peritoneal pouch</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sacrum, coccyx and piriformis, with the mesorectal fat and superior rectal vessels between</dd></dl><p>The continuation of the sigmoid colon from about S3, following the concavity of the sacrum and coccyx in the pelvis. At the rectosigmoid junction the teniae coli spread into a continuous longitudinal muscle coat, so the rectum has no teniae, haustra, omental appendices or mesentery. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the sigmoid colon down into the pelvis; where its mesocolon ends and the teniae fan out in front of the sacrum, you are on the rectum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_399_1901.jpg\" alt=\"Sigmoid colon entering lesser pelvis and becoming rectum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:89.75%;top:20.25%;width:7.72%;height:4.32%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Sigmoid colon: Sigmoid colon entering lesser pelvis and becoming rectum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.31</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_277_1657.jpg\" alt=\"Terminal ileum and large intestine (including appendix)\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:61.50%;top:73.67%;width:8.25%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Rectum: Terminal ileum and large intestine (including appendix) <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.52</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Sigmoid colon</b>: deep to coils of ileum and the lower anterior abdominal wall; superficial to left external iliac vessels, piriformis and sacral plexus</li><li><b>Rectum</b>: deep to bladder, seminal glands and prostate (male) or uterus and vagina (female), across the peritoneal pouch; superficial to sacrum, coccyx and piriformis, with the mesorectal fat and superior rectal vessels between</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Sigmoid colon","Rectum"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Superior mesenteric artery to the small bowel","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior mesenteric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Midgut: duodenum below the major papilla to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum</dd></dl><p>A single large artery from the anterior aorta about 1 cm below the celiac trunk (L1) that runs down behind the neck of the pancreas, then in front of the left renal vein, uncinate process and third part of the duodenum into the root of the mesentery. Its jejunal and ileal branches leave its left side, the middle colic leaves its front, the right colic and ileocolic leave its right side, and the superior mesenteric vein lies on its right. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the pancreas and the third part of the duodenum, then the artery emerging between them just left of the superior mesenteric vein; trace it up to the aorta.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior mesenteric vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas at its end; the root of the mesentery below that</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Third part of the duodenum and uncinate process, right of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd></dl><p>Drains the small intestine, cecum, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon, ascending in the root of the mesentery on the right side of the superior mesenteric artery, anterior to the third part of the duodenum and the uncinate process. Behind the neck of the pancreas it joins the splenic vein to form the hepatic portal vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the superior mesenteric artery below the pancreas, then take the wider, thin-walled vessel immediately to its right and follow it up behind the pancreatic neck.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:52.50%;top:11.47%;width:34.21%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior mesenteric artery: Superior mesenteric artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:11.90%;top:88.25%;width:22.04%;height:5.16%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior mesenteric vein: Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery), usually its first branch, or the first jejunal artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Head of the pancreas and lower duodenum; anterior and posterior branches join the superior pancreaticoduodenals</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Root of the transverse mesocolon (anterior branch); the pancreatic head covers the posterior branch</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Head of the pancreas, in the groove between it and the duodenal C</dd></dl><p>Usually the first branch of the superior mesenteric artery (or of its first jejunal branch), arising near the upper border of the third part of the duodenum and dividing into anterior and posterior branches. These ascend in the groove between the head of the pancreas and the duodenal C to anastomose with the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the superior mesenteric artery where it emerges below the pancreatic neck, then follow its first small right-sided branch into the groove between the pancreatic head and the duodenum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), about 15–18 branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Jejunum and ileum, through arterial arcades and vasa recta</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the mesentery, then the greater omentum in the intact abdomen</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Between the two peritoneal layers of the mesentery, in its fat</dd></dl><p>About 15-18 jejunal and ileal arteries arise from the left side of the superior mesenteric artery and run between the two layers of the mesentery toward the small intestine. They join neighbouring branches to form arcades, from which vasa recta pass to the gut wall. <i>Finding it:</i> Spread the small-intestine mesentery and hold it up to the light; trace the branches nearest the root back to the left side of the superior mesenteric artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Vasa recta (straight arteries)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Outermost (terminal) arterial arcade in the mesentery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Mesenteric border of the small intestine, alternating sides; long (jejunum), short (ileum)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the mesentery, with little fat over them by the jejunum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Between the two peritoneal layers of the mesentery, near the gut wall</dd></dl><p>Straight arteries that run from the outermost arcade to the mesenteric border of the small intestine, passing alternately to either side of the wall. They are long in the jejunum and short in the ileum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the outermost arterial arcade, then look at the fine straight vessels between it and the intestinal wall.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Superior mesenteric artery</b>: comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk; deep to neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery; superficial to left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum</li><li><b>Superior mesenteric vein</b>: drains into joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein; deep to neck of the pancreas at its end; superficial to third part of the duodenum and uncinate process, right of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</li><li><b>Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b>: comes from SMA (superior mesenteric artery), usually its first branch, or the first jejunal artery; deep to root of the transverse mesocolon (anterior branch); superficial to head of the pancreas, in the groove between it and the duodenal C</li><li><b>Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries</b>: comes from left side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), about 15–18 branches; deep to peritoneum of the mesentery, then the greater omentum in the intact abdomen; superficial to between the two peritoneal layers of the mesentery, in its fat</li><li><b>Vasa recta (straight arteries)</b>: comes from outermost (terminal) arterial arcade in the mesentery; deep to peritoneum of the mesentery, with little fat over them by the jejunum; superficial to between the two peritoneal layers of the mesentery, near the gut wall</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Superior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric vein","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries","Vasa recta (straight arteries)"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Middle colic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), just below the pancreas</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Transverse colon; right and left branches join the right and left colic arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Upper layer of the transverse mesocolon, the floor of the lesser sac under the stomach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lower layer of the transverse mesocolon, over the duodenum and jejunum</dd></dl><p>Arises from the anterior surface of the superior mesenteric artery just below the pancreas and runs forward between the layers of the transverse mesocolon toward the transverse colon, where it usually divides into right and left branches. Unlike the right colic and ileocolic arteries, which run behind the parietal peritoneum, it travels inside a mesentery. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the transverse colon upward to stretch the transverse mesocolon, then find the artery entering it from the SMA at the lower border of the pancreas.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right colic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); may share a trunk with the middle colic or ileocolic</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Ascending colon; ascending and descending branches join the middle colic and ileocolic</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (right infracolic space)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels on the posterior wall</dd></dl><p>A branch from the right side of the superior mesenteric artery, between the middle colic above and the ileocolic below, that runs to the right behind the parietal peritoneum of the posterior wall to the ascending colon, where it divides into ascending and descending branches. It is the most variable SMA branch and may share a trunk with the middle colic or ileocolic artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Reflect the ascending colon medially or strip the posterior peritoneum right of the SMA; the right colic is the branch heading straight across to the ascending colon between the middle colic and ileocolic.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1662.jpg\" alt=\"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.39%;top:27.04%;width:16.79%;height:4.75%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Middle colic artery: Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:68.87%;top:50.91%;width:12.80%;height:3.22%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right colic artery: Superior mesenteric artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ileocolic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery), its lowest right-sided (terminal) branch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Terminal ileum, cecum, appendix (appendicular artery) and lower ascending colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum along the root of the mesentery</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels, toward the right iliac fossa</dd></dl><p>The terminal right-sided branch of the superior mesenteric artery, running inferiorly and to the right behind the parietal peritoneum along the root of the mesentery toward the right iliac fossa. Near the ileocecal junction it gives an ascending (colic) branch, cecal branches, ileal branches and the appendicular artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ileocecal junction in the right iliac fossa, then follow the artery that reaches it back up toward the superior mesenteric artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Appendicular vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Vermiform appendix, via the mesoappendix</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Ileocolic vein, a tributary of the SMV (superior mesenteric vein)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the mesoappendix; terminal ileum crosses in front near its origin</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Inside the free edge of the mesoappendix, which hangs behind the terminal ileum</dd></dl><p>Accompanies the appendicular artery in the free edge of the mesoappendix and drains into the ileocolic vein, a tributary of the superior mesenteric vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the appendix and its mesoappendix; the vein running alongside the appendicular artery in the free edge is the appendicular vein.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Middle colic artery</b>: comes from anterior surface of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), just below the pancreas; deep to upper layer of the transverse mesocolon, the floor of the lesser sac under the stomach; superficial to lower layer of the transverse mesocolon, over the duodenum and jejunum</li><li><b>Right colic artery</b>: comes from right side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); deep to parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (right infracolic space); superficial to right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels on the posterior wall</li><li><b>Ileocolic artery</b>: comes from SMA (superior mesenteric artery), its lowest right-sided (terminal) branch; deep to parietal peritoneum along the root of the mesentery; superficial to right psoas major, right ureter and right gonadal vessels, toward the right iliac fossa</li><li><b>Appendicular vein</b>: drains into ileocolic vein, a tributary of the SMV (superior mesenteric vein); deep to peritoneum of the mesoappendix; superficial to inside the free edge of the mesoappendix, which hangs behind the terminal ileum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"7","ks":["Middle colic artery","Right colic artery","Ileocolic artery","Appendicular vein"]},{"l":"VB-A4","h":"Inferior mesenteric vessels and marginal artery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior mesenteric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L3, 3–4 cm above the bifurcation</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Hindgut: distal third of the transverse colon to the upper rectum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Third part of the duodenum at its origin, then the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta, then the left psoas major as it runs down and left</dd></dl><p>Arises from the anterior aorta at about L3, 3-4 cm above the aortic bifurcation, usually behind the lower border of the third part of the duodenum, and runs inferiorly and to the left behind the parietal peritoneum. Its branches are the left colic artery, the sigmoid arteries (usually 2-4) and its continuation, the superior rectal artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Push the small intestine to the right and find the aortic bifurcation, then look 3-4 cm above it on the front of the aorta for the artery heading down to the left.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior mesenteric vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Continuation of the superior rectal vein; receives the sigmoid and left colic veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Splenic vein behind the pancreas (usual); sometimes the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) or their junction</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum (paraduodenal fold), then the body of the pancreas</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left psoas major, just left of the duodenojejunal flexure</dd></dl><p>The continuation of the superior rectal vein, ascending on the posterior abdominal wall to the left of the inferior mesenteric artery and lateral to the duodenojejunal flexure, then passing behind the body of the pancreas. It usually ends in the splenic vein, sometimes in the superior mesenteric vein or at their junction. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the transverse colon and push the small intestine to the right; find the duodenojejunal flexure and look just to its left for the vertical vein heading up under the pancreas.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1662.jpg\" alt=\"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:80.47%;top:63.75%;width:15.84%;height:7.84%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior mesenteric artery: Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_281_1661.jpg\" alt=\"Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:80.54%;top:32.43%;width:17.77%;height:11.28%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior mesenteric vein: Veins, lymph nodes, and nerves of large intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.56</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left colic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its first branch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Descending colon and distal transverse colon; ascending branch joins the middle colic via the marginal artery</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (left infracolic space)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left psoas major, left ureter and left gonadal vessels</dd></dl><p>Usually the first branch of the inferior mesenteric artery, running to the left behind the parietal peritoneum toward the descending colon and dividing into ascending and descending branches. The ascending branch runs toward the left colic flexure and joins the middle colic artery through the marginal artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior mesenteric artery on the front of the aorta, then follow its first branch to the left across the posterior wall toward the descending colon.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Sigmoid arteries</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), usually 2–4 branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Sigmoid colon and the lowest descending colon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left psoas major and left ureter, then between the layers of the sigmoid mesocolon</dd></dl><p>Usually 2-4 branches of the inferior mesenteric artery that run obliquely downward and to the left between the layers of the sigmoid mesocolon to supply the sigmoid colon and the lowest descending colon. They lie between the left colic artery above and the superior rectal artery below. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the sigmoid colon to spread its mesocolon, then follow the vessels inside it back to the inferior mesenteric artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior rectal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectum via two branches beside it; anastomoses with the middle rectal arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon root, then the rectum in front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Left common iliac vessels, then the sacrum (S3) behind the rectum</dd></dl><p>The terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery beyond its sigmoid branches, descending in the root of the sigmoid mesocolon across the left common iliac vessels into the pelvis. At about S3 it divides into two branches that run on either side of the rectum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior mesenteric artery, then follow its main stem downward past the sigmoid branches over the pelvic brim to the back of the rectum.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Inferior mesenteric artery</b>: comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L3, 3–4 cm above the bifurcation; deep to third part of the duodenum at its origin, then the parietal peritoneum; superficial to abdominal aorta, then the left psoas major as it runs down and left</li><li><b>Inferior mesenteric vein</b>: drains into splenic vein behind the pancreas (usual); deep to parietal peritoneum (paraduodenal fold), then the body of the pancreas; superficial to left psoas major, just left of the duodenojejunal flexure</li><li><b>Left colic artery</b>: comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its first branch; deep to parietal peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall (left infracolic space); superficial to left psoas major, left ureter and left gonadal vessels</li><li><b>Sigmoid arteries</b>: comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), usually 2–4 branches; deep to peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon; superficial to left psoas major and left ureter, then between the layers of the sigmoid mesocolon</li><li><b>Superior rectal artery</b>: comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation; deep to peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon root, then the rectum in front; superficial to left common iliac vessels, then the sacrum (S3) behind the rectum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"8","ks":["Inferior mesenteric artery","Inferior mesenteric vein","Left colic artery","Sigmoid arteries","Superior rectal artery"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Muscles of the posterior abdominal wall","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Psoas major muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Sides of the T12–L5 vertebral bodies, intervening discs and lumbar transverse processes</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Lesser trochanter of the femur, joined with iliacus</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney, ureter, gonadal vessels and genitofemoral nerve, behind the peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Lumbar transverse processes and vertebral bodies; quadratus lumborum lies posterolateral</dd></dl><p>A thick fusiform muscle from the sides of the T12-L5 vertebral bodies, discs and transverse processes that descends lateral to the lumbar vertebral bodies and along the pelvic brim, joining iliacus to insert on the lesser trochanter deep to the inguinal ligament. The lumbar plexus forms within it: the genitofemoral nerve emerges on its anterior surface, the femoral nerve at its lateral border, and the obturator nerve at its medial border. <i>Finding it:</i> Find a lumbar vertebral body; the large round muscle hugging its side, anterior to the transverse process, is psoas major, with the kidney anterolateral and quadratus lumborum posterolateral.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Psoas minor muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Sides of the T12 and L1 vertebral bodies and the disc between</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Pectineal line and iliopubic eminence</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Psoas fascia and extraperitoneal fat beneath the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of psoas major</dd></dl><p>A small, often absent muscle with a short belly from the sides of T12-L1 and a long flat tendon that runs on the anterior surface of psoas major to the pectineal line and iliopubic eminence. <i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas major and look on its anterior surface for a thin, glistening flat tendon running along its length.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_341_1787.jpg\" alt=\"Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:77.50%;top:45.09%;width:12.80%;height:4.96%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Psoas major muscle: Muscles and nerves of posterior abdominal wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.97</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_403_443.jpg\" alt=\"Organization of fat and fascia surrounding the kidney\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:37.27%;top:93.90%;width:23.94%;height:4.73%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Quadratus lumborum muscle: Organization of fat and fascia surrounding the kidney <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.140</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Quadratus lumborum muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Iliolumbar ligament and internal lip of the iliac crest</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Medial half of the 12th rib's lower border and tips of the lumbar transverse processes</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Anterior thoracolumbar fascia layer, then the kidney; subcostal, iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves cross it</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Middle layer of the thoracolumbar fascia, then erector spinae</dd></dl><p>A flat quadrilateral muscle of the posterior abdominal wall from the iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest to the 12th rib and the tips of the lumbar transverse processes, lying posterolateral to psoas major and posterior to the kidney. The subcostal, iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves cross its anterior surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas beside the vertebral bodies; the flat muscle lateral to it at the tips of the transverse processes, behind the kidney, is quadratus lumborum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Iliacus muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa, inner iliac crest and ala of the sacrum</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Lesser trochanter of the femur, with the psoas major tendon</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliac fascia, crossed by the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve; cecum (right), descending colon (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliac fossa of the ilium</dd></dl><p>A fan-shaped muscle arising from the upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa that converges with psoas major to insert on the lesser trochanter; it is supplied by the femoral nerve, which runs in the groove between the two. The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve crosses its surface toward the ASIS. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the wing of the ilium; the muscle lining its inner (abdominal) surface, lateral to psoas, is iliacus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Psoas major (T12–L5 vertebrae) and iliacus (iliac fossa)</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Lesser trochanter of the femur</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Inguinal ligament and iliac fascia, with the femoral nerve on its front</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Hip joint capsule and superior pubic ramus, under the inguinal ligament</dd></dl><p>The combined psoas major and iliacus, which converge below the iliac fossa and pass together under the inguinal ligament, lateral to the femoral vessels, to insert on the lesser trochanter of the femur. Above the pelvic brim the two parts are named separately. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow psoas major and iliacus down to the inguinal ligament; where they have merged into one muscle passing under it into the thigh, it is iliopsoas.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Psoas major muscle</b>: arises from sides of the T12–L5 vertebral bodies, intervening discs and lumbar transverse processes; deep to kidney, ureter, gonadal vessels and genitofemoral nerve, behind the peritoneum; superficial to lumbar transverse processes and vertebral bodies</li><li><b>Psoas minor muscle</b>: arises from sides of the T12 and L1 vertebral bodies and the disc between; deep to psoas fascia and extraperitoneal fat beneath the parietal peritoneum; superficial to anterior surface of psoas major</li><li><b>Quadratus lumborum muscle</b>: arises from iliolumbar ligament and internal lip of the iliac crest; deep to anterior thoracolumbar fascia layer, then the kidney; superficial to middle layer of the thoracolumbar fascia, then erector spinae</li><li><b>Iliacus muscle</b>: arises from upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa, inner iliac crest and ala of the sacrum; deep to iliac fascia, crossed by the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve; superficial to iliac fossa of the ilium</li><li><b>Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus)</b>: arises from psoas major (T12–L5 vertebrae) and iliacus (iliac fossa); deep to inguinal ligament and iliac fascia, with the femoral nerve on its front; superficial to hip joint capsule and superior pubic ramus, under the inguinal ligament</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Psoas major muscle","Psoas minor muscle","Quadratus lumborum muscle","Iliacus muscle","Iliopsoas muscle (psoas major + iliacus)"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Kidneys, suprarenal glands and coverings","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Kidney</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Right: liver, duodenum, right colic flexure; left: stomach, spleen, pancreas, jejunum, left colic flexure</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Diaphragm, psoas major, quadratus lumborum and transversus abdominis aponeurosis</dd></dl><p>Paired bean-shaped retroperitoneal organs on the posterior abdominal wall beside T12-L3, the right slightly lower than the left. Each lies in perirenal fat inside the renal fascia, with the hilum facing anteromedially and the suprarenal gland on its superior pole. <i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas and quadratus lumborum; the bean-shaped organ lying on them in a bed of fat, with vessels entering its medial notch, is the kidney.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Perirenal fat, then the renal fascia</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal cortex (surface of the kidney)</dd></dl><p>The thin, tough, smooth membrane covering the surface of the kidney, easily stripped from a healthy kidney; at the hilum it turns in to line the renal sinus. <i>Finding it:</i> Clean the perirenal fat off the kidney; the thin shiny membrane left on its surface, which can be peeled off, is the fibrous capsule.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_403_443.jpg\" alt=\"Organization of fat and fascia surrounding the kidney\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:47.65%;top:13.24%;width:7.15%;height:4.74%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Kidney: Organization of fat and fascia surrounding the kidney <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.140</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg\" alt=\"External and internal appearance of kidneys\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:35.07%;top:51.72%;width:12.98%;height:4.27%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule): External and internal appearance of kidneys <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Kidney</b>: deep to right: liver, duodenum, right colic flexure; superficial to diaphragm, psoas major, quadratus lumborum and transversus abdominis aponeurosis</li><li><b>Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule)</b>: deep to perirenal fat, then the renal fascia; superficial to renal cortex (surface of the kidney)</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Kidney","Fibrous capsule of kidney (renal capsule)"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Inside the kidney, out to the ureter","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Renal cortex</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Fibrous capsule of the kidney</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal pyramids (medulla), between which it dips as renal columns</dd></dl><p>On a cut kidney it is the outer, usually paler and granular band just beneath the capsule, and it extends inward between the pyramids as the renal columns. It surrounds the usually darker, striated medullary pyramids. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the striated triangular pyramids; the uniform tissue between their bases and the capsule, and the columns between them, is cortex.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Renal pyramid (medulla)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal cortex at its base, beneath the capsule</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal sinus and minor calyx, into which its papilla projects</dd></dl><p>The medulla consists of several cone-shaped renal pyramids, seen on a cut surface as striated triangles with their bases toward the cortex and their apices (renal papillae) projecting into minor calyces. Renal columns of cortical tissue separate adjacent pyramids. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal sinus and calyces in the centre; the striped triangles pointing at them are the pyramids.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_322_1751.jpg\" alt=\"External and internal appearance of kidneys\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:82.62%;top:52.30%;width:10.65%;height:4.27%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Renal cortex: External and internal appearance of kidneys <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.80</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_404_444.jpg\" alt=\"Internal structure of the kidney\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:5.63%;top:67.80%;width:11.65%;height:4.40%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Minor calyx: Internal structure of the kidney <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.141</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Minor calyx</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal papilla of one pyramid (sometimes two or three), draining its collecting ducts</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Major calyx (several minor calyces join each one)</dd></dl><p>A cup-shaped tube in the renal sinus that receives urine from the papilla of a pyramid (sometimes two or three papillae). Several minor calyces join to form each major calyx. <i>Finding it:</i> Find a pyramid's apex and the small cup wrapped around it; follow its stem to where it joins others to form a major calyx.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Major calyx</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Union of several minor calyces in the renal sinus</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, inside the renal sinus</dd></dl><p>Each kidney usually has two or three major calyces (superior, inferior, sometimes middle), each formed by the union of several minor calyces in the renal sinus. The major calyces unite to form the renal pelvis. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow its two or three main branches outward; these are the major calyces, which then divide into minor calyces.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Renal pelvis</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Union of two or three major calyces in the renal sinus</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Ureter, at the ureteropelvic junction near the hilum</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal artery, then renal vein (most anterior), in front of it at the hilum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Posterior segmental artery, then psoas major, behind it at the hilum</dd></dl><p>The funnel-shaped upper end of the ureter, formed in the renal sinus by the union of the major calyces and tapering at the hilum into the ureter. At the hilum it is usually the most posterior structure, behind the renal artery and vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter and follow it up into the hilum; the expansion where it widens and receives the major calyces is the renal pelvis.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ureter</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, at the ureteropelvic junction</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels; mesentery root (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim</dd></dl><p>A thick-walled muscular tube from the renal pelvis that descends retroperitoneally on the anterior surface of psoas major, adherent to the posterior parietal peritoneum and crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels. It enters the pelvis by crossing the bifurcation of the common iliac artery (or the start of the external iliac artery). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow the tube down on psoas to the pelvic brim; check that the gonadal vessels cross in front of it.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Renal cortex</b>: deep to fibrous capsule of the kidney; superficial to renal pyramids (medulla), between which it dips as renal columns</li><li><b>Renal pyramid (medulla)</b>: deep to renal cortex at its base, beneath the capsule; superficial to renal sinus and minor calyx, into which its papilla projects</li><li><b>Minor calyx</b>: empties into major calyx (several minor calyces join each one)</li><li><b>Major calyx</b>: empties into renal pelvis, inside the renal sinus</li><li><b>Renal pelvis</b>: empties into ureter, at the ureteropelvic junction near the hilum; deep to renal artery, then renal vein (most anterior), in front of it at the hilum; superficial to posterior segmental artery, then psoas major, behind it at the hilum</li><li><b>Ureter</b>: empties into posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone; deep to parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels; superficial to psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Renal cortex","Renal pyramid (medulla)","Minor calyx","Major calyx","Renal pelvis","Ureter"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Renal and gonadal vessels","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Renal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Kidney via five segmental arteries; also the inferior suprarenal artery and upper ureter</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal vein; the right also passes behind the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Crus of the diaphragm and psoas major; renal pelvis behind it at the hilum</dd></dl><p>Paired lateral branch of the aorta, usually at the L1/L2 disc just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right renal artery is longer and passes posterior to the IVC, and the left lies posterior to the left renal vein. At the hilum it usually lies between the renal vein (anterior) and renal pelvis (posterior), often dividing into segmental branches before entering, and accessory renal arteries are common. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta just below the SMA origin and follow the lateral branch to the kidney; at the hilum check that the vein is in front of it and the pelvis behind it.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Segmental arteries of kidney</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Renal artery, at or near the hilum, via its anterior and posterior divisions</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Five kidney segments as end-arteries; each divides into interlobar arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Renal vein and its tributaries, in the renal sinus fat</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal pelvis (anterior segmentals); the posterior segmental artery passes behind the pelvis</dd></dl><p>Usually five end-arteries (superior, anterior superior, anterior inferior, inferior and posterior) given off by the renal artery near or inside the hilum, each supplying one vascular segment of the kidney without anastomoses. In the sinus they divide into interlobar arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Follow the renal artery into the hilum; the first set of branches it divides into, before entering the kidney tissue, are segmental arteries.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_325_1756.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply of kidneys and ureters\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.78%;top:54.02%;width:18.16%;height:8.16%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Renal artery: Arterial supply of kidneys and ureters <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.84</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_324_1755.jpg\" alt=\"Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:70.71%;top:58.71%;width:17.52%;height:4.27%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Segmental arteries of kidney: Blood vessels of suprarenal glands, kidneys, and superior parts of ureters <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.83</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Renal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Kidney, via veins joining in the renal sinus</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava), at about L1–L2</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Descending duodenum (right); SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and pancreas (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Renal artery behind it at the hilum; the left vein crosses in front of the aorta</dd></dl><p>The renal veins leave the hilum in front of the renal arteries and end in the IVC at about L1-L2. The left is about three times longer, crosses in front of the aorta just below the SMA and receives the left gonadal and left suprarenal veins; the right is short. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC; the large veins entering its sides from each kidney hilum are the renal veins.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left suprarenal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland; often joined by the left inferior phrenic vein</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Upper border of the left renal vein</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Body of the pancreas and splenic artery, behind the stomach</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Front of the left suprarenal gland, then the left crus of the diaphragm</dd></dl><p>It leaves the anterior surface (hilum) of the left suprarenal gland and descends medially to enter the upper border of the left renal vein, often joined by the left inferior phrenic vein. The right suprarenal vein is very short and drains straight into the IVC. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left renal vein where it crosses in front of the aorta, then trace the vessel entering its upper border up to the suprarenal gland.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Testis via the spermatic cord, or ovary via the suspensory ligament of the ovary</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed by the duodenum and colic vessels (right) or left colic vessels (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Psoas major and the ureter; the right one also crosses in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd></dl><p>A slender paired branch from the front of the aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2) that runs inferolaterally on psoas major deep to the peritoneum with its gonadal vein, crossing anterior to the ureter (the right one also crosses in front of the IVC). The testicular artery enters the deep inguinal ring to run in the spermatic cord; the ovarian artery crosses the pelvic brim into the suspensory ligament of the ovary. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter on psoas; the thin vessels crossing in front of it obliquely are the gonadal vessels. Follow the narrower one up to its origin on the aorta just below the renal arteries.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Renal artery</b>: comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); deep to renal vein; superficial to crus of the diaphragm and psoas major</li><li><b>Segmental arteries of kidney</b>: comes from renal artery, at or near the hilum, via its anterior and posterior divisions; deep to renal vein and its tributaries, in the renal sinus fat; superficial to renal pelvis (anterior segmentals)</li><li><b>Renal vein</b>: drains into IVC (inferior vena cava), at about L1–L2; deep to descending duodenum (right); superficial to renal artery behind it at the hilum</li><li><b>Left suprarenal vein</b>: drains into upper border of the left renal vein; deep to body of the pancreas and splenic artery, behind the stomach; superficial to front of the left suprarenal gland, then the left crus of the diaphragm</li><li><b>Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)</b>: comes from anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2); deep to parietal peritoneum; superficial to psoas major and the ureter</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Renal artery","Segmental arteries of kidney","Renal vein","Left suprarenal vein","Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Abdominal aorta and its other branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior phrenic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta just below the aortic hiatus, or the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Undersurface of the diaphragm; gives the superior suprarenal arteries</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right one; esophagus over the left one</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Crura and undersurface of the diaphragm</dd></dl><p>Paired first branches of the abdominal aorta (or of the celiac trunk) arising just below the aortic hiatus and running up and laterally on the undersurface of the diaphragm, the right behind the IVC and the left behind the esophagus. Each gives the superior suprarenal arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Look at the underside of the diaphragm beside the crura; the artery climbing onto it from the top of the abdominal aorta is the inferior phrenic artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Celiac ganglion</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Greater splanchnic nerves (T5–T9 presynaptic sympathetic); vagal fibers pass through without synapsing</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Foregut organs via periarterial plexuses on the celiac trunk branches</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right one; splenic artery and pancreas over the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Crura of the diaphragm, on either side of the celiac trunk origin</dd></dl><p>The celiac ganglia are a pair of large, irregular prevertebral ganglia lying on the crura of the diaphragm on either side of the origin of the celiac trunk, joined by the dense celiac plexus around the artery; each receives the greater splanchnic nerve, and its lower part is often separate as the aorticorenal ganglion. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk at the aorta and clear the tough fibrous tissue around its origin; the firm masses on each side are the ganglia.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg\" alt=\"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.82%;top:10.32%;width:13.04%;height:3.93%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior phrenic artery: Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_424_469.jpg\" alt=\"Prevertebral ganglia associated with the prevertebral plexus\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:81.82%;top:14.87%;width:14.73%;height:3.80%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Celiac ganglion: Prevertebral ganglia associated with the prevertebral plexus <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.159</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lumbar arteries</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal aorta opposite the L1–L4 vertebral bodies (four pairs)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal wall, back muscles, vertebrae and spinal cord</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Sympathetic trunks and psoas major; right ones also behind the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies</dd></dl><p>Usually four pairs of segmental arteries from the back of the abdominal aorta opposite the L1-L4 bodies. They pass laterally behind the sympathetic trunks and psoas (the right ones also behind the IVC) to the posterior abdominal wall and back. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the aorta off the lumbar spine; the paired arteries leaving its back and hugging the sides of the vertebral bodies are lumbar arteries.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Inferior phrenic artery</b>: comes from abdominal aorta just below the aortic hiatus, or the celiac trunk; deep to IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right one; superficial to crura and undersurface of the diaphragm</li><li><b>Celiac ganglion</b>: comes from greater splanchnic nerves (T5–T9 presynaptic sympathetic); deep to IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right one; superficial to crura of the diaphragm, on either side of the celiac trunk origin</li><li><b>Lumbar arteries</b>: comes from posterior abdominal aorta opposite the L1–L4 vertebral bodies (four pairs); deep to sympathetic trunks and psoas major; superficial to sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Inferior phrenic artery","Celiac ganglion","Lumbar arteries"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Iliac arteries and veins","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Common iliac artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Abdominal aorta bifurcation at L4, slightly left of midline</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>External and internal iliac arteries, in front of the sacroiliac joint (L5–S1)</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum and ureter at its end; small intestine (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>L4–L5 bodies and common iliac veins; the right crosses the IVC (inferior vena cava) origin</dd></dl><p>Each runs inferolaterally from the aortic bifurcation (about L4, slightly left of midline) along the medial border of psoas major and divides anterior to the sacroiliac joint, at about L5/S1, into the external and internal iliac arteries. The ureter crosses its end (or the start of the external iliac), and the common iliac veins lie posterior to the arteries, the IVC beginning behind the right common iliac artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the aortic bifurcation on L4 and follow either branch down to the pelvic brim, where it splits into external and internal iliac arteries.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_383_1878.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries of pelvis\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.69%;top:10.26%;width:19.21%;height:6.00%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Common iliac artery: Arteries of pelvis <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.17</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Common iliac artery</b>: comes from abdominal aorta bifurcation at L4, slightly left of midline; deep to parietal peritoneum and ureter at its end; superficial to L4–L5 bodies and common iliac veins</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Common iliac artery"]},{"l":"VB-A5","h":"Lumbar plexus nerves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Subcostal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior ramus of T12</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterolateral abdominal muscles, suprapubic skin and skin over the hip</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Kidney (posterior surface) and colon, then internal oblique in the anterior wall</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum, then transversus abdominis in the anterior wall</dd></dl><p>The anterior ramus of T12; it runs along the inferior border of the 12th rib, passes posterior to the lateral arcuate ligament and across the anterior surface of quadratus lumborum behind the kidney, then pierces transversus abdominis to run between it and the internal oblique. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the 12th rib on the posterior abdominal wall; the nerve just below its lower border crossing quadratus lumborum is the subcostal nerve.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Iliohypogastric nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior ramus of L1, often with T12</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Internal oblique and transversus abdominis; suprapubic skin and upper lateral buttock skin</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Lower pole of the kidney, then internal oblique, finally the external oblique aponeurosis</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum, then transversus abdominis after piercing it above the iliac crest</dd></dl><p>Branch of L1 (often with a T12 contribution) that crosses anterior to quadratus lumborum, pierces transversus abdominis above the iliac crest and runs between internal oblique and transversus, parallel to and above the ilioinguinal nerve; it ends by piercing the external oblique aponeurosis a few centimetres above the superficial ring, outside the inguinal canal. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ilioinguinal nerve in the inguinal canal, then look just above it between internal oblique and transversus near the iliac crest; the parallel nerve that stays above the canal is the iliohypogastric.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg\" alt=\"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:3.75%;top:20.26%;width:13.71%;height:3.80%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Subcostal nerve: Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_427_473.jpg\" alt=\"\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:66.95%;top:24.81%;width:19.90%;height:3.43%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch):  <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of L1–L2 (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Genital branch: cremaster and anterior scrotum or labium majus; femoral branch: skin over the femoral triangle</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Ureter and gonadal vessels crossing in front, under the parietal peritoneum</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Anterior surface of psoas major</dd></dl><p>From L1-L2, it pierces psoas major and emerges on its anterior surface, descends on the muscle deep to the peritoneum, usually crossed anteriorly by the ureter and gonadal vessels, and divides into a genital branch (through the deep inguinal ring in the spermatic cord, supplying cremaster) and a femoral branch (deep to the inguinal ligament to the skin of the upper anterior thigh). <i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas major; the nerve lying on its front surface, not at its borders, is the genitofemoral nerve. Follow it down to where it divides above the inguinal ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior rami of L2–L3 (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of the anterolateral thigh</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliac fascia, then the cecum on the right or descending colon on the left</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus, crossing obliquely toward the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine)</dd></dl><p>From L2-L3, it emerges from the lateral border of psoas major, crosses iliacus obliquely toward the anterior superior iliac spine, and passes deep to the inguinal ligament just medial to that spine into the thigh. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the anterior superior iliac spine from inside the abdomen; the thin nerve on iliacus heading toward it is the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Femoral nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Posterior divisions of the L2–L4 anterior rami (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Iliacus, anterior thigh muscles (quadriceps, sartorius, pectineus) and anterior thigh skin; saphenous nerve</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Iliac fascia, then the inguinal ligament as it enters the thigh</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Iliacus, in the groove between it and psoas major</dd></dl><p>The largest branch of the lumbar plexus (L2-L4); it emerges from the lateral border of psoas major low in the abdomen, descends in the groove between psoas and iliacus, and passes deep to the inguinal ligament lateral to the femoral artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find psoas and iliacus at the pelvic brim; the thick flat nerve lying in the groove between them is the femoral nerve.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Obturator nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior divisions of the L2–L4 anterior rami (lumbar plexus)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Medial thigh adductor muscles, obturator externus and skin of the medial thigh</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Common iliac vessels at the brim, then pelvic peritoneum and, in females, the ovary</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ala of the sacrum, then obturator internus on the lateral pelvic wall</dd></dl><p>From L2-L4, it emerges from the medial border of psoas major near the pelvic brim, passes behind the common iliac vessels and lateral to the internal iliac vessels, and runs along the lateral pelvic wall to the obturator foramen. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the medial border of psoas at the pelvic brim; the nerve slipping behind the common iliac vessels into the pelvis is the obturator nerve.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lumbosacral trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Part of the L4 anterior ramus joining the L5 anterior ramus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Sacral plexus, joining the S1 anterior ramus; feeds the sciatic and gluteal nerves</dd><dt>Deep to</dt><dd>Common iliac vessels, then the internal iliac vessels at the pelvic brim</dd><dt>Superficial to</dt><dd>Ala of the sacrum, medial to psoas major</dd></dl><p>A thick nerve trunk formed by part of the L4 ventral ramus joining the L5 ventral ramus. It descends over the ala of the sacrum, medial to psoas major and behind the common iliac vessels, to join the sacral plexus. <i>Finding it:</i> Retract psoas major laterally at the pelvic brim; the thick nerve crossing the sacral ala into the pelvis is the lumbosacral trunk.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Subcostal nerve</b>: comes from anterior ramus of T12; deep to kidney (posterior surface) and colon, then internal oblique in the anterior wall; superficial to quadratus lumborum, then transversus abdominis in the anterior wall</li><li><b>Iliohypogastric nerve</b>: comes from anterior ramus of L1, often with T12; deep to lower pole of the kidney, then internal oblique, finally the external oblique aponeurosis; superficial to quadratus lumborum, then transversus abdominis after piercing it above the iliac crest</li><li><b>Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)</b>: comes from anterior rami of L1–L2 (lumbar plexus); deep to ureter and gonadal vessels crossing in front, under the parietal peritoneum; superficial to anterior surface of psoas major</li><li><b>Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)</b>: comes from anterior rami of L2–L3 (lumbar plexus); deep to iliac fascia, then the cecum on the right or descending colon on the left; superficial to iliacus, crossing obliquely toward the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine)</li><li><b>Femoral nerve</b>: comes from posterior divisions of the L2–L4 anterior rami (lumbar plexus); deep to iliac fascia, then the inguinal ligament as it enters the thigh; superficial to iliacus, in the groove between it and psoas major</li><li><b>Obturator nerve</b>: comes from anterior divisions of the L2–L4 anterior rami (lumbar plexus); deep to common iliac vessels at the brim, then pelvic peritoneum and, in females, the ovary; superficial to ala of the sacrum, then obturator internus on the lateral pelvic wall</li><li><b>Lumbosacral trunk</b>: comes from part of the L4 anterior ramus joining the L5 anterior ramus; deep to common iliac vessels, then the internal iliac vessels at the pelvic brim; superficial to ala of the sacrum, medial to psoas major</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"7","ks":["Subcostal nerve","Iliohypogastric nerve","Genitofemoral nerve (genital / femoral branch)","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh)","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Lumbosacral trunk"]}]};
